      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-13_WidnesExaminer_art0013" n="art0013">
        <head>McDOUCALL'S PATENT SELF RAISINC FICURISAR:AM. THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, DECEMBER 13, 1913.</head>
        <p>McDOUCALL'S PATENT SELF RAISINC FICURISAR:AM. THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, DECEMBER 13, 1913.</p>
        <p>Sir,—ln reading the speech of Mr. Joynson- Hicks , I was surprised to find a man of his position guilty of giving utterance to the follow- ing: "Above them is the Lord God of battles. In His Name and under His protection I say to you, the Prime Minister, with your arms, with your batteries—fire, fire if you dare fire, and be damned." No'wonder the "Manchester Guardian" referred to it as a "remarkable outburst." People of all shades of opinion have deplored the dragging of the King's name into party politics, and rightly so; hut when the name of the Almighty is introduced into poli- tical controversy it is nothing short of out- rageous. We English love a fighter, but he has to gain our respect, and such extravagant talk as I have quoted will only merit the contempt of the intelligent portion of the comMun,ity. ARTHUR T: HUGHES. 14, Bedford-street, Stockton Heath.</p>
        <p>are supported by cadging—the blackest spot in our social system. How many are there who are unfit to be out of hospital and-cannot get- in, -because there is not room?</p>
        <p>I sincerely hope that the leading men in the town will go into the question thoroughly, to try and bring about the reform whereby we working men will benefit by the knowledge that the hospitals are ours, supported by everyone of us. I may lay it down as .an unehallenge- able fact "that it is the prime duty of every man to protect himself against sickness," and by municipalisation of the hospitals he would do so.</p>
        <p>PETER PEACOCK, juN. 44, Hood-lane, Sankey. December 10, 1913.</p>
        <p>tary entry of the. Church in Wales into the jurisdiction of the Church in England, by in- clusion in the Province of Canterbury. Con- sequently the Church both in Wales and Eng- land is one Church, and named the Church of England, though it would be more strictly cor- rect, perhaps, if it were called "the Church of Wales and England," seeing that the Welsh portion of it is much the older. Assuming this to be trile 'history, how -does' Mr. Barker explain the inference which his first remark seems to convey, that the Church in Wales is not the " National " (Welsh) Church, but the English Church? And what does he mean when he follows on to say that it "spoke well for the nationalism of the Welsh that through the centuries they had kept their own peculiar methods of worship? And to further remark that many of the people in Wales "believed the Church of England reli- gion to be sinful " ? Your speaking contributor tells us that he is " theoretically " in favour of disestablishment, but he does not seem to have told his audience what he really meant thereby, nor, indeed, what he understands to be the thing which is called "Establishment." Respecting Disendowment, he is clear and frank—he does not oppose taking away 12s. in the X of the property, of the Church in Wales by Act of Parliament (seeing it cannot be taken away by the ordinary process at law) "because ,it would not affect the present parsons"! May ask him if he would have no objection to:con- siderably more than half his estate being un- deservedly ,taken away from him, if he were assured by his despoilers that the process should not hurt him during his mortal life? C. STIDSTON-BROADBENT, Diocesan Church Defence Organiser.</p>
        <p>• • INCREASING RENTS. Sir,—Your correspondent "Trade Unionist? says that the speculative builders of Warring- ton have not paid the standard rate of wages to their workers •during the . last 15 years. I am a builder in Warrington and feel that this statement effects me just as much as it does Mr. .Smith. 1. feel it my duty to refute this absurd charge. I pay the standard rate of wages and always have done so. The same can be said of Mr. Daniel Cooper, my brother. Be- tween us-we have built as much house property as any other builder in Warrington. The con- ditions of labour on all our work are equal to, or better than, the conditions enforced by the union. When we employ men the question of union or non-union never enters into the ar- rangement, and the only., distinction between the two is that the bad worker gets his wages before the week-end. The bone of contention between the union and the speculator is the importation of foreign doors. The Canadian can be bought for 4s.- 9d., whilst the English costs Bs. 6d., or more. Why should the builder pay 88. when he can get equal value for -ss. If Free Trade is good for,the union joiner it should be good for the master, otherwise it is not equitable. Does "Unionist" suggest that none but union men can do good work? The timber in an inch and a quarter door (Pine) costs about 4s. 6d. Where does the labour come in? How is it done? An article on "Free Trade and Tariff Reform" might explain the whole matter. Anyhow, the word " Bunkum " throws no light upon the subject. In .answer to the statement made by "Unionist" that the speculative builder lives in a state of luxury is an absolute fabrication. Out of all the builders in .Warrington there is not one this Statement applies to, but at least half of th have met their creditors. This is a positive sign of poverty and not riches. Padgate-lane, Warrington.</p>
        <p>WM. COOPER</p>
        <p>. . . Sir,—The simplicity a your corresfiondent, "Fair Rent," is incredible. 116 asks, "Why should the .Warrington rents be raised to the Manchester standard, when it is admitted that the property here is valued at 30 per cent. less?" If he will carefully read my article he will see why. The value of property in Warrington is 30 per cent. below Manchester, not because it cost 30 per cent, less to Construct, but because the rents are ; considerably lower. A iihop in Bridge-street may not be as !arge or as well con strueted as 'fir 'cottage in Gorsey-lane, vet the rent may be four times that of the cottage. To arrive at the value of a property, we must deduct al.l the outgoings and see what is left. The second •argument used by "Fair Renter" is based on the portion of the cost of paving which should be borne by the tenant. He assumes• certain ratios to suit mnis method of analysis, and ultimately persuades himself- that sixpence- a week on a three-roomed cottage, pre- viously rented at five shillings per week, should compensate the landlord for interest on cost of paving and additional rates, which he admits should be paid for by the tenant. Before attempting to discuss this question, I advise the reader to keep in mind that the tenant 'pays the rates through the landlord; secondly, that the additional rent is subject to additional rating; lastly, that 5 per cent. is not an excessive rate of interest to charge for im- provements. If the paving cost £lB, then on this basis 18s. is due to the landlord. A cot- tage rented at five shillings a week is assessed at ..t.7 10s. "Fair Rent" admits that the rates have gone up is. 6d. in the .t, and seven_ and a half times one and sixpence equals ten and six- pence. and thus brings the total sum due for improvements and increased rates to 1 Bs. 6d. Take the previous rent paid by the tenant, £l3 per annum; deduct the. rates paid on behalf of the tenant by the owner to the Corporation, I'2 9s. This leaves £lO lls, is the net rent paid by the tenant. To this add Bs.. 6d. for im- provenients, and we have a total of £ll 19s. 6d. due to the landlord. The reader should ask the Corporation what the rates are. I have done this on his behalf, but there is'no reason why he should not do; this for himself,-if he doubts the truth of my figures. He will find that the rates are 024 125., which makes, the amount due to the landlord and the Corporation £l6 us. 6d., or, say, 6s. 4d. per week. The property owners sent a deputation to the rating authorities to discuss this outrageous anomaly in the rating of houses. It should be known to all tenants and landlords that in order to draw an additional rent of £1 Bs. 6d. per annum, the owner has, through the rating authorities, to charge his tenants £3'lls. 6d., and hand over... the difference to the Borough Treasurer. A "Fair Rent" has the audacity to offer his landlord sixpence a week, indulges in meta- phorical language, termil me an "innocent," de- clares " Nemesis " to be on my tkack. We are not nervous. " Nemesis " has been detected by the Association, and we have laid him by the heels. He is a "Fair Renter," who offers silt- pence a week instead of is. 4d. • There is not one reasonable argument in the whole of "Fair Rent's" letter. Epithets and names such as "Bunkum," "Rip Van Winkle," especially if made anonymously, are unprofit- able. Let "Fair Rent" come out of his shell, so that his lack of knowledge on this subject may be debited to the right account. I thank "Trade Unionist" for supporting me</p>
        <p>in such a• valiant manner: Not that my ',argu- mentew.equired support; none of tnein nas even been assailed. if all , that " Unionist" says is true, I am Zetter oil than I thought, and the building trade is in a more deplorable state than ever. The cheapest labour, the cheapest materials, union labour and non-union labour, have tailed to extricate it from its deplorable condition, and to use " Unionist's ", expression, -it is a good job .it -has „gone .f o..the , I wonder whether the house he lives in was trade union built, and if it was, what addi- tional rent he pays on that account. The whole tenor of his argument is that the builder has gone to the wall because he did not employ union labour. He distinctly infers that the public will not buy house property because it is draughty and badly constructed, a condi- tion that would have been averted if union joiners had been employed. What vanity! Surely a new claifn on behalf of union labour. "Trade Unionist" would not, or could not, explain why the ,speculative builder, though paying the standard rate of\wages, was refused the support of the union. It is summed up in two words, " Canadian doors." The joiners' executive served notice on the builders to the 'elect that in future no union joiner would be allowed to fix Canadian doors. The co si of a Canadian door at that time was four shillings, whilst that of an English door was' eight shillings, and without the slightest doubt the Canadian door was the better value. I am not antagonistic to trade unionism, but I fail" to 'see how a man is made a better 'and 'a more skilful worker by joining the union: I can understand the technical schools 'improv- ing him, but I have yet to learn that Irade unionism stands for increased efficiency. It enables the indifferent worker to demand the same wages_ as his better co-worker, especially where day: work is the custom.</p>
        <p>Much controversy has taken place on the ques- tion of the Canadian door. The union, either through sheer ignorance or stubbornness, re- fused to allow its members to fix or handle these doors. Most of the timber used in This country is imported. The cost of transit is considerable, and to facilitate handling and save space in shipping-',it iS cut up in -lengths and specified sizes. This cannot be done with- out leaving an enormous amount of small timber, which would never reach a market unless put up in a compact form. Not only does this practice 'save valuable timber from destruction, but it lessens the cost of boards and scantlings, an item of no small importance to the builder. It would be as reasonable to object to the importation- of flooring boards as doors, and it requires no genius to see that saw- dust- and shavings are not articles to import into this country., The speculative builder could not see the wis- dom of paying seven shillings for what could be had for four shillings. He refused to allow the union to dictate as to how he should spend his money, and demanded the same freedom in buying as that enjoyed by his union workmen. _ .</p>
        <p>For the purposes of this argument I will desig- nate the man who has just received the price of his week's hire a capitalist. He belongs to the union, and receives something over two pounds sterling. He starts out to purchase his week's necessaries. Does he insist on the trousers he buys being union made? Does he ask if his brother worker 'received the standard rate of pay? Has the worker who Made his shirt, the watch in his pocket. the furniture in his house, the standard rate of pay? Does he care? Who patronises the cheap clothiers and the .bazaars, where all the goods sold are German? The man who refused to fix the foreign door did not hesitate to measure his lengths with a German rule, nor to saw and-plane his master's. timber with American tools. Had the employer who was compelled to pay him a standard wage insisted on his buying nothing but English goods, what would his reply have been? Surely he would have said, "I have Worked for my wages and shall spend them how and when I like," and who would have blamed..him? Not a Free Trader. He would'haVe resented such in- terference. That was the attitude taken up by the builder. . _ _</p>
        <p>He refused to agree to the -terms insisteif upon by the union because the union did not live up to its Awn precepts. Can any reader believe that "Trade Unionist" is sincere when he-infers that if a union house was MAR for .him he Would pay any more rent on that account? My experience has taught me not to place any such reliance on my tenants. I hope to keep them by,tompetitton. •I do not trust a man because he has a loud voice and reiterates the one word "Union," but is a trade unionist only from Monday to Saturday, and between times a most uncompromising capital- ist. The trade unionist does not hesitate to wear a coat that was made under condition st a thousand times worse than "the cottage weavers ever dreamed of," yet he fastens the badie of his society on the most conspicuous part of the slop-made garment and sallies forth to teach the future aspirant of union oratory the fine art of "how not to do what you preach."</p>
        <p>What is the remedy for this? Let every trade unionist buy nothing jaut union goods, stAmp all the goods with a union stamp, engage in- spectorS to check, as far as possible,- the goods bought and sold. In five years there will be few men out of the union and fewer masters still who would dare to be antagonistic to its prin- ciples.</p>
        <p>answer to the question of increased wages for the industrial worker, and why I am in favour of the increase, the reply is simple. If " Trade Unionist" had invested in railway stock he would hope for a rise, or, at least, that it would remain at the price he paid. My capital is invested in labour (house property); if labour goes up the value of my stock will in- crease. Take the position- of the man who by Purchasing his own house has invested his capital in labour. If the cost of building is in- creased, the value of his house will go up accord- ingly. and if labour in all grades has advanced, he will have maintained his position and his earnings will be 'in ratio with the increased cost of living. The position of the man who has in- vested in Consols is bad. His money was ad- vanced at a fixed rate, and the invester Las no means of increasing the earning capacity of stock, with the inevitable result that the one- time " gilt-edged " security has declined in value, indeed, has fallen from ninety pounds to seventy-one pounds within the last few years. The obvious moral is to invest in labour by pur- chasing your own house. Socialists oughtmto feel happy at the result, for here We see 'the National Debt automatically extinguishing it- self pt the expense of the rich. Here we see the source from which increased taxes are drawn. In conclusion I must point out that it is very much against my liiclination to answer anony- mous writers. If a man is proud of his cause he need not be afraid of signing his letters, and it reflects no Credit on trade unionism when the armour of the crab is adopted to save the skin of its supporter. He accuses the speculative builder of never having paid standard wages, and distinctly infers that union men were never engaged. If he will prove that .his assertion is true, I will pay twenty pounds to the Infirmary; and if he does not lift this sum withifi a month, in spite of his shell, we shall suspect he is only "Larkin."</p>
        <p>THE ADULT SCHOOLS.</p>
        <p>The Story of the First Revolt of Agricultural Labourers.</p>
        <p>Act.—Mr. A. Bottomley said they brought the case as a warning.—The magistrates dismissed the woman, but said the next offender would be dealt with severely.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-13_WidnesExaminer_art0014" n="art0014">
        <head>VOICE FROM THE STATES.</head>
        <p>A VOICE FROM THE STATES.</p>
        <p>Su—l shall be extremely obliged if you will allow me through the medium of your valuable paper, which I read weekly with inter- est, to thank the large number of friends for their good wishes extended to me.</p>
        <p>I have read your article on Labour in your issue of November Ist, and I think that great credit is due to Mr. Clynes and good old Charles Dukes in trying to elevate the general labourer to a better position in the fight for a living wage. Now with two • such leaders it requires men to use the influence they possess to make the G.W. and G.L.U. army larger by inducing their fellow men to follow their example and uphold the "buttons up " principle. I should say that praise is due to a friend of mine in the good old town for the energy he dis- played during a labour struggle a short while ago. Has untiring efforts to bring forth a better issue was deserving of a better fate. "Nil des- perandum."</p>
        <p>In regard to the brewery workers, only one word have I to say, " I have read with interest " A.S.'s" notes on Angling, and, as an old friend, may- I congratu- late him on the excellent way he contributes to "The Examiner."</p>
        <p>In conclusion I 'wish your paper every success. P. TULLY.</p>
        <p>Peoria, Illinois, U.S.A. must tell you that I pass your paper along to several men from Lancashire.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-13_WidnesExaminer_art0015" n="art0015">
        <head>IRISH QUESTION.</head>
        <p>THE IRISH QUESTION.</p>
        <p>you kindly allow me to assure "Phil Squod " I still adhere to every word of my expressions of opinion respecting his letters, as in my rejoinder to his first letter on the Home Rule question? And, again, it was the furthest thing from my thoughts to charge him person- ally with Pharisaism. It was just that senti- ment he uttered, comparing the wealth and in- telligence of Ulster, as against Home Rule, to the poverty and ignorance of the rest of Ire- 'land, who were in favour, that drew the remark from me in condemnation of that line of argu- ment which I have heard for so many years, ad nauseum, that my very soul revolts against it, because I believe it to be so inaccurate. As a matter of fact, four probably of the wealthiest men in Ulster are in favour of Home Rule and hundreds'of its business men. Ignorance is cpm- parative, and politically it cannot be laid to the charge of the Irish fieople. Their consistency for so many years belies that statement. Of course, as our friend "Phil Squod" rightly says, the 'result of this correspondence must rest on the judgment of your readers, who may have followed it. Therefore I want to reiterate, if possible, my opinion. The true aim of Home Rulers to-day is an Irish Parliament to deal with Irish affair', only, subordinate to the Im- peHal Parliament, and securing civil and re- ligious liberty to all within its borders.</p>
        <p>PATRICK O'BRIEN 125, Wilderspool-road, Warrington.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-13_WidnesExaminer_art0016" n="art0016">
        <head>MUNICIPALISATION OF HOSPITALS.</head>
        <p>MUNICIPALISATION OF HOSPITALS.</p>
        <p>Sir,—This is a question of great importance, which, to my mind, is dpserving of every con- sideration from the ratepayers, and a question which will eventually lead to the greatest social reform this country has ever known. The question not only deals with "munici- palisation," but th 4 reform of our wretched nursing system. Hospitals and nurses are an absolute neces- sity to the publiB, equally as necessary as our schoolmasters, police, army, navy, but how are .these latter supported? Are we forced tip make special collections in the churches and from the various workpeople? No, they are "State- owned."</p>
        <p>Vhy not municipalise the hospitals, and do away with the scandalous cadging methods by which our hospitals are forced to obtain funds? Let us municipalise the. hospitals, better the conditions of the nurses, pay them something approaching a living wage, do away with the ridiculous "living-in" system, get enough nurses to abolish it, and you will have plenty of applications if the conditions were altered and a"decent wage offered. What would the nurse be told should she ask for an increase in salary at many institutions? The answer would be, "Sorry, funds are low; have not sufficient to pay expenses." People do not realise that the nurse Performs noble work, the noblest in the world, tending the sick, and many times making an invalid of herself. Yet, she is the worst paid professional in the world. Why? Because our institutions</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-13_WidnesExaminer_art0017" n="art0017">
        <head>figIAGE MADE A--c" – – 4</head>
        <p>figIAGE MADE A--c" - - 4</p>
        <p>BY READING</p>
        <p>':'-tfif,,•..M.ATR.R.40404...,c.1F,k0kp</p>
        <p>az: introductory journal full of GENUlNE,.adver- tisements appealing to all classes of ladies and gentlemen desirous of marriage. 64. Post Free in Sealed Envelor; Editor, 18, Hogarth Road, Earl's Court.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-13_WidnesExaminer_art0018" n="art0018">
        <head>TRADE UNIONISTS AND TORYISM.</head>
        <p>TRADE UNIONISTS AND TORYISM.</p>
        <p>Sir,—lt is really interesting, and at the same time amusing, to read the speech of the gentle- man who toasted every section of the Conserva- tive Association at the annual dinner last Fri- day. The gentleman who proposed "Our Member" had doubtless read the fictitious menu that some characteristic Tory scribe had written for the occasion, when he said "he won- dered whether people were getting more ignor- ant, more susceptible to thestimulation of their passions and the loss of their senses." It is not a stretch of imagination to think that such thoughts were prompted by the tabulated abuse of the silliest kind against politicar opponents that was put in front of the leaders of the party in the shape of a menu.' But the most signifi- .cant remark fell from the gentleman who had to propose an omnibifs• toast' whh embraced every- Tory organisation in the borough. It was obviously thought advisable not to pledge thelm separately, or the consequences might he serious, and the speech even worse. •This gentleman remarked that bad organisation might lose an election. He had possibly in his mind the once famous Unionist Labour party of blessed memory, and its famous offspring, the Trades .Unionist Tariff Reform' League. It, is not,. to say the least, consistent with the optim- ism, and the professions of honesty of purpose of the speeches, to have a League without mem- bers, and a 'gentleman to answer on behalf of the League who is not a trade unionist. , TRADE UNIONIST.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-13_WidnesExaminer_art0019" n="art0019">
        <head>NATIONAL SERVICE AND CONSCIENCE.</head>
        <p>NATIONAL SERVICE AND CONSCIENCE.</p>
        <p>Sir,—Mr. 11. Gray writes in your issue of December 6th kindly asking me questions about conscientious objectors to military training in the Antipodes. . I gladly reply and give below the exact clauses on the subject from the Australian Defence Act.</p>
        <p>Section 127, Clause (c) : All persons liable to be trained under sections (3) (c) and (d) of Sec- tion 125 of this Act, who are forbidden by the doctrines of their religion to bear arms, shall, as far as possible, be allotted to non-combatant duties.</p>
        <p>Under Section 61, Clause (1), it is also stated that persons who satisfy the prescribed authorities that their-conscientious beliefs do not allow them to bear arms are exempt from service in time of war.</p>
        <p>Mr. Gray must beware of the partisan state- ments with which the fighting seotion of the Peace party are flooding this country. He speaks gaily of hundreds of lads being gaoled. If he will come down to facts he will find that in New Zealand only 78 Territorials have been committed to detention under the Defence Act, and that in Australia those who shirk drill are one-sixth of the number of those who shirk com- pulsory education. As for his delightful idea that we in -Warrington, who were- largely re- sponsible for Lord Roberts's Manchester meet- ing, actually cabled the chief speech out free of charge to Colonial papers, Mr. Gray is too credulous. His suggestion is absolutely untrue. If Mr. Gray can bring any evidence to prove it, I will.give him a turkey for Christmas, just to show goodwill towards one of the fairest of our Opponents.</p>
        <p>B. S. TOWNROE</p>
        <p>Mere House. Newton-le-Willows. December 10th, 1913</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-13_WidnesExaminer_art0020" n="art0020">
        <head>DISESTABLISHMENT AND DISENDOW-</head>
        <p>DISESTABLISHMENT AND DISENDOW-</p>
        <p>MENT.</p>
        <p>Sir,—lt was more than I Could do to under- stand the speech of Mr. IL Barker, reported in your last issue. He calls himself a I.,hurchman "High Churchman," to be correct—as a pre- liminary •to 'siding up (evidently for party purposes) with Nonconformist gentlemen who are desirous of depriving the Church of both position' and possessions! Would his political associates dream of being so considerate if the deeds of establishment, and .the endowments, of Nonconformist Churches were assailed?</p>
        <p>Mr. Barker's first remark, as reported, was</p>
        <p>that "the Welsh Established Church (sic) was not in any way the National Church, but merely a btanch of the Church of England in Wales." Apparently there is such confusion here that the only way to clear what may easily be taken to convey a most Untrue statement, is to state some actual facts. The Church in Wales is the • same Church which existed in ancient Britain during the time of the . Roman occupation. With the Ancient Britons, or Celts, it was driven into Wales by the next corners to the Romans—the Angles and Saxons. There, in Wales, the Celts and the Celtic Church have been ever since. Some people like to give their 'reputation away by calling that Church "alien." The Angles and Saxons, who were idolaters when they came to Britain, in their turn, be- cattle converted to Christianity by Augustine, who settled in the .country in, the year 597, as Mr. Barker correctly says.- In due course the Church of the Angles was embraced by all the kingdoms of the Heptarchy. And the "Church of the Angles" is the present "Church of Eng- land."</p>
        <p>The only "establishment" either by the Celtic people in Wales of their Church, or by .the Angle-Saxon people in England (or Angleland, as it then was) of their Church, v.-as the accept- ance by both peoples of the one and only reli- gion or "Church" then known. It was long after the Church. was definitely formed that any complete constitution of the State existed. Nothing has • happened since completely to alter the respective relation or position of the two branches of the Church, beyond the volun-</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-13_WidnesExaminer_art0021" n="art0021">
        <head>G. SMITH</head>
        <p>G. SMITH</p>
        <p>The Limes, Orford. December 11th, 1913</p>
        <p>ECONOMY IN DRESS.</p>
        <p>No man, unless he is enormously wealthy or terribly poor, can afford to neglect his appear- ance and dress. The first point to consider above everything else; is quality. In the matter of hats, boots and raincoats, a man who seeks real value for money could not do better than go to Jacksons'. In the case of a hat, it will retain its original stylish shape and smartness long after one of an inferior make is worn out; the slime thing, in a marked degree, is the case with Jacksons' raincoats and boots. At Jack- sons' branch shop, 22, Horsemarket-street, War- rington, a department is opened for ladies, who may obtain raincoats and boots of the latest fashion at the same prices as charged for men. It is not surprising that Jacksons' ladies' de- partment is being more appreciated every clay.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-13_WidnesExaminer_art0022" n="art0022">
        <head>Cathedral Cities.</head>
        <p>Cathedral Cities.</p>
        <p>The Dean of Peterborough has said. that dur- ing the five years he has been in Peterborough he has never seen a drunken person in ;the streets. Peterborough, it appears, is free from the reproach that attaches to niost Cathedral Aties.... It can regard Winchester with a lofty scorn. Many years ago Lord Tennyson drove through the streets of Winchester on the ,box- seat of a coach; and the pos.t said to the coach- man, " What , sort of place is this?" "De- bauched, sir," was the 'sad reply, "Debauched, like all other Cathedral cities.," ,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-13_WidnesExaminer_art0023" n="art0023">
        <head>SEQUEL TO THE "BLACK DEATH."</head>
        <p>SEQUEL TO THE "BLACK DEATH."</p>
        <p>The most interesting paper delivered in con- nection with the Adult Schools this week was that given to the Sankey Bridges Women's School on Tuesday evening. It was another of the series on Old English history-, the subject being "The Black Death and the Peasants' Revolt." The lecturer said that it was a re- markable thing that so much attention was given by historians to the various Continental wars of England, whilst an event like the Black Death, which involved not' only the deaths of one-third of the population, but led to a revo- lution in agricultural and all industrial life in England, was passed over in a few casual para- graphs. The Black Death was first heard of in the East, and travelled westward over Asia Minor, Syria and Egypt, till it reached our shores, via Dorset, in August, 1348. By 1349 it had over-run the whole of the kingdom. Although' the figures' for the towns are very high, in London 200 per day was the mortality, and the coßntry districts suffered. most. Death overcame the victims in one to three days. Men and women were attacked, rarely the children.</p>
        <p>A Chaotic Condition. The country was in a chaotic condition owing to the lack of labourers to till the soil, crops rotting in the ground. The few labourers left began to wander the country, and refused to stay at work. To .understand the consequences of the plague it must be borne in mind that for some time the manorial system, under which the majority of men were bound to the soil, was breaking down. They were beginning .to de- mand their .freedom and payment for their labour. Towns were rapidly rising up and down the country,. trade was becoming established; and men were specialising in crafts. The lamt- owners were in a fix, and they appealed to the king. Parliament. not being in session, he issued a proclamation -To the effect that all men must return to work at the old rates. This was after- wards embodied in the famous Statute of Labourers.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-13_WidnesExaminer_art0024" n="art0024">
        <head>Severe Penalty.</head>
        <p>A Severe Penalty.</p>
        <p>The penalty of refusal was branding, and the landowner who offered to pay more than the 2d. a day enacted was also liable to-heavy punish- ment. The law was evaded by both parties. The country was in a state of unrest, and men were gradually breaking into rebellion. The spirit of revolt .was. fanned, into flame by such men as John Ball, the "mad priest of Kent," and Wat Tyler, and when a secies of poll taxes were instituted in 1379, it proved the la'st straw, and an active rebellion burst out. The riots were eventually crushed, but the laws relating to the holding of land were slowly changed, and there arose what may be described as a wage- earning class for the first time in this country. Next week the subject is "The Rise of the Towng."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-13_WidnesExaminer_art0025" n="art0025">
        <head>BEWSEY MEN'S.</head>
        <p>BEWSEY MEN'S.</p>
        <p>The Bible lesson on Nunaay was on "Paul's boldness and confidence." It was pointed out that it was Paul's love for the Corinthians Which led him to be so severe in denouncing the vices .which'had-taken -hold of the Corinthian Church. Although Paul was a much Changed man since his acceptance of Christianity, yet his fighting qualities still remained. But now his aggres- sion was turned to other things. No longer did he use the old weapons. His defence of his allostleship formed one of the most stirring episodes in .his remarkable, career. Paul was still anxious to evolve the spirit of true fellow- ship among them, in spite of all the opposition he encountered. Although men might differ in matters such as church government and organi- sation, and on polity, yet love and service to- wards each other should characterise their rela- tions. Unity of spirit and purpose should_ever underly Christian activity. And Paul wat3 con- fident that in course of time these greater things would prevail. It was claimed that Adult School men should be broad-minded, charitable, and he ever ready to co-operate with other Christian organisations; working with them for the eommon good.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-13_WidnesExaminer_art0026" n="art0026">
        <head>LONGFORD.</head>
        <p>LONGFORD.</p>
        <p>The President on Sunday morning introduced the Bible lesson and gave some useful informa- tion about the difficulties Paul had to face in the Corinthian Church, and he also gave some account of the various parties or sects who opposed Paul's teaching and apostleship.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-13_WidnesExaminer_art0027" n="art0027">
        <head>STOCKTON HEATH MEN'S.</head>
        <p>STOCKTON HEATH MEN'S.</p>
        <p>Mr. E. A. Sanders gave an interesting lec- tiirette on "Why working men do not go to church?" A. fairly exhaustive analysis of the many reasons for the apathy of the workers to- wards the organised churches was given by the lecturer, and an animated discussion was after- wards held, some strong opinions being ex- pressed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-13_WidnesExaminer_art0028" n="art0028">
        <head>WINTER'S WORK ON FARM.</head>
        <p>WINTER'S WORK ON FARM.</p>
        <p>Most farmers find it difficult to keep their men occupied during the winter months,, and I know they would welcome any suggestions as to useful and profitable. work. Of course, there is always a fair .amount ,of work to do. Ditches and fences must be attended to, stock take up a lot of time also, and ploughing must be done, but op some days, especially when it is wet or snowy, it is hard to find work for all. This note may help to solve the problem.. If a farmer or his man has any taste for car- pentry, he will find plenty to do. Various ar- ticles can be made which require very little skill, and. not very large outlay, except for the wood. Feeding troughs and (O\V cribs are always useful, while farmers who keep poultry can construct chicken coops and runs also with wire netting attached on top. Although home- made appliances may not look very handsome, they have one great meril, they .can be made very strong, and they are much more useful than same of the .flimsy ready-made articles which one sometimes comes across.</p>
        <p>The tarring, of woodwork provides- another opportunity of employing spare time. It is not much, but it certainly preserves the wood, be- sidts making wooden buildings watertight, and, moreover, it generally gives the farmyard a neat appearance. Fowl houses and chicken houses are similar. Buildings should certainly be tarred occasionally to keep out the wet. The tar can be bought cheaply in barrels, and it must be applied hot. The care of implements igain will furnish plcinty of work for the wintry days. Too often one sees farm implements neglected, especia4 those used for haymaking and .harvesting-; binders, mowing machines,</p>
        <p>- swath turners, and so forth, very often when done with for the season are pushed away into a corner of the barn or shed, and left there till the next year, without any attention. The iron rusts, the, woodwork rots, the nuts get loose, and, in consequence, the machine. does not last half as long as it would do if a little care was taken of it. A little thought would enable many farmers to take reasonable care of their machines, and to occupy their spare time both pleasantly and profitably.</p>
        <p>Is Seeing Relieving?</p>
        <p>The Christian Sdentists and most Buddhists deny. 'any physical-existence whatever, and their position appears to them perfectly logical from a metaphysical standpoint. "The argu- ments of the Buddhists regarding the unreality of the Objects of perception," says a writer in the journal of the American Oriental Society. "may thus be summarised. Our perceptions in dreams do not, in principle, differ from, those in the waking state, • and consequently the lattermust be just as void and as independent of something existing beside them (their object, as the dream of impressions: further examples of impressions void of really existing objects are fata morgana and mirage. As dreams- or magic fata morgana•are regarded as unreal by ordinary men, so this whole world is regardtd by those versed in the Vedantas."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-13_WidnesExaminer_art0029" n="art0029">
        <head>NEARLY SUFFOCATED. Bad Case of Whooping Cough Cured bY VENO'S LIGHTNING COUGH CURE.</head>
        <p>NEARLY SUFFOCATED. Bad Case of Whooping Cough Cured bY VENO'S LIGHTNING COUGH CURE.</p>
        <p>"I am certain that Veno's Lightning Cough Cure saved my child's life," says Mrs. Helen Tedds, of 28, New Town-road, Bedworth, Nun; eaton. "I thought at first my little girl hau caught cold, but soon she was whooping badly: She got worse, and seemed on the point or, suffocating, and I had to get up in the night an°, give her things to relieve the awful strain or the cough, as she went black in the face. DI the end I got Veno's Lightning Cough Cure, and what a change My child got easier B.t once, and a short course of this wonderful eine completely cured her. I wish I could teh every mother what a good thing Veno's is." It is hard to see a child suffer, is it not? Ther don't let.it suffer. Get Veno's Lightning Cougg Cure, as Mrs. Tedds did, and your child wi" be cured as hers was. Veno's Lightning Cough Cure is a perfect remedy for all diseases of chet: lungs and thrbat, in children or adults. Pricy 94d., 1/Ik, and 2/9, of all chemists. °</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-13_WidnesExaminer_art0030" n="art0030">
        <head>FOOT JOY.</head>
        <p>FOOT JOY.</p>
        <p>It is now, when the children come to Ptit father's slippers to warm, that it is found they: are looking the worse for wear. The question of renewal is therefore considered, and it is decided that a nice new pair from the Public Benefit, Boot Co.'s establishment in Bridge-street wall(' be just the thing for a Christmas present. But that is only one of the many attractions in foot- wear displayed at this popular emporium. There are evening shoes, gaiters, &amp;c., in numerous styles, and indeed every class of boot and shoe required for the "wee bairn" up to the " grand old man" is on view. A feature which will please the ladies is that this noted firm suPPIY satin topped shoes and hose to match the various dresses.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-13_WidnesExaminer_art0031" n="art0031">
        <p>'</p>
        <p>IN THE COURTS.</p>
        <p>BOROUGH POLICE COURT</p>
        <p>SATURDAY.</p>
        <p>Before Mr. R. T. Fairclough (in the chair), Alderman Tinnion, Mr. T. Pemberton, and Mr. A. J. Jolley.</p>
        <p>A CYCLE THEFT.—Fred Robert Kirk, 23, Ward- street, New Tupton, Derbyshire, was charged with stealing a bicycle, the property of Mr. Hughes, draper, 10, Crosfield-street.—The Chief Constable said Mr. Hughes left his bicycle in the garden of his house, and it was stolen. The police ascertained that prisoner had sold the machine for 15s. in Derbyshire. When charged, prisoner replied: "I plead guilty. I am sorry I did it."—Prisoner said he stole the bicycle to help his wife, who was ill." A letter appealing for leniency was read from the wife.—Prisoner was sent to prison for one month, with hard labour.</p>
        <p>A LENIENT EMPLOYER.'-.Thomas Gahgan, 13, Gee-street, was charged with stealing 301 b. of scrap lead, value 35., the property of Leonard Aukland.—lt appeared that Gahgan was em- ployed by the prosecutor and had sold some of the metal. Mr. Aukland was not anxious to press the matter and was willing to take Gahgan back into his service if the Bench dealt leniently with him.—A fine of 10s., including costs, was imposed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-13_WidnesExaminer_art0032" n="art0032">
        <head>FRIDAY.</head>
        <p>FRIDAY.</p>
        <p>Before Mr. S. Wright (in the chair), Alderman Smethurst, Councillor Wilkinson, Messrs. A. J. Jolley, G. Pilling, T. Locker, and W. Furness.</p>
