      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-13_WidnesExaminer_art0038" n="art0038">
        <head>ITALY IN TRIPOLI.</head>
        <p>ITALY IN TRIPOLI.</p>
        <p>Mr. W. K. McClure's "Italy in North Africa" is made up in part of his writings"as special correspondent of the "Times" during the occu- pation. The author is a defender of Italian policy and military action. He is able to show by his excursion into diploniacy that Italy's " claims " to the reversion of the Tripolitaine had the informal permission of the powers. What he does not clear up, and what may never be cleared up unless the secrets of the Chancel- leries are revealed, is why Italy took precipi- tate action in the autumn of 1911. He repeats the story, without expressing belief, or disbelief that Germany had sounded France with an offer to acquiesce in a protectorate over Morocco if France would give Germany a free hand in Tripolitania and Cyrenaiea, and that France gave Italy intimation of the German plan. It has been officially denied, but it holds the field in Italy as an explanation of the pre- cipitate seizure. _ _</p>
        <p>-The fact is that Italy was cletermined_tograb</p>
        <p>the country, and grabbed it then lest she</p>
        <p>might find herself forestalled. Equity and fair dealing does not enter into such matters be- tween nations. I\l'r. McClure's narrative of the occupation, the so-called " revolt " of the Arabs, its merciless suppression, and such fighting as there was between the Italian troops and the Turkish regular forces, is chiefly interesting because of the strong light in which it sets the Italian soldier, of whom the author formed extremely• favourable impressions. 'Taking the campaign as a whole, it was unimpressive. The Italians played for safety. A peace was patched 'up without anything decisive having been accomplished. Turkey recognised the fact of the occupation of the coast, anU turned to meet her enemies in Europe. Whether the coast re- gion, not to speak of the hinterland, is worth. having now that Italy has got it is an obscure question, on which the book: throws some light but not much. There are districts that may be</p>
        <p>worth developing ; there are spots along the coast where there is soil, not sand; but It has yet to be proved that the New Romans can re- vive the wealth and glories of the civilisation of ancient days. Centuries of erosion and of drifting sand make the entire region a doubtful asset to the Italian nation.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-13_WidnesExaminer_art0039" n="art0039">
        <head>ALBERTA HARVEST.</head>
        <p>THE ALBERTA HARVEST.</p>
        <p>An increase of 17,069,942 bushels of grain over that garnered last year, is shown in the esti- mated crop yield for Alberta for the year 1913, in the figures compiled by the Provincial De- partment of Agriculture. The total estimated crop for the present year amounts to 81,535,000 bushels. During the year the crop area placed under cultivation totalled 2,809,310 acres, as compared with 2,391,752 acres during 1912, thus showing an increase in the cultivated area of 417,558 acres. The figures also show a very large increase in the amount of oats and barley grown, indicating that more attention has been given to mixed farming. A decrease is shown in the acreage sown in winter wheat, but this is more than counterbalanced in the yield of spring wheat.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-13_WidnesExaminer_art0040" n="art0040">
        <head>Fr NEUBERT F.S.M.C. by Exam.</head>
        <p>Fr NEUBERT F.S.M.C. by Exam.</p>
        <p>Consulting Optician.</p>
