      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-13_WidnesExaminer_art0041" n="art0041">
        <head>CHAMPION LAYERS.</head>
        <p>CHAMPION LAYERS.</p>
        <p>White Wyandottes Make Another Good Start in Competition.</p>
        <p>CHICKENS IN DECEMBER.</p>
        <p>We live in an age of laying competitions</p>
        <p>- - Hie White Wyandottes, which did so well in the twelve months' contest, ended a few weeks ago, have again started well in a contest began at Sedlescombe. The Utility Poultry Club, dealing with this new competition, reports: "The competition is run in two sections: (1) Small house and run 6ection,- and (2) semi-in- tensive section, and it comprises thirty-two pens of four birds each in each section, each compe-</p>
        <p>- titor entering a pen of four birds in each sec- tion. The houses in section 1 are of tongued- grooved matchhoard, measuring 121 f. by Sit. by 6ft. Gin, in front, sloping to Sit. Duck, and -divided in the middle by a, partition ; in each half there are eight birds (two pens of four birds each) to which is allowed a run of 300 square yards. The whole of the birds in section 2 are housed in a large house 401 t. by 201 t., Oft. high in front, sloping to sft. at back, with a run of about 1 acre attached. In unfavourable weather the birds- are confined to the house. In each section the birds are trap-nested; so that individual records are kept. . • The man- ager's repart, for the first four weeks shows that during the period 1,319 eggs have been laid, 767 in section 1 and 552 in section 2.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-13_WidnesExaminer_art0042" n="art0042">
        <head>WHITE WYANDOTTES LEAD.</head>
        <p>WHITE WYANDOTTES LEAD.</p>
        <p>Pen 21, white WyandotteS,' holds the lead in both sections, with a score of sixty-six eggs, value Bs. 101 d., in section 1, and a score of seventy eggs, value Bs. 10d., in section 2. At the end of the month fifty-five birds were,lay- ing in section 1, thirty-five in section 2. The 'manager reports that many birds are still back- ward, and that several in section 2 have been affected with colds, which unfortunately have spread, in spite of birds which were found suffering with -cold on arrival being iscilated. Three birds have died during the month, and have been sent to the Veterinary Department of the Board of Agriculture for examination. It appears from the report that the weather for the time of year has not been unfavourable for egg production, though the recent heavy rains and cold morning fogs have been trying to the birds. The following are the five leading pens: Pen 21; breed, White Wyandottes ; small house section, Ist month, eggs ,66, value Bs. 101 d. ; semi-intensive section, Ist mouth, eggs 70, value 8s; 100. 7, White Leghorns, 51, 7s. Bd.; 53, 7s. 111 d. 15, White Wyandottes, 46; ss. lid.; 54, 13s. lid. 10, White Leghorns, 52, '7s. 5d.; 27, 4s. Old. 8, White Leghorns, 36, 4s. 10d.; 48, 6s. 3id.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-13_WidnesExaminer_art0043" n="art0043">
        <head>DECEMBER CHICKENS.</head>
        <p>DECEMBER CHICKENS.</p>
        <p>It is still very early indeed to be thinking about chickens, but, says a writer in "Farm and Home," it will pay the farmer well to do so. When one is living under favourable con- ditions this is a practice that may be recom- mended very confidently, for such birds quickly grow into money. Especially is the plan advo- cated this season, for eggs being so extremely scarce .We may reasonably expect 'spring chicken's to be very expensive. The artificial method of rearing must be adopted, and a reli- able brooder should be chosen. Rearing chickens in December and January is by no means child's play, but it is worth the extra labour and expense by reason of the fact that the profit from early birds is so much greater than is the case with those that are brought out later in the season, when the supply is plenti- ful'. I should like to recommend every farmer, save those who live amid unfavourable sur- rour.dings,Ao make the attempt of hatching and rearing a few chickens during the next month or six weeks. Make but the experiment 111,, year, and I am confident that The results \\ more than justify the labour and expense.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-13_WidnesExaminer_art0044" n="art0044">
        <head>"PEACE AND WAIL"</head>
        <p>"PEACE AND WAIL"</p>
        <p>The November number. of "Peace and War," the new monthly published at 3d., has, among other articles bearing upon one of the most vital questions Of our times, the following: "A Mercenary or a Conscript Army," by Viscount Esher; "The Industrial Danger's of Conscrip- tion," by Sir Robert Hadfield ; "Spheres of In- fluence—and no Great Wars," by Sir Harry Johnston; 'The Clever Mr. Churchill," by Nor- man Angell; "The Racial Evils Of War," by the Hon. Rollo Russell. The policy of "Peace and War" is to advocate the cause of peace, and to give reasons for the faith it proclaims. " We believe in ,peace," say its promoters, " in the sense of the substitution of union for dis- union, Of partnership for rivalry, of comrade- ship for quarrel, only in part because it is a means to the end whereby men can more effi- ciently carry on their war with Nature ;_ much</p>
        <p>_ more because it is a means to the end of Mak- ing human intercourse of greater worth and finer quality, more purged of cowardly suspi- cions, of hatreds and misconceptions masquer- ading, as ,virtues, of cruelties and stupidities that darken life. It is a means to this end because the emergence of the finer things de- pends upon a form of human intercourse which can only rest Upon a basis of justice, and that in its turn upon understanding; both of which are in jeopardy .so long as they are subject to the mechanical hazard of physical force." Their intention is to bring before the mind of the European public the significance of a few simple, ascertainable, tangible facts in such fashion that they will frame. unconsciously a working hypothesis of international society, which will lead to deductions sufficiently cor- -rect and sufficiently widespread to do for the political groups what has already been done for the religious groups.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-13_WidnesExaminer_art0045" n="art0045">
        <gap reason="ocrQuality" quantity="1" unit="words">
          <desc>skipped/gap: fewer than 20 non-control characters after cleanup</desc>
        </gap>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-13_WidnesExaminer_art0046" n="art0046">
        <head>FARM AND GARDEN.</head>
        <p>FARM AND GARDEN.</p>
        <p>TREATMENT OF GRASS LAND. Field .work is so forward that many farmers at a loss td find suitable employment for</p>
        <p>leir men and horses, says " The Times." The winter treatment of grass land is giving</p>
        <p>3easonable occupation where the clvintages of riarrowing and manuring are properly • appre- ]iated4:- Example in the meLaanical and iltinurial treatment of 'pastures and meadows is finding an increasing number of followers.</p>
        <p>'Me practice has long since emerged from the experimental stage and will one day be a fixed feature in the winter routine. Already many farmers who formerly questioned the ability of grass land to respond to harrows and manures have seen reason to alter their views and have fallen into line with the advanced school of</p>
        <p>_ agricultural thought. With caution character- istic. of their class, they have proceeded •in graduated fashion, preferring to shape their plans in the light of personal experience. Changes effected in this way are the more likely to be successful and permanent, and it is a sig- nificant circumstance that few. beginners have regretted the step or halted until ,they had covered the whole of the. available area. Pas- tures are as sensitive to judicious treatment as</p>
        <p>_ uleadows, as has been shown by the increased stock-carrying capacity .of land that has been manured.</p>
        <p>ROYAL AGRICULTURAL SOCIETY. The report of the council of the Royal Agri- cultural Society presented to the general meet; ing On Wednesday, showed an increase in the membership of 127, compared with last year, the total number of governors and ,members being 10,434, compared with 10,307. During .the year 13 new governors and 700 new mem- bers were elected, but 6 heavy death roll, resig- nations,, and ,other causes , accounted for the difference between these totars and the, net gain. The successful show at Bristol 'yielded a balance of receipts over expenditure of £3,115, a result Upon which the Bristol and'sur- rounding districts are to be congratulated. The arrangements for next year's show at Shrews-, bury are well advanced, and the .particulars supplied foretell an exhibition equal in dimen- sions and representation to that at Bristol.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-13_WidnesExaminer_art0047" n="art0047">
        <head>SWINE FEVER WARNING.</head>
        <p>SWINE FEVER WARNING.</p>
        <p>Mr. G. L. Courthope, M.P., Chairman of the. Departmental Committee . appointed by the House of Commons to inquire into the swine fever question, speaking at Wadhurst (Sussex) on Saturday, said that great pressuXe was being put on his committee to advocate the sweeping away of all area restrictions and reliance upon serum only for the eradication of the disease. That was an ereor which had reduced pig farm- ing in the United States of America to an ap- palling state, that country last year losing Over 100,000,000 dollars through the failure of serum. Extensive use of serum only spread the disease, and no successful treatment had yet been dis- covered to stamp out the fever. The country must also 'beware of bringing- pressure to bear on the GoVernment to adopt the serum 'form of treatment, for if the pressure were great enough the Government might give way and the result could only be disastrous.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-13_WidnesExaminer_art0048" n="art0048">
        <head>POINTS OF TIIE BACON PIG.</head>
        <p>POINTS OF TIIE BACON PIG.</p>
        <p>The chief points in the pig are those which influence the value of the meat, says a Writer in "Farm and Home," and if breeders took them into full consideration when .selecting their stock they would realise more money than before. The value of a side of bacon depends upon abundance of the best meat and a mini- mum quality of the worst. The inferior meat is at the fore-end, and this is invariably larger in quantity when produced by pigs with short ears, heads and necks. These pigs, too, lay on too much fat, hence the ,fore-end, already in- ferior, is weighted with fat to such an extent that it diminishes ' the value of the:better-por- tions of the side. Well-bred bacon pigs pro- duce a maximum of meat on the belly, loin and hams, the fat not weeding 2 inches in thick- ness on the back, the belly well *caked with lean, the loins and the hams well furnished with tender, lean meat, which is the result of careful feeding and early maturity. A side of bacon should be "sizeable," as it is termed in the trade, neither too large nor too small, but such as will produce useful hams and loins and rasher bacon not too large in size. In these days small meat is preferred to the huge hams and other joints of the past. What is true of bacon is equally true of pork; the butcher wants what will sell—plenty of the best meat and a minimum of that which has no other resort but the sausage machine.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-13_WidnesExaminer_art0049" n="art0049">
        <head>IN THE GARDEN.</head>
        <p>IN THE GARDEN.</p>
        <p>Turn some old manure into the soil around rhubarb, and afterwards put on a dressing of rank manure for protection. The common sweet briar makes a beautiful hedge for the rose garden.. If left to themselves they make a strong thick hedge in a few years. Clumps of montbretia may be taken•up and divided where they have become too thick. Before • re-planting trench and manure the ground. Many roses die annually from bad cultivation. They are starved and neglected, become eaten up with insects, and expire from exhaustion rl3.s.e than from climatic conditions.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-13_WidnesExaminer_art0050" n="art0050">
        <head>SOOT AS A FERTILISER.</head>
        <p>SOOT AS A FERTILISER.</p>
        <p>Soot is not only a most valuable fertiliser, but the cheapest remedy for ridding plants of ' insect pests. It should not be used direct .form the flues, but be kept some months in a .lry place before using. It will clear winter greens of caterpillars. The best way to use it is to get a good sized tin; drill holes in the lid, and dredge the plants with the soot. It will 'keep roses clear of green fly and maggot, and keep chrysanthemums, celery, asparagus and other plants free from insect pests.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-13_WidnesExaminer_art0051" n="art0051">
        <head>DIGGING AND MANURING.</head>
        <p>DIGGING AND MANURING.</p>
        <p>The basis of all good cultivation of the' soil consists in keeping it dug as deeply as possinle for every crop; preserving it open in texture, by means of a hoe or fork, all through the growing months; and enriching it with manure by which is meant any such fertilising sub- stances as replace what the crops have taken out of it—to a sufficient degree to maintain it in good heart, says the, "Daily Telegraph" garden correspondent. If the soil is good for twenty inches deep it should be double dug' every autumn; in this way the top spit before digging will afterwards be the bottom one, and what was 'previously the bottom one will be brought to the surface. Thus for at least twenty inches -down the soil will be loosened, and rain, air, and sunshine will each help to make it more fertile.</p>
        <p>Shallow soils, ten or twelve inches deep only, must be treated differently; the top spit must not be buried, but must remain on the top; while for the depth of another spit below it the soil must be stirred and loosened as well as c:,n be done by the help of a good steel digging fork. Plenty of manure must be worked into it. In time the depth of soil will be so greatly increased that a portion of it may be brought to</p>
        <p>the surface. Whichever of these plans is adopted will be found useftil, and a greater space for the roots of plants to grow in will be provided than if the ground be simply eug one spade's depth, as is but commonly the practice. The best gardeners all dig their ground as deeply as possible, and one of the results is that their plants do not surfer „from drought or excessive rains as they otherwise would.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-13_WidnesExaminer_art0052" n="art0052">
        <head>CHARGE AGAINST A ST. HELENS MAN</head>
        <p>CHARGE AGAINST A ST. HELENS MAN</p>
        <p>John Grady, 28, Bold-street, was remanded at St. Helens on a charge of wounding Julia Toole. The Chief Constable said that the man was alleged to have thrown a kettle full of boiling water at Mrs. Toole, and she was in hospital in a serious state, the doctor saying that it would be a fortnight before she could appear in Court.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-13_WidnesExaminer_art0053" n="art0053">
        <head>Annual Prize Distrihutions at Heathsidey Beamont, Trinity, and Fairfield Schools.</head>
        <p>The Annual Prize Distrihutions at Heathsidey Beamont, Trinity, and Fairfield Schools.</p>
        <p>VALUE OF EDUCATED DEMOCRACY.</p>
        <p>It was prize week at the various branch Technical Schools in Warrington this week, as is customary prior to the Christmas recess. The year's work was reviewed, and at each the visit- ing members of the Education Committee gave advice and guidance to the pupils. The first gathering took place at Heathside. on Monday evening. Councillor JENKINS, who presided, said he looked back on his school life with the greatest pleasure. He was brought up in a wild, rough country. He had none of the advantages which they had to-day, but he was pleased to tell them, with all the disadvantages they had in his early days, they turned out some good scholars, men of whom they had no need to be ashamed, men who had taken -very high 'posi- tions in life. One of the boys whom he and his school-fellows considered one of the dullest in the school became Premier of South Australia. CAMP FOLLOWERS OF LABOUR.</p>
        <p>Mr. J. UPsoN, who was warmly received by the students, said he would like to add his word of praise for the very successful session they had last year, and express his sincere hope that the present 'session would be still more success- ful. He was sure all the students would like to express their sympathy with Mr. T. Eden, their headmaster, in his illness. Pupils went to that, school that they might improve their conditions in life, and improve the material advantages they might get, .so that they might not bbe camp followers in labour's great army.' The Education Committee rejoiced that so large a number of the youths of the town should take life so seriously, that they tried to fit them- selves for the duties of life, and they deserved the highest praise and all the credit that could be given them for going to those schools for that reason. They did not attend those classes alto- gether to increase their value on the labour market, but increase the value of life to them- selves; that was, they came for educational reasons.</p>
        <p>The schools led to development of character, and character was power, as well as discipline. They did not merely come under the discipline of the school, but under self-discipline, as their voluntary attendance at the. schools showed. The Education Committee had no power to force them to attend night schools, yet they had come, and had learned to say "No" to the temptations which encouraged participating in the rough games of the street corners. When they learned to say " No " to those minor temp-</p>
        <p>tatiOns they had learned to say "No" to the more powerful temptations that come to young men.</p>
        <p>ley, Frank G. Speakman, James A. Tilling and Henry N. Yearsley. The following passed the Preliminary Com- mercial Course (first year) Examination, and qualified for scholarships to the Commercial Institute: Thomas Brookes, Edward • Coghla, Walter Davies, Harold Gleave, Sydney Holden, Walter James, Edwin F. Jennings, William Livesey, Arthur Makin, Arthur B. Mellor, Richard, Morland, Albert Potter and Herbert B. Timnlins.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-13_WidnesExaminer_art0054" n="art0054">
        <head>Educated Democracy.</head>
        <p>Educated Democracy.</p>
        <p>The result of the training they received at the schools was that they came to recognise their personal responsibility in life. It was some- times said that the most perfect form of human government was that of an educated democracy, and the most tyrannical form that of an ignorant democracy. In a few years' time the pupils in the night schools would be the men of the town, the electors, and the men to go into the Town Council, and Upon than would fall the responsibility- of having a knowledge which would not be possessed by some of their fellows, and it was in the exercise of that responsibility they would find the value of the training they were now receiving.</p>
        <p>ifr.-dEORGE NEW-BURN, the acting .headmaster, proposed a vote of thanks, and mentioned the work done by Messrs. Crosfield's in causing their lads to attend evening schools. Mr. W. GREEN seconded, and Mr. J. MOORE MURRAY, the Direc- tor of Education, supported. The following were the prize and scholarship winners and the list of , pftsses : Prize-winners.—Horace, C.- L. Bonehill, George Burns, Arthur Dodd, Fred S. Ellison, George Hart, Edward Spenc:r, William Beswick, Wil- liam Hankinson, Adam Herbert, and George E. Pepperall. Scholarships, tenable at the Heathside Branch Technical School.—Trevor Cherritt, William Eaton, Frederick Leah, Thomas Palin, John Ryder, Harry Smart, William Stanton, John J. Cawley, Ernest Cox, and Ernest Garvin. Passes.—The following passed the preliminary technical course (first year) examination and qualified for scholarships to the Technical Institute: Harold Ashton, Horace J. Bellfield, John Bennett, Arthur Bridge, George S. Carter, Harold W. Crossley, William Dalton, Sidney E. Dutton, Arthur K. Forbes, James Gibson, Wil- liam E. Gill, Edward Holt, Thomas Hough, Frank Hughes, Alfred Joneys, John Lane, John Massey, Stanley Ogden, Walter Payne, Harold Peaker, Frank 0. Roberts, John Rowley, John Smith, Harold J. Stain, Harold S. Thomson, John Whittaker, Stephen Williams, and Thomas Worrall. .</p>
        <p>The following passed the preliminary com- mercial course (first year) examination and qualified for scholarships to the Commercial Institute: Norman G. Dean, Stanley Hayes, Cyril W. Hodgkiss, Alfred H. Miller, Percy J. Morton, David H. Orford, Frederick W. Palmer, Bertram Plinston (scholarship transferred to Technical Institute), William A. Taylor, Frank Turner, and Sidney Whitfield.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-13_WidnesExaminer_art0055" n="art0055">
        <head>BEAMONT BRANCH S, /100 L.</head>
        <p>BEAMONT BRANCH S, /100 L.</p>
        <p>A Successful Year's Work.</p>
        <p>At the gathering also held, on Monday even- ing, the chairman was Councillor Jenkins. Speaking on the subject of recreation, he said in his young days they were not encouraged to play football and similar sports, as the pupils wer-e permitted to do to-day. They in those days had none of the advantages pupils of to- day possessed, and they certainly had not the splendid training like what was now offered in the Technical Schools. UPSON said the Education Committee last week received the Inspector's report on the work at that school, and he thought he was revealing no secret'when he stated that the Inspector had ' used words of the highest praise for the work done. There were some, of course, who had not won prizes, but by the fact of their attendance at the school they would in a prize of an en- riched and fuller life. Mr. LONGSHAW, head master, in proposing a vote of - thinks, said he started the school 13 years ago with 33 students, and now there were 212 on the roll. Mr. J. MOORE 3SURRAT seconded. The following were the prize and scholarship winners and the list of passes : Prize-winners.--James A. Gilbert, Thomas Bolton, John H. Bridge, Joseph Heesom, Her- bert Middleton and Frank Wilson.</p>
        <p>Scholarships tenable at the Beamont Branch Technical School.—John Crolla, Samuel Dutton, Wilson Ogden, Ernest Phillips, Sidney Webb, John Bellis, tanley Brown, Thomas Crawshaw, John Lydon and Thomas O'Day. Passes.—The following passed the Preliminary Technical Course (first year) Examination, and qualified for scholarships to the Technical Insti- tute: John Allen, William Allen, George E. Bannon, Thomas Bellis, Arthur Buckley, Henry Coombs. James Earle, Joseph Eckersley, Aloy- sins Gilligan, Harry Gleave, William Knight, Charles A. Lea, Leonard Lees, Joseph Mannion, Joseph Mason, David Mould. John Pemberton, William Phcenix, James Platt, George Rough-</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-13_WidnesExaminer_art0056" n="art0056">
        <head>Pains after Eating.</head>
        <p>Pains after Eating.</p>
        <p>Nine times out of ten pains after eating are due to faulty action of the -stomach, liver, or bowels. Some people have to force themselves to eat even the daintiest of dishes because indigestion inevitably extracts its toll of misery and pain. But pains after eating, loss of appetite,. and the distressing symptoms of indigestion, liver trouble, or constipation have been banished, in thousands of cases, by Mother Seigel's Syrup. It is made from more than ten varieties of roots, barks, and leaves, which give to the stomach, liver, and bowels the help they oft-times need. Thirty drops of Mother Seigel's Syrup, taken after each meal for a few days, aid digestion, and will [enable you to enjoy your food without the fear of distressing pains to follow.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-13_WidnesExaminer_art0057" n="art0057">
        <head>AT TRINITY BRANCH.</head>
        <p>AT TRINITY BRANCH.</p>
        <p>Father Wilson and the Home.</p>
        <p>The distribution of Certificates to the young ladies took place on Wednesday evening at two centres, the first at Trinity Schools and the sectnid at Fairfield Schools. In both cases Father Wilson presided, and Mrs. C. Jackson Holmes presented the prizes. Mr. J. Moore Murray was also present. There was a good assembly of smiling girls at Trinity School when Father Wilson rose to address them. He said they had been learning the arts. and industries of the home. In olden days the school for those arts and industriee, was undoubtedly the home, and those things must always be practised in the home, and all they learned now would be of no great use unless they took their lessons borne with them and tried to put them into practice. He believed the home- was the best place for those arts yet, and it was only the social changes which had taken place, on account of their great indus- trial development and enormous sub-division of work, and the vast millions of capital in the hands of a few, that made it necessary for girls to go out and earn wages, which necessitated their learning those arts in the evening school. The home was no longer the school. It was to be earnestly wished that by the teachings in their evening schools they might gradually build up the traditions of the, homes again. (Ap- plause.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-13_WidnesExaminer_art0058" n="art0058">
        <head>MRS. J. HOLMES AND THE WORK.</head>
        <p>MRS. J. HOLMES AND THE WORK.</p>
        <p>At this stage Miss Vera Clarke presented Mrs. Jackson Holmes with a bouquet, and Clara Swift handed buttonholes to Mr. J. Moore Murray and Father Wilson. Mrs. JACKSON HOLMES, after expressing her thanks for the bouquet, said that she had been reading the report of their work and exanaina- lions, and she congratulated them that so many who had entered for the latter had passed. She often wondered if all of thepa appreciated the many advantages that were now placed in their way. It was quite common to come across people some years ago who could neither write nor read. She herself had written even love letters for such people when she was young. (Laughter.) Those wha were at the head of affairs, and who took such great interest in working for young people in every degree of life, looked to the young people to do their part as well; not only to do their best to attend even- ing classes, but to carry out in after life in a Practical way all they had been taught.</p>
        <p>WALKING ABOUT THE STREETS.</p>
        <p>Mr. J. Moons MURRAY said they should take</p>
        <p>the advice, and practise in the ho-me what they were taught .in school. To put it in another way, he said-the time would come, probably it had come, when they did not know what to do with, themselves during the, latter part of the week, after spending the first three nights in the evening schools. The first thing.that came into the minds of some of .them was to go out and walk about the- streets. They spent far too 'much time in doing this. Sankey-street, on a Sunday night, was no better than it was ten years ago when he first came to Warrington. He was sorry to say that they were mostly young girls who went there, and who ought to be at home.</p>
        <p>Passes.—The following have passed the Pre-</p>
        <p>liminary Domestic Coliise Examination of the Union of Lancashire and Cheshire institutes, and have been awarded scholarship's to the Technical Institute: Ada Bennett, Edith M. Bennett, Agnes Booth, Edith M. Bowers, Edith A. Bradshaw, Jane B. Brierley, Mabel P. Burns, Bessie R. Chaney, Ruth Cotterill, Amy M. Craven, Ada Fairhurst, Lilian Fenton: Annie Forsyth, Ada Garnett, Beryl Gifford (scholar- 'ship transferred to Commercial Institute), Marie Hardy, Lily Harrison, Edith Heywood, Frances M. Hewitt, Priseilla Hill, Eva Jervis, Elsie Johnson, Elizabeth Jones, Harriet E. Knowles, Lily Leah, Doris Leigh, Elizabeth Leigh, Doris McMillan, Winifred M. Malone, Amy Milner, Elizabeth Moore, Eva D. Nixon, Lena Nixon, Marie Perdue, Ada F. Philip, Mary Ryan Eveline A. Scrutton, Eliza Siddall, Nellie Smith, Phcebe Smith, Edna Thornton, May ' Walker, Emily Webb, Hilda Webb,, Harriet Williams, May Williams, Hannah Winstanley, Ethel Wright.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-13_WidnesExaminer_art0059" n="art0059">
        <head>FAIRFIELD SCHOOL.</head>
        <p>FAIRFIELD SCHOOL.</p>
        <p>A similar programme was gone through at the Fairfield School later in the evening, when the Chairman and Mrs. Jackson Holmes delivered speeches on the same lines as those at the Trinity School. Miss Kay presented Mrs. Jackson Holmes with a charming bouquet. Mr. J. Moore Murray again gave an address, and Miss Annie M. Ewart voiced the thanks of the students to the chairman and the lady vho so kindly distri- buted the prizes. Passes.—Sarah- Bradshaw and Ruth Russell, certificates in principles and practical cookery. The following have passed. the Preliminary Domestic Course Examination of the Union of .Lancashire and Cheshire Institutes, and have been awarded scholarships to the Technical In- stitute: Annie Booth, Dora Boscow, Gladys Caldwell, Ellen Crawley, Maude Crookes, Annie J. Donoghue, Maude Dutton, Laura A. H. Fern, Amy Goodfellow, Agnes Harrop, Alice Hares, Eileen Healing, Bessie V. Hewitt, Lily Hibbert, Hilda Houghton, Mary Hudson (scholarship transferred to Commerical Institute), Mary A. Hughes, Ethel Hunt, Bertha Been, Mary E. Lamb, Elizabeth M. Lancashire, Kathleen Langley, Violet Lea, borothy E. Lehrle, Lily Marsh, Mary E. Mason, Margaret Morrall, Jeanette Norton, Hetty Pemberton, Amy Red- dish, Elizabeth Rigby, Cecily J. Ryan, Edna Sadler, Hilda Sergeant (scholarship transferred to Commercial Institute), Angie Taker, Jane Warbrick, Doris M. Woods:</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-13_WidnesExaminer_art0060" n="art0060">
        <head>WIDNES MOTOR 'BUS LOSSES.</head>
        <p>WIDNES MOTOR 'BUS LOSSES.</p>
        <p>Alderman Owens, at Widnes Town Council on Tuesday, reported that the Motor 'Bus Com- nalttee.had underspent-RB6, but that was on the surface only.' The net loss on the half-year was actually £1,231, or £47 per week. It would take another six months to wipe off the debt on the garage, and then they would have a chance to breathe more freely. If, however, they could not conceive some plan for remedying this ex- pensive system they would be confronted with a,loss year by year of 2d. to 20. in the Motor 'buses had, however, come to stay, but any 'buses Purchased in the future would have to be purchased out of revenue. Councillor Welding said the Motor 'Bus Com- mittee were not as despondent as Alderman Owen. They had improved the position during the last six months, and he believed that in the next 13ix months they would reduce the loss by one-half.</p>
        <p>HORSES ELECTROCUTED AT ST. HELENS. William Alker, a farmer, was awarded £Bl and costs against the St. Helens Corporation, in the Liverpool County Cmirt, in respect of the death of two horses which were killed as, the result of a curious accident. It appeared that, following upon stormy weather, a telephone wire came into contact ,with one of the defend- ants' electric tramway wires and, when charged with a high current of electricty, fell upon the horses and electrocuted them.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-13_WidnesExaminer_art0061" n="art0061">
        <p>WOMEN'S QUESTIONS.</p>
        <p>Imitation Furs Can Never Compare</p>
        <p>With Real Furs.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-13_WidnesExaminer_art0062" n="art0062">
        <head>QUEEN DISLIKES THE TANGO</head>
        <p>QUEEN DISLIKES THE TANGO</p>
        <p>The King and Queen, like the German Em- peror, do not approve of the "new dancing." Her Majesty dislikes extremes, whether in matters of dress or manners, and her favourite dances are those of the Victorian period. She is fond of the quadrille and the waltz, with an occasional polka, but the. lancers seldom, if ever, appear on Royal programmes. It is, therefore, not surprising that their Majesties should have expressed ,a decided opinion that the Tango is not suitable for ballrooms, and</p>
        <p>neither it, nor any of the negroid importations from across the Atlantic, will be permitted at Court, or at any of the great houses when Royalty is present. The -Tango is not an American dance. Attempts have been made to trace it back to ancient times, but it is really of Spanish origin. For the time being it has captivated all classes of society, and, despite Royal disfavour, it seems likely to carry every- thing before it in social entertainments this winter. It even threatens the fancy dress ball, notwithstanding the fascination which, the op- portunity of "dressing up" has for the terpsi- chorean enthusiast:</p>
        <p>The Picture Ball.</p>
        <p>Princess Louise, Duchess of Argyll, occupied</p>
        <p>the Prince of Wales' box at the Picture Ban given in the Albert Hall fa aid of the invalid kitchens of Lbndon. A most interesting group was Lady Constance Hatch's Cosinay minia- tures, the effect\of which was much heightened by a black velvet screen, against which, in diamond studded frames, the heads appeared. Lady Constance wore a dress that belonged to an ancestress who died a hundred years ago. Baroness d' Erlanger's pictures from mosaics of the first to the tenth centuries, representing saints and angels, in dresses of Vivid colour- ings, were very much admired, for they were a unique 'conception and beautifully carried out. Assyrian, Egyptian, Greek and Boman schools of painting were Most effectively portrayed in the form of a frieze of black velvet.</p>
        <p>Christmas Shopping. The spirit of Christmas is abroad again, and the shops overflow with a bewildering variety of novelties and the most tempting presents. Only to look at them is A sheer delight, although one may be conscious that possession will never be ours. Wise people are accomplishing their Christmas shopping betimes this year, and the shops have done their best to be in readiness. There is a' fashion even in Christmas gifts, and every .year some article or accessory looms largely before customers. This season, thither, clear or clouded is accorded a place of honour, more especially amber beads, and these are often of barbaric proportions. They form an acceptable gift, and are most effective in con-</p>
        <p>trast with several shades now much donned. Coral leads are favoured, too, so are beads of Chinese jade, in pink or shaded green and white, but the latter are more expensive than amber. Buckles 'for evening shoes are popu- lar gifts, and fans and gloves are as much sought after as ever before. Quite a safe selec- tion is one of the. many useful and beautiful ,bags. There are capacious bags for household shopping, and those of gold and silver mesh for the afternoon. The dinner party and the theatre are also amply provided for, and bags</p>
        <p>for these functions are of the daintiest materials, brocade, and especially moire, being much in evidence.</p>
        <p>The Decline of the Black Hat. A great French artist is of opinion that Eng- lishwomen should wear large hats, and that the small toque and cap, with their bizarre, up- standing feathers, for Which there is such a vogue at present, are not suited to the English face. But the large hat is still worn by many women ,whom it suits, and in the spring it will doubtlegs come back again, for people arc already tiring of the tiny hat. The all black velvet hat has evidently had .its day, and is being replaced with a confection that matches the dress worn with it. The fantastic mount also shows signs of having reached the zenith of its popularity, for milliners are using one or two ' large, but beautiful flowers, carelessly fastened to the side of the hat, on a number of the latest models. The flowers are generally of velvet, beautifully made, the preference being given to gardenias, Christmas roses and pansies.</p>
        <p>Magpie Suits in Duvetyn. Black duvetyn has quite replaced velvet for the moment in Paris, and is making headway ' on this side of the Channel. It has a shiny sur- face, but less so than velvet, it also hangs more gracefullysthan the latter fabric, and therefore lends itself admirably to the draperies of coats, and the folds of skirts. For tailored suits it is just now extremely popular, and when trimmed with skunk is at its best. A white waistcoat, also of duvetyn, adorned with.black buttons, is a favourite relief to one of these costumes. Coats and skirts are this winter built frequently of striped and fancy materials. Batine, more especially in green, striped with black, has met with much favour. Plain fabrics are equally popular, and diversity in materials and styles is the order of the day. Never has there been such an unlimited choice. Waists may be high, normal or abnormally low; coats short or fairly -long, and endless in variety of styles and decoration.</p>
        <p>Imitation Furs.</p>
        <p>The pelts of the hare, rabbit,, cat and some species of rats and weasels are all impressed into the service of Dame Fashion, but though the skill of the furrier is manifested' in the dressing and dyeing of these pelts, making them quite presentable, t'they cannot compare with the real furs they are lintended to imitate, and always look what they are. For young girls, the necklet is a most sensible, as well as siliart idea, for it takes so little fur that a really good one may be indulged in without, extravagance. Fortunately for women of small means, who yet like to look smart and pp-to-date, a com- bination of velvet and fur is highly favoured this winter. A long stole of We former trimmed with ii narrow band of, good fur, with a big muff to match, is a desirable possession, and " goes " \\ ith any dress. Even the severest coat' and skirt can be.worn at an afternoon function, if relieved and smartened by one of these hand- some-looking sets. 'Chiffon or net ruffles arc' often added to the muffs, and are kept in place by pretty buckles, small in size.</p>
        <p>Madame de Areas. The death of Madame de Arms was 'a great, blow to the venerable Empress Eugenie—the (Continued :in nut citt4mn.)</p>
      </div>
