        <p>WOMEN'S QUESTlONS—continued.</p>
        <p>Mater Dolorosa of modern times—although the 'event had been expected for some time. The: Empress sent no flowers, but a wreath made of ' rushes fro the plant brought from South m Africa, that grew on the spot where the Prince' Imperial ma, his death. Madame de Arcos's parents were Well known to the Empress, and olthough the former never occupied a position in the Iloyal ,household, she was, after she became a widow, the constant companion of the Empress, 'and the two friends frequently spent the autumn in Scotland during the life of Queen Victoria, who generally lent the Em- press a, house there. Madame de Arcos was , married in 1859, and became 'a widow in 1872. 'She was nearly 80 years old at the time of her deutla. • . .</p>
