      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-13_WidnesExaminer_art0073" n="art0073">
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, DECEMBER 13, 1913.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-13_WidnesExaminer_art0074" n="art0074">
        <head>SOME CHRISPIAS JEATING.</head>
        <p>SOME CHRISPIAS JEATING.</p>
        <p>Dreams, Visions and Stories of the Spirit World.</p>
        <p>"MILLIONS OF SPIRITUAL CREfITLIQES WALK THE EARTH UNSEEN, BOM WHEN WE WAKE aND WHEN WE SLEEP."</p>
        <p>I may tell you at the onset that I know noth- ing whatever about the bourne from which, as Shakespeare says, no traveller returns, to tel; us as to its locality, or to give us any particulars as tà. its composition; but while we have no direct evidence of modern times that there is such a world, we have had sundry mysterious signs, or, as I should pre- fer to term them, indications, that there is a certain affinity betwn the spirit that inhabits our bodies, and the ether or space cvith aich we are surrounded, that to a thoughtful and highly sensitive person is very apt to carry him out of himself—or herself. . There are very few families who have not had certain signs or imagined they have had warn- ing of a Certain great event that was about to happen, and which has given them a presenti- ment as to what they might expect. Sir Walter Scott, in his book of demonology and witch- craft, gives us some very interesting details of this character. We are told of a very singular case which happened before the time of the telegraph or steam engine.</p>
        <p>about the time he heard her voice she was call- ing him; she was very ill and hardly expected to live.</p>
        <p>health depends. Every white corpuscle that swims in our veins is now declared to be the armed knight .of Life for ever on the look-out ,f or the microbe fiend of Death. Day and night, sleeping and waking, the white knights of Life are constantly on the alert, for on their vigi- lance hangs our existence. Sometimes, however, the • iniading microbes come in, not in com- panies, but in battalions, and we die. But recent researches into the nature and "constitution of our mental and spiritual parts would suggest almost inconceivable possibili- ties as to our own nature; they leave us as- tounded-on the brink of a new world, of which until recently most of us .were unaware. • A very curious instance is told in which a vision or dream was instrumental in preventing a murder. It is recorded of a daughter of Mrs. Rutherford, at Ederton, the granddaughter of Sir Walter Scott. This lady dreamed more than once that her mother had been murdered by a black servant. She was so much upset by this that she returned home, and to her great astonishment, and not a little to her dismay, she met on entering the house, the very black servant she 'had seen in her dreams. He had been engaged in her absence. She prevailed upon a gentleman to watch in an adjoining room during the .following night. About three o'cloCk in the morning the 'gentleman heard footsteps on the stairs. He came out and met the servant carrying a quantity' of coals. On being asked where he was going with them, be answered 'confusedly that he was going to mend the mistress's fire, which, at three o'clock in the morning in the middle of summer, was evi- dently impossible. On further investigation a strong knife was found hidden in the coals. The lady escaped, but the man was subsequently hanged for murder, and before his execution he confessed that he intended to assassinate Mrs. Rutherford.</p>
        <p>DO SPIRITS WALK AMONGST US?</p>
        <p>The spirits of men do not die, they still live and walk abroad amongst us. It was a fine and true thought, uttered by Mr. Disraeli in the House of Commons, on the death of Richard Cobden, "that he was one •dl those men who, though not present, were still members of that House, who were independent of dissolution, of the caprice of constituencies, and even of the course of time."</p>
        <p>There is no need to be a Pagan in order to people the world with invisible denizens, with which our forefathers peopled every hill and glen and every flowery Mead. No doubt there has been, and is, a great deal of superstition and nonsense talked about ghosts, and a very creepy and weird atmosphere has plagued the whole regiOn in which these ideas have been in vogue, but these should not keep the resolute soul from its appointed ,path; and the path of all mankind is to try all things, to prove all things, and to hold fast to that which is good. A very curious and striking instance is told of the father of a son who had sailed in a vessel called the "Strathmore," an emigrant ship out- ward bound from the Clyde. The father saw one night in a dream the ship floundering amid the waves, and saw that his son, with some others, had escaped safely to a desert island near which the wreck had taken place. .He was so much impressed with this vision that he wrote to the owner of the "Strathmore," telling him what he had seen. His information was scouted, but after awhile the " Strathmore " was over.-de and the owner got very uneasy. Day followed day, and still no tidings of the missing ship. Then, like Pharaoh's butler, the owner remembered his sins, and one day hunted up the letter describing the vision. It supplied at least a theory to .account for the vessel's dis- appearance. All outward bound vessels were requested to look for any survivors on the island indicated in the vision. These orders being obeyed, the survivors of the " Strathmore " were found exactly where the father had seen them. Taken in connection with other instances of a similar nature, what can be said of excepting that it almost necessitates the supposition that onr spirits have a latent power of Which as yet we are not conscious?</p>
        <p>Air. Ed. H. Lipsitt, of Tralee, tells the follow- ing curious , experience: When. quite a boy, lie says, some ten years .of age, he happened to sleep 'one night in the same ropin with a young friend 'of about his own age. There existed a very strong sympathy between them. He says lie got up very early and went out for a short walk, leaving his friend fast asleep in bed. He went in the direction of a well-known lake in the district. After gazing for some moments at the silent water, he espied a large black dog making for him. He turned his back and fled, the dog following him for some distance. His boots then being in a bad condition, one of the soles came of/ in the flight; however, he got away unmolested by the dog; but lie was very Much amazed. on entering the room where his young friend. , who was just rubbing his eyes and yawning, related to him his adventure, word for word, 4escrilAg even the colour of the (log and the very boa '(the right one) the sole of which gave, .waY•" *. Sir Walter Scott . says:</p>
        <p>There is something in this ancient superstition Which, errlng as it is, our fancy loves!</p>
        <p>Wild as is the theory that, some of the stories may suggest, it is less fantastic than it would have appeared to our grandfathers a hundred years ago, if they had been asked to believe that at the end of the 19th century portraits would be taken by the sun, that conversation would be carried on across a distance of thousands of miles, that a ray of "light would be made the agent for transmitting the human thoughts across an abyss which no wire had ever spanned, and that by a simple mechanical arrangement which can be carried in a man's hand, it would be possible. to. reproduce the words, voieb and accent of the dead. The photograph, the tele- graph, the telephone, the phonograph were all more or less latent in, what seemed to our fore- fathers; the folly of 'Benjamin Franklin. Who knows but that in telepathy we may have the faint foreshadowing of another latent force, which 'may be destined to cast into the shade even the marvels of electrical science? The fairy tales of science are innumerable, and, like the fairy tales of old romance,. they thefa arsee fascinated el lacking horrible.e kti the imagination Oiifnm engnarttiimon, yearstehvee tragicnothing nela di least so aenot imaginative of men as the theory of disease which t • forms every drop of blood in our bodies into the lists of which phagocyte and tehfin the ah microbe wage the mortal strife on whieh our</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-13_WidnesExaminer_art0075" n="art0075">
        <head>DREAMS THAT CAME TRUE.</head>
        <p>DREAMS THAT CAME TRUE.</p>
        <p>The assassination of Mr. Percival in the lobby of the House of Commons was foreseen in the Minutest details by John Williams, a Cornish mine manager, eight or nine days before the assassination took plape. Three times over he dreamed that he saw a small man dressed in a blue coat and white waistcoat enter the lobby of the House of Commons, when immediately another person, dressed in a snuff-coloured coat, took a pistol from under his coat and shot the little man in the left breast. -On asking who the little man was, he was informed that it was Percival, Chancellor of the Exchequer. He was so much impressed by the dream that he consulted his friends as to the desirability of warning Mr. Percival. Unfortunately they dissuaded him, and on May 13th the news came that Percival had been killed on the 11th. Some time afterwards, when he saw a picture of this scene, it reproduced all the thrilling details of this thrice dreamed vision. Some people would call this a coincidence, and I have heard others venture the opinion that the spirit is 'capable of wandering from the body while it sleeps, and while this gentleman slept, his spirit had, strange to say, Wandered in the precincts of the House of Commons. My grandfather, who was a good old local preacher among the Independent Methodists, tells •a very funny story of a dream or vision he once had. He says* it was the night of the Walking Day, and he found, when he got home, that he had lost his purse. He went .to bed and dreamt he was standing on his head against a stump, and while in that position he saw his purse drop out of his pocket and fall at the foot of the post. He was so deeply impressed by this dream that when he got up the nelt morn- ing he went to the place and found the identical spot where the stump or post was, and, on examining round it, sure enough, there was his purse intact, with the money all right. Dreams and visions were vouchsafed to our forefathers. Why should they not be given to us to-day? We are told that the world is getting more materialised and practical every day, but in the face of 'this assertion, science is demonstrating to us that there are elements and agencies that we cannot understand, and that makes' Us stand awestricken at the un- fathomable mysteries of the unseen world.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-13_WidnesExaminer_art0076" n="art0076">
        <head>SOUND TRANSMISSION.</head>
        <p>SOUND TRANSMISSION.</p>
        <p>Marconi, the great discoverer of the wireless telegraphy, tells us that in the very ether or air we breathe there exists a latent power that can transmit sound through a mountain range, or across a trackless sea, and who knows but what this power, which he can harness and make to do his will, may not be the same power which Madam Blavatsky and her followers say is capable of carrying thought from people who have an affinity one for the other through space of almost illimitable expanse? Those of us who may not have passed through the transforming stage of a sound conversion cannot realise the beautiful divine feeling which Pervades the soul Of one who has been soundly converted, and very few can explain why the countenance of a person gets transformed from a hard commonplace to a radiant .attractiveness that was never there before. I have heard people explain this transformation by saying that the affinity of the known with the unknown works this miraculous change, and who is there of us can gainsay this? A relation of mine, who was studying for the ministry in Manchester, was reading one night in his study. It would be about 10 o'clock, when. he fancied' he heard his name called by a very familiar voice, which he at once recognised as the voice of a dear little lady friend. He was so impressed with this that he at once lay down his book-, caught the 11 o'clock train from i'ionchester, and Came on here, to find that</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-13_WidnesExaminer_art0077" n="art0077">
        <head>44,i4.1(.8.v.4.14 44,14++ +.44+44+44 .14.1.4,,-Hii.*14.1444+ ++44 +.l4+i</head>
        <p>44,i4.1(.8.v.4.14 44,14++ +.44+44+44 .14.1.4,,-Hii.*14.1444+ ++44 +.l4+i</p>
        <p>TO PROVE LIFE AFTER DEATH.</p>
        <p>When we come to visions pure and simple, one of the best known leading cases is that re- corded by Lord Brougham, who was certainly one of the hardest-headed persons that ever lived, a .Lord Chancellor trained from his youth up to weigh evidence. The story is. given as follows in the, first volume of Lord Brougham's memoirs: "A most remarkable thing happen to me, so remarkable that I must tell the from the beginning. After I left the High Selx 1 I went with a friend to attend classes at University. There were no divinity classes, but we.: frequently in our walks discussed niany grave Subjects, amongst others the immortality of the soul and the future state. This subject and the possibility of the dead appearing to the living, were subjects of much speculation, and we actually committed, the •folly of drawing up .an agreement written with our blood to the effect that whosoever died first should appear to the other, and thus solve any doubts we Dad entertained of the life after' death. After we had finished our studies, my friend went out to India, where he had got an appointment in the Civil Service. He seldom wrote me, and after the lapse of a number of years I had almost for- gotten hixn. One day I was taking a warm bath, and while lying in it, and enjoying the com- forts of the heat, I turned my head round, looking towards the °hair upon which I had deposited my clothes. On the chair sat my friend G. . . . looking calmly at me. 11Ow I got out of the bath I know • not, but on recov- ering my senses I found myself sprawling on the floor. The apparition or whatever had taken the likeness of my friend had vanished." This vision had produced such a shock that he says he had no desire to talk about it; so sfronglY was he affected by it that he wrote down the date, December 19th., "No doubt," he proceeds, "I had fallen asleep and the ap- pearance presented so distinctly before my eyes was a dream, I cannot for a moment doubt. I recollected quickly enough our old discussion, and the bargain we had made. I could not dis- charge the thought from my mind that 'my friend was dead, and that his appearance to me was to be received as a proof that there was a future state. This was on December 19th, 1799." On October, 1862, Lord Brougham adds, as a postscript, "I have just been copying from my journal the account of this strange dream, and now. to finish the story begun 60 years ago. Soon after my return to Edinburgh there arrived a letter from India announcing my friend's death, and stating that he' died on December 19th, 1799." • Shakespeare says:</p>
        <p>There are more things in heaven and earth, Horatio, Than are dreamt of in your philosophy.</p>
        <p>WILLIAM FURNESS Fern Lea, Great Sankey.</p>
      </div>
