      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-13_WidnesExaminer_art0086" n="art0086">
        <head>CHILD DRINKERS.</head>
        <p>CHILD DRINKERS.</p>
        <p>Amazing Revelations About a Warrington Public-House.</p>
        <p>A MAXIMUM FINE IMPOSED.</p>
        <p>Amazing revelations w6re made in Warring- ton Police Court on Tuesday about drinking amongst boys and girls. The police found these comparative children not only drinking, but, along with grown-up men and women, acting in a noisy mann4r. As a result of the police raid, the maximum penalty was imposed on George Eastwood, licensee of the Crown and Sceptre Buttermarket-street, who was charged with permitting 'riotous conduct on his licensed Premises on Saturday, November 22nd. Mr. A. Browne appeared for the defendant, who was unable to attend through illness, and Mr. W. 11. J. Jenkins represented the interests of Messrs. Greenall, Whitley and Co., the pro- prietors. THE CHIEF'S 'STORY.</p>
        <p>The Chief Constable stated tlukt those pro- ceedings arose out of the 'fad that periodical visits were paid to the licensed-premises in the town. Inspector Forster, in company with P.C. Bennett, on the date in question ware paying these periodical visits to a number of the licensed • premises, and at 9 p.m. visited the Crown and Sceptre in Buttermarket-street. They entered the premises by the side door leading from Bank-street and founil the passage packed with men and women standing there drinking. They then visited the billiard room awl found this .densely packed, principally by youths and girls 16 or 17 years of age. In one -part of the room was a notorious character, holding the hand of another man, and those two were endeavouring to sing, in fact the whole of the company, in the room was endeavouring to sing. To use the words of the, Inspector, the noise was "most hideous." The police visited other parts of the house, and there the same condition existed, and the Inspector would tell them that a great percentage of the persons on the premises were intoxicated, • although they could not swear thy were drunk, but certainly they were. under the influence of drunk.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-13_WidnesExaminer_art0087" n="art0087">
        <head>LANDLADY RESENTS.</head>
        <p>LANDLADY RESENTS.</p>
        <p>Inspector Forster saw Mrs. Eastwood am] asked tO see her husband. She stated that her husband was ill in bed, and she resented the</p>
        <p>CLOTUS PRAISED BY RUSKIN. The St. George's Guild, of which uskin, was master, founded at Laxey, in the Isle of Mari, a mill for the production of good honest cloths. Any- one needing Dress Lengths, Suitings, Rugs, etc., as recently supplied to H.M. the King, of unim- peachable quality, and beautiful design, is invited to send for patterns to'</p>
        <p>THE RUSKIN HOMESPUN, 110, St. George's Mill. Lames', Isle of Man; or G. HOLYROYD &amp; SON, 110, Station-street. Huddersfield.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-13_WidnesExaminer_art0088" n="art0088">
        <p>interference of the police, and was most abusive to the officers. He was quite unable to explain anything to her and she absolutely refused to listen- to him, acting more like a woman \ far advanced in • drink than one responsible for licensed premises. The Inspector- told her he would report the case, and she replied "Be quick or you might forget." The Inspector aild the constable left the premises shortly after nine o'clock, and between that time and a second visit at 1,0.30 Inspector HodgkisS and Sergt. Reddington were in the vicinity of the house, and heard the row and disorder con- tinued the whole of,the time. .</p>
        <p>One thing the Chief Constable wished to point out in particular was that a man in a drunken condition w.ent into the Pelican Hotel, but almost immediately left. He then went into the Crown and Sceptre, but the police failed to. see him come out. The police visited the house at 10.30 p.m., and immediately they got in they were met by a woman carrying a baby twelve months old.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-13_WidnesExaminer_art0089" n="art0089">
        <head>BABY THERE FOR AN HOUR.</head>
        <p>BABY THERE FOR AN HOUR.</p>
        <p>The woman was questioned as to how long she had been on the premises, and she replied she had been there sixty minutes, and admitted having had drink. The police found exactly the same state of affairs prevailing as on the previous occasion.</p>
        <p>- The statement of the Chief Constable was corroborated by Inspector 'Forster and P.C. Bennett.</p>
        <p>Mr. Browne asked. Inspector Forster if on the day in question the Widnes football team visited Warrington, and on receiving an answer in the affirmative, intimated that that was his .only question.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-13_WidnesExaminer_art0090" n="art0090">
        <head>LAMEN7ABLE CASE.</head>
        <p>A LAMEN7ABLE CASE.</p>
        <p>Mr. Browne stated that he was not, going to dispute what Inspector Forster or Constable Bennett had said, and he ,had no doubt that Ins-peCtor Hodgkiss, if he was called, would give a similar truthful story. It was a most lament- able case. Mr. Eastwood had kept that house for six years; and the Filecutters' Arms, in Fennel-street, for three years before that, and there had 'been .no .conviction or serious com- plaint made up to this time. Defendant had</p>
        <p>been suffering from chronic rheumatism for nine months, with the result that he could not get downstairs only on rare occasions, and thus he was unable to conduct the business himself, and he left it in charge of his wife and a man named.Fairbrother. It was entirely against the defendant's wish that anything of this kind should have taken place. They had heard the police evidence 'that the house was so densely packed that :they could not get through, and the billiard room was also densely packed. They had also heard that the snug and the parlour were packed. He submitted that that day was not an ordinary occasion. The Widnes and Warrington football match was played on that day, and a very large number of persons came from Widnes, with the result that every public- house in town was packed. The place absolutely got beyond the control of Mrs. Eastwood and Mr. Fairbrother. The billiard-room would seat 50 or 60 people, the. passage would hold a large number, the parlour would seat 20 or 30, the snug 12 or 15, and every passage was packed, and he asked what was a woman to do under those circumstances.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-13_WidnesExaminer_art0091" n="art0091">
        <head>"LOST HER TEMPER."</head>
        <p>"LOST HER TEMPER."</p>
        <p>Unfortunately she lost her temper. It was absolutely impossible for any landlady or land- lord to properly conduct a house which was invaded by something like 120 or 150- people, wherr„ perhaps, on other Occasions they would only find something like 20, 30 or 40 present. It Was only a rare occasion that the house was in such a state, and the owners would see that there was no repetition. Mr. W. H. J. Jenkins said that ale owners by no means approved of conduct of this kind, and as Mr. Browne had intimated, steps would at once-be made to see that defendant did not con- duct any licensed house the property of the owners, whom he represented. That was set- tled. The character of the proceedings in that house on the night in question were such that it would be impossible for tlit owners for one moment to countenance, and they understood that the conduct of a licensed house should be carefully safeguarded. There should be no neg- lect Or inattention on the part of the tenant which could bring about a state of things, as in that case. He intimated that any wish from the Bench would receive the most careful attention of the owners.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-13_WidnesExaminer_art0092" n="art0092">
        <head>MAXIMUM PENALTY.</head>
        <p>THE MAXIMUM PENALTY.</p>
        <p>, The Bench retired, and upon re-entering the Court the Chairman, .Councillor H. Roberts. stated that they felt the case was as bad as .it possibly could be, and they failed to see any redeeming point in it. Steps should have been taken long ago to make proper supervision pos- sible. They had decided to inflict the maximum penalty of £lO ;Ind costs.</p>
      </div>
