      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-13_WidnesExaminer_art0100" n="art0100">
        <head>EXAMINER, SATURDAY, DECEMBER 13, 1913.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, DECEMBER 13, 1913.</p>
        <p>"ft Merry Christmas!"</p>
        <p>—from the Shops.</p>
        <p>How the Business Part of the Town is Providing an Arabian Night Entertainment.</p>
        <p>NO TIME TO BE DISMAL.</p>
        <p>"Dark and dull night, flue hence away, And give the honour to this day,. That sees December turned to May.</p>
        <p>Why does the chilling winter's morne Smile like a field beset with corn? Or smell like to a meade new-shorne. Thus on the sudden. Come and see The cause Nsilly things thus fragrant be." (Herrick.)</p>
        <p>Yes! Come and see all the charming thing, which are displayed in the beautiful shops Warrington! Come and seo pretty things whic: will delight,your hearts and eyes. Come ii hundreds, do not come alone, bring your uncles cousins, aunts, and don't leave behind tin children, for they will draw your attention to scores of pretty things which attract their eyes, until at last you may imagine that you are liv- ing in the wonderful realm of Arabian Nights' Land. But no matter, come! That is what we ask of you. Come and see! Oh! what shops of toys! What delightful displays of Christmas cheer! What a glow of warmth emanates from the shop windows just now! What feasts, in store for bright-eyed boys and girls! And the empty pockets for father and mother! Well! even that is quite all right, and 'tis mom* well spent to give the bairns a good time once a .year. One can't be a skinflint at Christmas! We have no room for any snarling Scrooge in our midst, and we have the' 'bright lights and glorious colours peeping at us from all the shop windows in town, and wishing us—as far as lights and colours can wish us—a real, cheery, good, old- fashioned Merry Christmas! And we mean to have it, too! Who was it who suggested the idea of the year 'l3 being a black, dismal, dole- ful year? I don't know, but if that narrow- minded Mt. Pessimist and his rival, Mr. Opti- mist, took a walk round the town and stopped here and there, arrested by the display in the windows, I fancy he would stop and .say %to his friend:</p>
        <p>"Old 'l3 means to finish up brightly! He might have kept up his reputation and died dismally." "Fiddle-sticks," says Mr. Optimist, "there's no time to be dismal. Just come with me and I'll show you a thing or two." " Where are you going?" "Into the shops, of course. I'm going to have a gay time and spend all my money. Come along." " No, thanks. I'm not coming. Spend my money! Catch me."</p>
        <p>"Look here, Mr. Pessimist, of Mess'rs. Skin- flint, ‘Seurface, and Co., dealers in Christmas cheer destroyers, didn't you want to see our shops?" "Of course I did."</p>
        <p>"Well, wait until you've seen our Christmassy town and then you'll change your name double quick."</p>
        <p>kinds of things suitable for ladies. There is plenty of choice. . Kings and queens,. princes and princesses in the:class:of 'trade of which I speak'are Messrs. W. Hodgkinson and Son, Messrs. J. and. W. Dutton, Messrs. J. Atkinson and Son (three shops), Mr. W. T./Williams, Mr. T, Grime, T. L. Corbett, Messrs.' W. Isherwood and Co., Messrs. Lee and Clarke, Madame Lillie, the Co- operative Society, And Messrs. Robinson Bros., \Vinwick-street.</p>
        <p>If you feel you'll like to go farther afield -ou'll find scores of pretty things al- Kendal, Elne's in Manchester; Messrs. Watts and Co., 2ompton House, Liverpool; and Messrs. G. H. -,ee and Co., Basnett-street, Liverpool. Photographs make the best of. Christmas cards, and pictures of oneself dressed after the Christmas shopping has' been done and the Christmas party costumes are available, will make lasting and valuable souvenirs. Never forget that "a thing of beauty is a joy for ever; its loveliness increases; it-will never pass into nothingness." So that the memory of this glad time will never fade away. but remain as a Pernianency, the best services of Mr. Birtles, Sankey-street; Mr. Parkinson, 'Bridge-street; and Mr. W. Burrell, Manchester-road, will be placed cheerfully at your service.</p>
        <p>scent bottles, ,chain buttons, silver enam- elled boxes—they Would do nicely for Christmas bonbons. Golf and whist pries! Christmas pudding sets! Pendants, cake baskets, rings of all sizes, engagement and wedding rings—but— can a wedding ring be a Christmas present? I'm not good at canundrums, so I shall leave that one for., all the would-be Benedicts. At least I've done my best. I've told them where they can be bought, and I don't mind helping, them a bit more.</p>
        <p>What use is a wedding ring without a nice house of furniture? Well! just trot round and look at Messrs. J. S. Garland, Pendleburys, Jos. Mounfield and Sons, Mr. E. Coates, Messrs. Wilcock and Sadler, Tanner Bros., Mr. Sam Green, and Mrs. Marsh. I fancy you'll have a stock to choose from. Tables, cupboards, sideboards,- suites for draw- ing, dining or bed rooms; chairs, everyone guar- anteed EASY, sofas, bedsteads, pictures, fenders, carpets; oh! anything you want from a picture hook, a knife cleaner, or a rolling pin to a dignified hall stand or a Grand piano! But, then, you wouldn't think of getting a nice house like this at Christmas time mintis a vtredding cake. If you live in Stockton Heath I would suggest you tried Hayes Bro.; in town you would get equally well served by. the Win-' marleigh Cafe, Messrs. Toft, Messrs. Carter and Son, Mr. Prince, Mr. J. E. Hannan (Parker- street), and Miss Ashurit. • • They would,;alsa supply you with pyramids of cream crackers, sponge cakes,' tarts, real Eng- lish buns, oh! arn't those buns just delicious?• I know. And meat pies and biscuits and every- thing nice to suit the most fastidious palate! And then after this delightful feast take a peep into Mr. Hewitt's ' shop and there you'll find your curiosity aroused, and you'll say "My , word! that would suit my drawing-room, my dining-room, and so on." Snap it up at once. He who hesitates is lost! No! your house isn't complete, I was nearly forrtting. You can't guess: Why you want a gramophone. Call at Messrs. .Dawson's, Sankey-Street;' Mr. T. Tay- lor's, Priestley-street;' or Mr. Gould's, Bridge , Foot. They will give you good value for your money; and get some of those cheery records, you will enjoy them immensely. You can't have a Christmas without' music. Impossible! It can't be done! So tune yourself up to a glorious key, and with one big, beautiful, melodious chord sing—" A Merry Christmas to all !" E. W.</p>
        <p>P.S.—Like a woman, I leave a matter of first- rate importance for the P.S. The grocers, butchers, poulterers and the like, I shall deal with next week,when the windows will be groan- ing, as. the descriptive writers say, under the weight of the eatables that will make our Christmas happy, In a tentative kind of way I would ask you to consult' the stocks of Messrs. Milling and Co.; Messrs. W. Owen and Son, Messrs. Lipton, Ltd., Bridge-street, Messrs. Monks, and Son, Mr. Peter Leigh. Mr. .Povey,‘Mr..J. 8.-Isaac; Mr. 'Arthur Clowes, Mr. Gillighan, Messrs. T. and J. Riley, and Mr. W. Cooper (Buttermarket-street). Messrs. Ray and Miles, London-road, Liver- pool; the Pioneer, Bold-street, Liverpool; Davies' 30 Lowhill, Liverpool; the Globe Furnishing Co., ' Pembroke-place, Liverpool; I3unney's, Church-street, Liverpool; Mr. Rich- ard Jeffreys, Bold-street, Liverpool, are making special displays.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-13_WidnesExaminer_art0101" n="art0101">
        <head>WHAT TO BUY AND WHERE.</head>
        <p>WHAT TO BUY AND WHERE.</p>
        <p>The' ideal -present should-be both attractive and useful. We should appreciate it for the kindly thought, and . also for its. own particu- lar purpose. Our leading shops make all things possible. Here we can see heaps of beautiful Irish hand-embroidered table linen. What would delight the house-proud woman more' than a dainty addition to her damask table linen stock? There are wonderfully cheap and cosy eiderdowns. What a comforting gift an eiderdown! The thought makes us feel warm. Indian hand-worked handkerchiefs, enticing furs and coats, evening dresses, flowers of every description, and toys in abundance. Dainty lace, and a lace handkerchief or scarf will please a lady at any age, in any clime. Elsewhere we can secure beautiful and irresistible things in the way of furs, pretty blouses, lamp shades, cushions, lace collars, pretty bows, winter hats, and for the„child,ren heaps of toys. Structators for the boys who wish to devdlop their engineer- ing skill. Mistresses can set up, their maids in toys, n( w i wonder wherei ent caps and aprons. What more useful gift could they have? Everywhere in another establish- e we g beautifulo ei to s 7 all y.es., w n H heaps of Yuletide gifts. even, to suit the most lastidiOns. And if parents* wish to give their' girlies a cosy garment for the' winter, here is a stock to choose from. Cosy winter coats in velvet and warm cloth. Bonnets for babies and comfortable socks—oh! dear! the weeniest baby is not for- gotten. Hats, blouses in every style and colour, boots and shoes for every kind of weather. In the millinery establishments and depart- ments there are the latest creations in hats that will suit any. taste or idea; there' are feathers with thedaintiest and most Sinuous curl, placed to suit any face. - And wouldn't you be rewarded 'with a bewitching smile from the wearer? You can buy flowers, neckbands, ruffles, -and all</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-13_WidnesExaminer_art0102" n="art0102">
        <head>MEN'S NEEDS, JEWELLERY AND BRIDESCAKES.</head>
        <p>MEN'S NEEDS, JEWELLERY AND BRIDES- CAKES.</p>
        <p>Perhaps you would like to remind me that I'm forgetting the men. What an accusation! How could I do such a thing when I see shops like Messrs. C. Mounfield, J. Banks, J. E. White, gentlemen's outfitter; Messrs. Boydell Bros., Messrs. Smith and Walker, Horsemarket-street, Mr. George Sutton, Messrs. Thomas and Son, Grand Clothing Hall, Suits Ltd., Messrs. Mar- shall and Wright, and the Manchester Clothing Depot, tailors?</p>
        <p>It's very unlikely I should forget \them when I see the warm overcoats, hats, caps, collars, ties of every hue and pattern, cosy dressing gowns, gloves, comforters, and ,mufflers. And the suits! What choice! What beautiful cloth! What homely serges and cheviots! And the boys are not forgotten either. Oh! the scores of little coats and caps! Any size, any shape, sailors, soldiers, Highlanders, and any fancy dress suit for the Juvenile Ball. There is plenty to choose fromi And what could be nicer than a cosy dressing 'gown for father? We can picture him sitting by the fire, after his .Christmas dinner, smoking a pipe of peace, wearing his new _dressing gown and a pair of warm slippers. Oh, yes! you can get them at Messrs. Roberts and Son, the Public Benefit, or Messrs. Jacksons'.</p>
        <p>These shops will give you an ample stock from which to choose. And, then the evening dress shade and every colour, silk', satin or suede! are so beautiful, in every shoes! Oh ! the High-class goods everywhere, and splendid value. If father or husband prefer a special kind of footwear he can be supplied witir the best. Slippers, cosy and dainty, peep at us from every side in these shops; shoes from any price to suit any size of foot. Footballers can be satisfied, too: so that in more ways than one it will be "Well played, Warrington !" Good leather did the trick. Nothing like leather! Put how time flies!</p>
        <p>I must tell you that Boots, Cash Chemists, And Mr. A. Davidson have plenty of new calen- dars. You can take your choice of heaps of ;hings, pretty Christmas cards, picture postcards, perfumed stationery, diaries, fountain pens. booklets of every description, dresing bags, leather cases, and so on, ad lib. But you'll have to, get a good stock of paper in order to let your friends know about your glorious shopping excursions, and your wonderful bar- gains, and perhaps to enclose one of those photo- graphs you lad from the photographeft, I mention. Mr. F. J. Dowling has a large selec- tion of Christmas cards.</p>
        <p>Smokers will find all they need at the shops of Messrs. Sudlow and Co.</p>
        <p>Eye specialiatathave many opportunities .for supplying acceptable Christmas gifts, and Mr. Johnston Watson, Mr. P. Neubert and Mr. R. Jackson Moss will be found capable of meeting all requirements.</p>
        <p>Then we have silversmiths, who are quite ready to show you any amount of the loveliest of lovely articles. There are Mr. F. Hallett in Bridge-street, Messrs. Eustance and ' Co. and Messrs. Miller in Sankey-street, and MT. Fin- layson in Horsemarket-street. What glittering Christmas gifti! Silver teapots; watch brace- lets, expanding gold watch braeelets, too, if you prefer that style, the latest fichu .brooches with diamonds, pearls or rubies. Manicure tools,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-13_WidnesExaminer_art0103" n="art0103">
        <head>CHRISTMAS PARCELS.</head>
        <p>CHRISTMAS PARCELS.</p>
        <p>The London and North-Western Railway and</p>
        <p>Quick Transit.</p>
        <p>The London and North-Western Railway Com- pany have made complete arrangements for the collection, quick transit, and prompt delivery of Christmas parcels in all the principal towns on their system, and all parts of Wales, Scot- land and Ireland. Special " parcel " trains will be run, and through vans will also be attached to the principal passenger trains for the accom- modation of the traffic, and additional delivery and collection services by parcel vans and carts between the receiving offices and the railway stations will be in operation during Christmas and the preceding week. Senders are advised thitt as, an _extra precaution a duplicate address label should be enclosed in each parcel, so that the name and address of the consignee may be ascertained in the event of the outside label becoming detached or defaced.</p>
        <p>CHRISTMAS TRAINS. Provision Made for our Comfort by the L. and N.-W.</p>
        <p>An advertisement on this, page gives de- tails of the arrangements made for travelling at Christmastide to and from the parts of , the kingdom; covered by the L. and N.-W. system, which means practically the whole of the kingdom.</p>
      </div>
