      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-13_WidnesExaminer_art0116" n="art0116">
        <p>THE EMA.MINER, SATURDAY, DECEMBER 13, 1913.</p>
        <p>BIRTHS. MARRIAGES. DEATHS.</p>
        <p>attend to their own affairs that has made the Empire what it is to-day, and the withholding of which from Ireland has kept that sister nation poor, disconsolate, and the victim of office seekers, and the worst class of faction mongers.' The justifications to rebel given by , Mr. JOYNSON-HICKS might be given in favour of a rebellion by the Nationalists, but'they are in a tremendous majority, and they ask, in a Constitutional way—that is, by their votes— that they shall be treated justly and fairly, and so given a chance to show what a really united Empire may mean. It would be too tedious to deal in this man- ner with all the false issues raised by Mr. JoYNsoN-Hicks. But he made a point of the benefits Ireland would get at the extiense of the English taxpayer. We may take it for granted that Ireland will pay her share for any services rendered. She usually has done so. We suppose .Mr. JOYNSON-HICKS is not so un- informed as not to know that a Royal Com- mission report, issued as recently as 1896, found that the Act of Union imposed upon Ireland burdens greater than she could bear, and that LORD MACDONNELL estimated the financial injustice to Ireland for the century to ,be £300,000,000. If Mr. JOYNSON-HICKS did not know this, we make him a present of the fact, and ask him to blush at the mean suggestion he served out for the delectation of his Saturday night Audience. If LORD MACDONNELL, on financial adjustment, is not found satisfying, may we recall to his'memory fhat Mr. BALFOUR, speaking in the comparatively calm times of 1898, for the Unionist party, said: "There was a time, n unhappy time, when the British aPar- liament thought. it well employed in crushing out Irish manufacture in the interest of the British producer. It was a cruel, and it has proved a stupid policy." Finally, may we re- mind Mr. JOYNSON-HICKS that ,his own party offered to give Home Rule, and that Tory news- papers were set to work to extol ilk virtues in exchange for the saving of the House of Lords. But the Lords were beyond redemption, and</p>
        <p>Home Rule has had to conic through the ordinary channel, the party fight. Constitu- tionally, that fight is sound, three-fourths of Ireland demanding it, and a majority of about 100 in the House of Commons being anxious to accord it.,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-13_WidnesExaminer_art0117" n="art0117">
        <head>TARIFF REFORM RECKONING APPLIED TO HOUSING.</head>
        <p>TARIFF REFORM RECKONING APPLIED TO HOUSING.</p>
        <p>Tory speakers are stating that the shortage of houses in the rural districts has ,been caused by the action of the present Government. They state that when the Tories werp in power. over 100,000 cottages were built Per annum, while, when the Liberals Were in poWer, only 10,000 were provided. Many points have to be borne in mind,i such as the fact that the more insani- tary dwellings are Cleared away , the smaller win appear • the ITICIVO.,SC:on the same number of new houses erected.</p>
        <p>Dealing with the figures themselves, we find that in the following years the increase in the number of houses under a £2O rateable value .11 each year was as follows :</p>
        <p>Financial Dwelling-</p>
        <p>Year!i</p>
        <p>Year. 1900-01 19011.02 1902-03 1903-04 1904-05 1905-06 1906-07 1907-08 1908-09 1909-10 1910-11 1911-12</p>
        <p>Houses. ... 5,612,000 ... ... 5,703,000 ... ... 5,791,000 ... ... 5,833,000 ... ... 5,935,000 ... 6,048,000 ... ... 6,128,000 ... ... 6,208,000 ... ... 6,280,000 ... ... 6,368,000 ... ... 6,379,000 ... ... 6,459,000 ...</p>
        <p>Increaae.</p>
        <p>91,000 88,000 41,000 102,000 113,000 80,000 80,000 72,000 88,000 11,000 80,000</p>
        <p>To take 10,000 as being the average Liberal year, and 100,600 the average Tory year, is a piece of gross juggling with figures. It is the old Tariff Reform dodge of selecting the best figure for your own purpose, and the worst figure for your opponent's. Thus it will be seen that in 1903 the increase was only 41,000, although it was a Tory year, while in 1912 the increase was 80,000, though it was a Liberal year. The year in which the figure went down to 10,000 was that immediately succeeding the rejection of .the Budget by the Lords, and the consequent financial chaos which resulted.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-13_WidnesExaminer_art0118" n="art0118">
        <head>PASSIYG WEEK.</head>
        <p>PASSIYG WEEK.</p>
        <p>MR. CROSFIELD SUCCEEDS LORD AVEBURY Our congratulations to Mr. A. H. Crosfield, who has been specially sought out by the National Association of Grocers' Assistants to fill the President's chair, rendered vacant by the death of Lord Avebury. Mr. Crosfield's experi- ence in business matters and his keen interest in all movements for the betterment of the con- ditions of the workers, combine to make his appointment a very fitting one. Certainly to be selected to follow Lord Avebury is no small com- pliment, and Mr. Crosfield will see in it the measure of the confidence the Association have in him.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-13_WidnesExaminer_art0119" n="art0119">
        <head>COKE.</head>
        <p>COKE.</p>
        <p>At the meeting of the Council last week, Councillor Osborne .called attention to a reso- lution of the Gas Committee "that the Deputy- Chairman and Alderman Tinnion be appointed to act with the General Manager in disposing of the surplus stock of coke." It appears that there has been a difficulty in finding customers, and Councillor Osborne suggested that greater facilities should be afforded the poorer people to get it. At the present time, a person cannot purchase less than 5 cwt., and he has to arrange for his own carting. Coun- cillor Osborne's contention is that as all Corporation Departments e7iist for the con- venience of all classes of ratepayers, and as it is possible to get a pennyworth of gas, it should be equally easy to get a pennyworth of coke, if required. Further than that, he contends that depots should be provided in different parts of the town, so that the difficulty of get- ting the coke home might be obviated. It appears to be a reasonable request, and one that, especially at this time of the year, would be a great boon to those people who cannot take, for various reasons, a large supply. There should then be no difficulty in disposing of the stock.</p>
        <p>NEW CURATES.</p>
        <p>"An Old Journalist" writes As already announced, a new curate is ex- pected at St. Paul's in .the New Year, and an- other for the Parish Church is to be ordained deacon on the 21st inst. One who has gone through it all says that few experiences can be more trying., Suffer it short extract from his detailed account: ." At the most solemn dai of his life, the day of his ordination, the young man finds himself one of a 'considerable body upon whom 'the laying on of hands' takes place. The stately service in the cathedral is of considerable length, and the strain upon the Physical endurance of the candidate is very great. When all is over, he is hurried away to the parish to which he has been appointed, and, almost before he knows where he is, he finds himself in .a state of breathless astonishment in the presence of a crowd of people whom he has never seen before, and whom by the book he is addressing as his dearly beloved brethren.' The 'dearly beloved are all there, and alas! nearly all of them are in their most active critical mood." And ,the writer goes on .to- " pitch into 'em" and "show 'em up," even as they "let the new curate have it." No doubt there is a good deal of truth in Ills rather strong stA.cnients-•-villieri I do not now- reproduce.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-13_WidnesExaminer_art0120" n="art0120">
        <head>Atfi ,</head>
        <p>Atfi ,</p>
        <p>"FIRE, AND BE DAMNED."</p>
        <p>Mr. JOYNSON-HICKS, M.P., visited Warrington last Saturday, and, speaking in the name of Sir EDWARD CARSON, the uncrowned king of a small corner of Ulster, demonstrated, in twelve or fourteen lines, much better than we could clo in a column, the desperate plight into which alleged "Unionists" find themselves. Personify- ing CARSON and apostrophising the PRIME MINISTER, this shining light of the Tory party said:</p>
        <p>"Let the Army do its duty. lam still going to do mine. I face the Prime Minister to tell him, 'Bring up your Army, bring up your batteries, bring up your Navy. Behind me thee are 90,000 men.in Ulster. Behind them there are half a million of women and children. Behind them is the whole Unionist party in the House of Commons. Above them is the Lord God of battles. In His name and under His protection I say to you, the Prime Minister, with your arms, with your batteries, 'fire—fire if you dare, fire and be damned."</p>
        <p>The sight of Orangemen doing all these things in the name and under the protection of the Almighty savours too much of blasphemy to oall for serious treatment. But we may be excused if we pause to say a few words upon the more mundane, ot the more Ulster, portion of a characteristic outburst by a representative of the gentlemanly party. To .our mind it appears, and we have no doubt that all thought- ful men and women will share our opinion, that the last ditch has, in reality been reached when, for lack of argument, and in sheer annoyance at finding himself in a hopeless plight, a man gives way ..to cursing his opponent. The meeting in question began with song, "the red, white and blue"—which "Unionists" endeavour to appropriate ,for poli- tical purpos/es—being pressed once more into the service of a party who are ever ready to raise the cry of danger to the Empire, and to wrap the Union Jack around a bureaucracy now fighting, fortunately, its last fight for its life. We shall have a nobler use for the Union Jack when the people of Ireland have been indeejd united, when the men who dare to separate a people into sections, and hypo- critically dub themselves "Unionists," . have been driven beyond even the cursing stage, and finally routed. Between the singing and the swearing points on Saturday night the case against Home Bute was not presented as an argument on points; it was presented as a case of irrelevancies, unsubstantiated statements following up6n false premises, and it broke down in the pitiful exhibition which Mr. JoyNsox-HicKs would call his peroration.</p>
        <p>One thing emerged clearly from a riot of strong and abusive language. Many English- men have been careful about saying, in so many words, that they are in favour of civil war. It was once a dangerous attitude to assume, and may be again. But Mr. JOYNSON- McKs has no misgivings. He recited the con- cocted grievances of Ulster, with which we will Shortly deal, and then said: "I tell you that those causes not merely justify; they demand rebellion on the part of the people of Ulster." Fortunately this champion of Constitutionalism, who favours a disappointed minority appearing in arms against the KING'S peace, gave us his reasons, which we can submit to examination. lie said that rebellions might be justified on one of four grounds—treachery, cruelty, op- pression, and violation of the Constitution— and all these were present here. For the</p>
        <p>Purposes of our argument we assume that the King, his Ministers, and the State, are, as in fact they must be in a Constitutional country, one, and it is against the State that the rebel- lion must take place. When was treachery practised by the State towards the Orangemen of •Ulster? Since 1885 the Liberal party have promised Ireland this measure of justice, and the fact that it is to be given at the first oppor- tunity that the pledge can be redeemed cannot constitute treachery towards the minority, who have had quarter of a century's notice of what to expect, and have, as a matter of fact, been talking about fighting and being in the right for the whole of that long period. Who has been cruel to the Orangemen of Ulster? Who has ever oppressed Orangemen? These are questions that are answered in the asking. But we suppose that Mr. JorNsoN-Hicks's clinching "argument" was that the Home Rule Bill is a violation of the Constitution. It is here the gentleman is on the thinest of his very thin ice. The rock bottom nof Con- stitutional government is that the majority must—in the absence of cruelty, oppression and treachery—have its way, provided for in Con- stitutional method. 'W'hat have we here? For centuries the majority of the people of Ireland —not minority, be it remembered—nave been fighting against the cruelty, the oppression, too bare-faced to be treacherous, studiously prac- tised upon them both by Orangemen and British politicians. PROFESSOR DICEY, a</p>
        <p>Unionist, has summarised the Act by which, a century ago, it was sought finally to curb the people of Ireland to other people's selfish ends. "The Act of Union," he says, "was an agreement which, could it have been referred to a Court of Law, must have at once been cancelled as a contract hopelessly tainted with fraud and corruption." Since that fraud was perpetrated upon Ireland in the sacred name of the Union Jack, these Constitutional gentle- men have tried to hold their ill-gotten ground: But since the people had the vote placed at their disposal, and Constitutional methods have increasingly taken the place of violence, the people have advanced, and they now "demand"—to use Mr. JON:NSON-lIICKS'S word in circumstances where right, and not arro- gance, permits of its use—that freedon-, to</p>
      </div>
