      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-13_WidnesExaminer_art0123" n="art0123">
        <head>From Everywhere.</head>
        <p>From Everywhere.</p>
        <p>It is wise to be sure, but otherwise to be too sure.--S'ophie Loeb.</p>
        <p>Job's-. Comforter. Another embryo genius reveals himself in a recent examination paper. "A Job's com- forter," he howls, "is a thing to give a baby the patience of Job." International Exchange. . .</p>
        <p>"Sir John Simon, proposing the health of Dr. Page, the United States Ambassador, said the two countries sent to each other their best and their worst. The Americans sent us rag- time and we sent them our militant suffragists."</p>
        <p>Times Have Changed. Farmer. hum "How do you 'like your auto- mobile, Ezra?" Farmer Hoot: "Well, I can't go to sleep on mY way home. from town, and wake up in the barnyard, as I could with old Dobbin."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-13_WidnesExaminer_art0124" n="art0124">
        <head>Letters We Have Not Yet Received.</head>
        <p>Letters We Have Not Yet Received.</p>
        <p>"Dear Sir,—Since you are one of our softest customers we are sending you a little Christmas gift in the hope that it will place us on such a friendly footing that we shall have many oppor- tunities of taking advantage of you in the com- ing year.—Yours faithfully, ROOKEM AND SONS." —"The Tatler."</p>
        <p>Strategy. A farmer went to the home of a 'country doctor in the village late one night, and be- sought him to come at once to a distant farm- house.</p>
        <p>The medicine man hitched up his horse, and they drove to the farmer's home. Upon their arrival the farmer said:</p>
        <p>"How much is your fee, doctor?" "Half a guinea," said the physician in sur- prise.</p>
        <p>"Thank you," said the farmer, handing over the money; "the liveryman wanted 15 shillings to drive inc home."</p>
        <p>Could Mend It. A surly old farmer who had succeeded in quarrelling with everybody in the parish except the mild-mannered vicar, at length so tested that gentleman's temper as to force him to call the farmer a broken-down old mule. The farmer Sought the services of the village lawyer, who himself had suffered under the tongue of his would-be client. The man of law declared his powerlessness in such a case. "But he called , me a broken-down mule," yelled the farmer. . "Weil," said the lawyer, "don't come to me about that. I'm net a veterinary surgeon."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-13_WidnesExaminer_art0125" n="art0125">
        <head>MR. CROSFIELD AND THE GROCERF</head>
        <p>MR. CROSFIELD AND THE GROCERF</p>
        <p>'incomes President of the National Assistants' Association.</p>
        <p>LORD AVEBURY'S SUCCESSOR.</p>
        <p>The office of President of the National Association of Grocers' Assistants, which for the past thirteen years has been honourably occu- pied by Lord Avebury, was unhappily rendered vacant through his Lordship's death. Recently Mr. A.,H. Crosfield was approached by the officers of the Asso6iation and invited to fill the vacant\position, and, after an interview with a deputUtton, he has decided to accept this honourable position.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-13_WidnesExaminer_art0126" n="art0126">
        <head>MAJORITY DOUBLED.</head>
        <p>MAJORITY DOUBLED.</p>
        <p>Wick's Emphatic Reply to Mr. Bonar Law.</p>
        <p>Wick's reply to the Tory claim that the Gov- ernment has lost the confidence of the country is decisive and crushing. The Lord Advocate has not only retained the seat, but ,on a poll of just over 2,700 he has more than doubled his majority. The official figures are:—</p>
        <p>Mr:R. Munro (L) Mr. A. G. Mackenzie (U)</p>
        <p>_ Liberal majority As compared with the December election of 1910, these figures show an increase in the Liberal vote of 62, an increase in the Liberal majority of 232, and a decline in the Tory vote of 170.</p>
        <p>After the declaration of the poll the Lord Ad- vocate addressed a cheering crowd from an hotel window.</p>
        <p>Thanking the electors for their magnificent support, he recalled that Mr. Bonar Law had said: "Consult the people." Well (said Zfr. Munro), he had consulted the people of Wick Burghs, and he hoped Mr. Bonar Law liked the answer they had given him. (Cheers.) The election had been fought upon Home Rule first and Home Rule last, and was an emphatic en- dorsement of the Liberal position.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-13_WidnesExaminer_art0127" n="art0127">
        <head>ECONOMY IN THE HOME.</head>
        <p>ECONOMY IN THE HOME.</p>
        <p>At this time of the year the housewife is con- fronted with the problem of how to make her cakes sufficiently rich and tasty without undue expense. It is just at this time that Bird's Egg Substitute (Powder) becomes doubly valuable. Not only does it give that richness to cakes that is imparted by eggs, but it is much simpler in use, and there is no risk of tainted cakes. Cakes made with Bird's alternative for eggs are ex- ceptionally light, and it makes deliciously light Yorkshire puddings and pancakes. In cost, the comparison is overwhelmingly in favour of Bird's. A number of excellent recipes are given with each packet and tin, and a booklet contain- ing many more can be obtained post free by sending a postcard to Alfred Bird and Sons, Ltd., Birmingham.</p>
        <p>AT LAST !—Continued.</p>
        <p>House of Commons, be law, without being sen again to the Lords.</p>
        <p>"For it never can be thought. by any man who has the blessing of reason, that there ought to be two authorities in a State, one capable of barring whatever the other would do. The powers of legislation exercised for the good of the people is good government; the powers of legislation exercised for the benefit of any set of men is bad government, and is naturally carried to excess; for the good of the set can only be pursued at the expense of the community. If we are told that this expedient of ours would no doubt be effectual to its end, if we could obtain it, but that to such a measure as this the Lords will never give their consent, we answer that, in a case of necessity, what cannot be obtained one way must in another; and the probability is that this being seen by the Lords, they will not hold out to the last.</p>
        <p>"But if they do, the House of Commons have only to proceed a step, further, and declare that</p>
        <p>Bills, as passed by them a certain number of them a certain number of times, and at certain intervals, are law. This resolution the people would hail with transport, and make the enact- ment laws by ' their obedience, and from that moment the House of Lords is blotted out."</p>
        <p>The rest of this article is interesting, as show- ing that the House of Lords.was 78 years ago, as it is to-day, an unpatriotic and selfish mob. HENRY WHITHAM, 659, Broderick-street, San Fr4ncisco.</p>
