      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-13_WidnesExaminer_art0128" n="art0128">
        <head>Long silence.</head>
        <p>Long silence.</p>
        <p>WONDERFUL LLOYD GEORGE.</p>
        <p>To-day we publish an article by Mr. Henry Whitham, of San Francisco.</p>
        <p>Years ago Mr. Whitham left Warrington, where he was engaged as a wire drawer at Mel;Vs. Rylands Brothers, Ltd.</p>
        <p>He is a well-known working man writer on political and social subjects, and we welcoms his return to this kind of activity in the columns of "The Examiner."</p>
        <p>Mr. Whitham's letter is notable for its appeal to an unknown public in his old town, after the lapse of many years.</p>
        <p>There is also a joyful recognition of the feet that Mr. Lloyd George has taken up the crY of generations of old-time reformers, himself included, on behalf of the poor and suffering section of humanity, a cry which was uttered in the wilderness till the great-hearted Welsh" man gathered up the tangled skein, and the cause of the poor became, as by magic, pow tical politics.</p>
        <p>But it is remarkable most of all for its quota- tion from a paper in pre-Victorian times, which not only ridiculed the farce of an Arre. sponsible House of Lords nullifying the work of the Commons„ but pointed out how the Lords' veto could be blotted out, the method suggested being practically that which Ms been so effectually adoptld by the present Liberal Government.</p>
        <p>All things come to these who know how to waits is Mr. Whitham's philosophical reflection.</p>
        <p>One who is, physically, far away from the old country, and yet keeps himself in prettY close touch with its doings, would like to have a few words with-who? I am almost afraid to venture an attempt to answer my own .question. Certainly a nelv generation has sprung up since October, 188'7. "Clifford Dix," previous to the above date, tried, and succeeded in at least one of his Con- tinued efforts, that was to cover' a fair amount of space in "The Examiner" with his stuff. At, that far-away time "Clifford Dix" found, what, the Henry Whitham of to-day finds, a paler' not only fit for the times and the home, but one that was proud to be neutral neither in politics nor yet in religion.</p>
        <p>The Wrongs of the Poor. I received Copies of the London "Times" 011 also of the "Daily News and Leader," as sc -1 as possible, with full reports of the great, t..j wonderful, speeches of your own Lloyd George' I couldn't possibly tell you of the effect those speeches hat' on me. I was that excited I Or; tainly could not go on with my reading. would cry out., "Come here, .ma, and listen to this" :</p>
        <p>The interests of agriculture have been sacri- ficed to other interests, whiCh are injurious to agriculture, and which to that extent are have' ful to the whole community. Now take the wages on the land. The general average °f wages paid on the land for its cultivation are lower than those paid in. any great industry is this country. (" Shame.") The hours of labour are longer than in any great industry. You have no Saturday half-holiday; you hay° many working on Sunday. It is not unskilled labour. (Cheers.) They talk about the work Of the labourer as if it were something anybodY could do who could just throw a spade over hie shoulder. . . . Twelve shillings a week, 13, 14, eked out with all sorts of allowances, has to keep the men from death and starvation. "Now what do you think of that?" ques- tioned my wife. - Why," she answered, "you have said that in 'The Examiner' a score of times." Yes, that is true, and hundreds of other men during the past fifty years have said the same thing. They were scorned and scoffed at, their names were simply by-words to those who plumed themselves on being "True-blue patri- ots "—those who mouthed the words "Suffer little Childreo to come lint° Me," yet never missed the chance to lake, not only the bread from the little one's .mouth, but the roof from Its head. "Grachus," "Nortliumbrian," "Davidson," "Verax," and a thousand more skilled writers have voiced the wrongs of the poor.</p>
        <p>Memories of the Past. I was with Ernest Jones on the night in MO; chester when he caught his death of col." by getting wet through. May I give a hint 14) the "Old Journalist," who writes so entertain.; ingly of the scenes of other days. that he foilree a " jotting " of that test election that took I:4-1 so long ago between Ernest Jones and—was,lci John or Jacob Bright? The meeting was hel in a hall away up Oldham-road, Manchester. I have near me two fairly large voltunes, Their contents are varied. During the past fe,‘;'r weeks my curiosity has .moved me to pore ov% them a little. The contents of these volurhe" are letters, each signed with the name of the present writer or one of his various noms-def: plume. At the beginning of one volume is cutting taken from a Warrington paper, givilire the ." unexpected and unwelcome news" of rejection of the Ship Canal Bill. It is a loal clipping. . It gives the names and amounts the various subscribers to the preliminary eS: penses of the Bill. One item reads, " Warring ton Wiredrawers' Association, .E 10. Ireille,rrl,,; ber I met Alderman Davies in his office in Market-place. We went together to ,the oftleu of the Ship Canal Company in Rlanchester, where I paid in the money. I find also a number of letters urging for; ward the movement to establish a Free Librapo in Warrington. All things come to those velirs know how to wait, which saying the folloWlso of Mrs. Pankhurst might take to heart, 3 the bit.of.history I am now going to give.'</p>
        <p>How the Veto Was Vetoed. fs of Among my books is one, the conten- Ann Ile' which are most interesting, "The Lonuv- view," 1835-6. I am going to select for readers' delectation a tit-bit that deserveS fitting send-off :</p>
        <p>"Great families of yesterday we show, _ld And Lords whose parents were—the IN knows who."</p>
        <p>In the years 1835-6 there was quite a Ini,gl,/ big struggle going on between the Little D"rci landers, who were landless and poor, and had to fight battles of which they knew nothing, tit_ for pay that always seemed to be at the vanls; ing point, and the aristocratic party, who alWaiid looked after the Money end of the business had a great love for hearing themselves sil°" out "Quick march!"</p>
        <p>Says the "London Review," 1835-6: thei 4.T0 set up a class of men, by giv powers which they may use for their own aa vantage, at the expense of the rest of the cllahe- munity, is to set up a body of enemies' to m rest of the commtmity ; for they Will be sure 7; ,act like enemies; which is, to prosecute thcof own advantage to the utmost, regardless the mischief they do to the rest of the eOlll- ing r_ munity." The writer goes on to say W : e think that the power of • the effect the incredible mischiefs involved in thvee.ii, power of frustrating all schemes .0f irnPNi.or ment might be taken away by a change v.-, 4 little perceptible. Let it be enacted, that 11 Bill, which has been passed by the House 4(ot )1. Commons and thrown out by the flouse le Lords, it shall, if passed a third time in fl Lords (Continued in preceding column).</p>
        <p>p 1 ea PETERIL FIRE GRATES never fail to lue— Sole agent. J. S. GARLAND. Ironmonger, Bridge-street, Warrington.</p>
