      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-13_WidnesExaminer_art0133" n="art0133">
        <head>Hand Caught in Machinery.</head>
        <p>Hand Caught in Machinery.</p>
        <p>John Clough (20), 6, Victoria-street, employe of the Castle Rubber Co., was admitted to War- rington Infirmary on Wednesday, with a severe injury to the left hand, which he had caught in machinery. The hand has been amputated, and the youth is progressing satisfactorily:</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-13_WidnesExaminer_art0134" n="art0134">
        <head>Timely Discovery.</head>
        <p>A Timely Discovery.</p>
        <p>There was a small fire yesterday morning at 119, Mersey-street. It appears that Mrs. Massey. who sleeps above the shop, was awakened by the smell of smoke. She came downstairs and found a quantity of goods alight in the shop. An alarm was raised and the flames extinguished by P.C. McDonald. Mr. Matthew J. Knowles, and Mr. Charles Ilankinsoi,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-13_WidnesExaminer_art0135" n="art0135">
        <head>Women Workers.</head>
        <p>Women Workers.</p>
        <p>At the meeting 'of the Wycliffe Church Y.P.S.. held on Thursday night, the Rev. T. T. James in the chair, Mr. James Gauld read a paper on "Do women workers oust men?" and con- tended that they do not, except from "unmanly" occupations. A vote taken after the discussion showed 28 negative and five affirmative.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-13_WidnesExaminer_art0136" n="art0136">
        <head>Missing Teacher.</head>
        <p>The Missing Teacher.</p>
        <p>The mystery of the teacher who disappeared from the Warrington Secondary School has not been solved, and the. Education Committee re- port that in consequence of Mr. W. Donaghy, who lived in Liverpool, having absented him- self without notice, they have decided to fill the position. Mr. Leonard G. Rose, Lincoln. has been appointed, at a salary of &lt;2135 per annum.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-13_WidnesExaminer_art0137" n="art0137">
        <head>"Sixty Years .a Queen."</head>
        <p>"Sixty Years .a Queen."</p>
        <p>At the invitation of the management of the Park Picture Palace, the scholars from the Blue Coat School yesterday afternoon witnessed the famous picture dealing with the life of the late Queen Victoria, and in the evening the guests were from the Orford Barracks. To-day there will be special performances for children at 11 and 1 o'clock, and for adults at 3,5, 7, and 9 o'clock.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-13_WidnesExaminer_art0138" n="art0138">
        <head>Musical Successes.</head>
        <p>Musical Successes.</p>
        <p>The following pupils of Miss M. Jennings, 142, Wilderspool-causeway, passed very successfully -Itt the recent examination held at Manchester in connection with the Victoria College of Music, London, namely: Piano, Miss Hilda Baker, Penketh, (advanced) senior grade, 94 marks out of a possible NO, Ist class with honours; also May Mather, 1, Church-street; .89, marks, Ist class with honours; intermediate ,grade, Ellen Miller, 12, Nora-street, 91 marks, Ist class with honours; junior grade, Alice Furnival, 59, Bewsey-streef, 95 marks, Ist class with honours.</p>
        <p>Boots' Diaries, DM :No .doubt most of our readers have seen and lised one or. more of those Wonderfully- cheap and useful diaries published by Boots Cash Chemists. The three editions for 1914 are now ready and may be obtained at each of their 550 branch establishments.' For those ho do rhneli writing the "Scribbling Diary" Is worth its price for the blotting paper alone. The "Home Diary " is indispensable to housekeepers who .ttre wise enough to keep accounts; whilst its information, legal and otherwise, in relation to .domestic affairs, renders it a most valuable 'companion. The many thousands who have fused the little "Pocket Diary" for engage- Inents, &amp;c.;for years, can speak from experience .of its i'alue. In addition to all this, to each of these diaries is attached an insurance coupon, -by which, for a modest registration fee to the ." Ocean Accident and Guarantee Corporation, you are insured for .21,000.</p>
        <p>Warrington Field Club. A meeting was held in the Museum on Satur- day. There was a fair attendance of members, presided over by Mr. A. J. Jolley. There were also several visitors. Mr. Miln, of Garton's, Ltd., gave a very interesting lecture, illustrated by lantern slides, on "British Cereals." lie traced the history of the agriculture of the cereals, and exhibited excellent slides of the original members of the grass family, from . which man had produced our crops of to-clay., , The latter portion of his paper was confined to those members which were only suitable for these northern climes, via., wheat, oats, barley and rye. lie dealt very lucidly with the method of fertilisation and other botanical features of these grasses, and remarked it was accepted nowadays that they were all self-fertilised. Numerous slides were also shown of the types and the improvements made by the scientific plant breeder. A discussion was taken part in by several members and friends.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-13_WidnesExaminer_art0139" n="art0139">
        <head>Mayor as Ancient Shepherd.</head>
        <p>Mayor as Ancient Shepherd.</p>
        <p>The quarterly meeting of the IJoyal Order of Ancient Shepherds, late "Spring Rose'' Lodge, No. 828, was held at the house of Bro. James °leave, Barley Mow Hotel, on Saturday. The Secretary, P.M. W. E. Clegg, gave a very creditable report regarding the past year's work- ing, showing a gain of over £7O, or making the total worth of the Lodge £2,668. After the con- zlnsion of the election of Lodge and ,District Officers, a very pleasing event took place, in the initiation of his Worship the Mayor, Coun- cillor Peter Peacock, and Councillor James Halliwell as honorary members. The Mayor , expressed himself highly pleased at becoming a ' member of the Shepherds' Order, and promised to take an active part in its welfare in the future. Songs were rendered by Bros. Radcliffe, Hussey and Massey. Bro. Brobbin gave a selec- •tion on the concertina. P.M. James Parr pro- posed, and P.P.C.S. F. S. Massey seconded, that • the best thanks of the meeting be given to Bro. Peacock for his presence and advice, which :brought the meeting to a close. ,</p>
        <p>, Mayor and Municipal Employes. The Warrington and District Local Govern- ment Officers' Association held Os annual social evening and whist drive at the Town Hill on Friday evening. There were some 140 members .and friends present. His Worship the Mayor .tCouncillor Peacock) and Mayoress were pre- sent for the opening of the proceedings, but could not stay throughout the evening, owing to having accepted a previous invitation. Mr. J. Moore Murray, on behalf of the association, expressed the members' gratification and plea- sure at the kindness the Mayor and Mayoress had shown in joining them, even if it was only for a short while, and he wished them both a very prosperous and extended period of office. In replying, his Worship expressed the pleasure it gave the Mayoress and himself in being pre- sent, and he only regretted that, owing to a previous engagement, he could not stay very long. Ile thought that in Dr. Joseph (the Deputy Mayor) would be found an excellent , -'substitute for 'himself. The pleasure of the evening was greatly added to by the contribu- tion of violin solos by Miss A. Moar (leader of the first violins of Handley's Tutorial Orchestra, Bootle) and humorous items by Messrs. H. M. Ashton and J. H. Knowles. A very enjoyable evening ended by the distribution= of the whist •Prizes to the winning players by Dr. Joseph.</p>
        <p>3. TlLEs.—Large 6kR.LAND. Iron- .-t, Warrington.</p>
        <p>KTTCHt'i RINGES, 17irol Gtr stock. t 323 '%lOliger, :51 55, L</p>
        <p>Victoria the Good.</p>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, DECEMBER 13, 1913. LOCAL INTELLIGENCE.</p>
        <p>Messrs. Stanley Paul and Co., 31, Essex-street, Strand, W.C., are issuing a set of cinema books, the second part of which deals with the film "Sixty Years a Queen," which is on view at the Park Picture Palace, this week. The illustra- tions, 54 in number, are exceedingly well pro- duced, and the reading matter makes an inter- esting record of an illustrious reign. The books are published at is.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-13_WidnesExaminer_art0140" n="art0140">
        <p>.6010 vers als°</p>
        <p>' ad- -001- the 010</p>
        <p>heir of ow I</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-13_WidnesExaminer_art0141" n="art0141">
        <head>Life and Death.</head>
        <p>Life and Death.</p>
        <p>Twenty female and seven male children were born in Warrington last week, giving a birth- rate of 19.6. Seven males and 17 females died, giving a rate of '16.8. Four deaths were due to cancer.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-13_WidnesExaminer_art0142" n="art0142">
        <head>No Christmas Trams.</head>
        <p>No Christmas Trams.</p>
        <p>In response to the request of the tram em- ployee and the decision of the Council last week, the Tramways Committee have decided that no trams run on Christmas Day.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-13_WidnesExaminer_art0143" n="art0143">
        <head>Doctor Leaves £29,757.</head>
        <p>Doctor Leaves £29,757.</p>
        <p>The will of Dr. Edward Austin Fox, Holly Bank, Mount-road, New Brighton, and formerly of Warrington, consulting surgeon and a life governor of the Warrington Infirmary, has bee'n proved at £29,757.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-13_WidnesExaminer_art0144" n="art0144">
        <head>Explosions in Mines.</head>
        <p>Explosions in Mines.</p>
        <p>Under the auspices of the Warrington Society a very profitable lecture was given on Thursday night in the Old Academy by Professor Harold Dixon, of Manchester, on "Explosions in Mines." He illustrated many of his points by experiments.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-13_WidnesExaminer_art0145" n="art0145">
        <head>People's College Concert.</head>
        <p>The People's College Concert.</p>
        <p>The entertainment of the week at Parr Hall will be that to be given by the talented pupils of the People's College on Thursday evening. The programme is an excellent one and there should be a bumper house to hear the merry youngsters in songs, choruses and other features.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-13_WidnesExaminer_art0146" n="art0146">
        <head>Lion-hearted LIN IngSlOllC.</head>
        <p>The Lion-hearted LIN IngSlOllC.</p>
        <p>Mrs. James Dodds presided at a lecture given on Thursday evening in the Legh-street Primi- tive Methodist Church by the Rev. John Brad- bury, of Blackpool, on "Livingstone, the Lion- hearted." The attendance was good and many interesting episodes in the great explorer's career were given.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-13_WidnesExaminer_art0147" n="art0147">
        <head>Tramway men and Sunday.</head>
        <p>Tramway men and Sunday.</p>
        <p>The Tramways Committee this week decided that on and after the first Sunday in the New Year the tram service for Sundays should start at 12.30 p.m., instead of soon after 9 a.m., as at present. This will enable the drivers and conductors to have a day off duty every alternate Sunday.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-13_WidnesExaminer_art0148" n="art0148">
        <head>Territorials' Dance.</head>
        <p>Territorials' Dance.</p>
        <p>. The annual inspection dance in connection with the Territorials as held last night ,in the Drill Hall. There was a large attendance, and excellent music was supplied by the Territorial Band, under Bandmaster C. Wainwright. The 31.C.'s were Colour-Sergeant C. Walker, and Sergeant B. Appleton.</p>
        <p>Christmas Decorations Doomed. A. small fire occurred yesterday afternoon at the sweet shop of Mrs. Elizabeth Wright, in Buttermarket-street. The window was showing the Christmas decorations, and was ignited whilst the gasses were being lighted. The Fire Brigade were called, but advised that.their ser- vices were not required before th'ey had a chance to leave the station.</p>
        <p>For Deserted Mothers. The Warrington branch of the National Union of Women Suffrage Societies have arranged to make a collection at Market Gate, next Satur- day, December 20th, in aid of deserted mothers and widows of the town. Mr. Rowland, the secretary of the Guild of Hello, will superintend the distribution of the fund. Donations may be sent to Mrs. R. Pemberton, 9, Ellesmere-road, Stockton Heath.</p>
        <p>Doctor Sacrifices Ills Life. Dr. Frank Woods died at Ramsgate on Wed- nesday morning -from blood poisoning contracted after an ,operation of a patient. He was the youngest Son of the late Mr. William Woods, of Warrington, and brother of Mr. Henry Woods, R.A. He was educated at Warrington. Gram- mar School, and then proceeded to Victoria University, Manchester, and St. Bartholomew's liospital,*. London.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-13_WidnesExaminer_art0149" n="art0149">
        <head>Buckley-street P.S.A.</head>
        <p>Buckley-street P.S.A.</p>
        <p>The monthly meeting was held on Sunday. Mr. W. Shaw presided. The speaker was tht) Rev. H. Ogden, who took for his subject "The Prodigal Son." The soloist, Mr. J. Turton, was heard to advantage in the singing of "The Watchman." Another pleasing item was a quartet, " Remember Me," sung by the Misses L. Makin and A. Fairhurst, and Messrs. E. Tay- lor and G. H. Cox. The lesson was read by Miss A. Harrison.</p>
        <p>Cycling Club Ball. There was a scene IA gaiety and festivity in the 'Parr Hall last night, when the 21st annual dance of the Warrington Cycling Club was held. The hall had been artistically decorated by Messrs. Johnson's. About 70 couples danced to the delightful strains of Mr. Boothroyd's Band. Mr. Fieldhouse was the M.C. and Mr. Berry, the secretary of the club, along with members of the committee, acted as stewards. The refreshments were supplied 14 Atkinson's Cafe, and Messrs. Rowson. Dancing was continued until an early hour this morning.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-13_WidnesExaminer_art0150" n="art0150">
        <head>Overlapping Tram Stages.</head>
        <p>Overlapping Tram Stages.</p>
        <p>The Tramways Committee have decided to introduce, early in the New Year, intermediate or overlapping stations. One will enable pas- sengers to travel from Fletcher-street to Victoria-square, or Fletcher-street to Milner- street, passengers changing at Rylands-street, for id. each way. The penny fare on the Latch- ford /route will be from St. Mary's-street to Milner-street, or Fletcher-street; and on the Cemetery route from the Parochial Schools, Church-street, to the Jockey Bridge, Longford- street.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-13_WidnesExaminer_art0151" n="art0151">
        <head>Warrington Dock.</head>
        <p>The Warrington Dock.</p>
        <p>The Warrington Chamber of Commerce has I passed the following resolution: "That this Chamber hears with much regret that the Man- chester Ship Canal Company are proposing to fill in the old bed of the river at Wilderspool ; and that, as a suitable dock may eventually become essential to the commercial and indus- trial progress of the borough, and as this is the most suitable site available, the Chamber trusts that the Town Council will make every effort to retain the site for the purpose originally in- tended?'</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-13_WidnesExaminer_art0152" n="art0152">
        <head>Four Burning Accidents.</head>
        <p>Four Burning Accidents.</p>
        <p>Curious enough, there have been four patients admitted into the Infirmary during the week, suffering from burns, but all, with the exception of one, are progressing favourably. Francis Percival (42), 94, Longford-street, was burned whilst employed by Messrs. J. Crosfield -and Sons. He was admitted on Thursday. Eliza- beth Burke (16), domestic servant, of Hall Cafe, Stockton Heath, was admitted on Monday; Mrs. Annie Lloyd (36), of 33, Hamilton-street, was admitted on Sunday, and Mary Jewitt (4), 11, Williamson's-square, whose condition is critical, was admitted yesterday, suffering from burns.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-13_WidnesExaminer_art0153" n="art0153">
        <head>"The' Making of a Foundryman."</head>
        <p>"The' Making of a Foundryman."</p>
        <p>At the monthly meeting of the British Foundrymen's Association, held in the Manches- ter School of Technology on Saturday, a paper was read by Mr. W. H. Sherburn, of Warring- ton, on the above subject. The training of apprentices, vital as it was to the future of good craftsmanship, was never in a more un- satisfactory position, said the lecturer, than at the present time. Great changes should be made in elementary educational methods, in order that boys might leave school better fitted to enter industrial life. • Will There Be a Settlement?</p>
        <p>There were signs that the platelayers on the Cheshire Lines may yet come to a solution of the difficulty before Sunday, when ,they threat- ened a strike. At a meeting of the Joint Vigilance Com- mittee, held at Warrington last night, a further conciliatory spirit was shown, and a deputation was appointed to interview the company offi- cials. After a prolonged discussion, in which it was stated the men were determined, the follow- ing resolution was passed: For the purpose Of facilitating negotiations, the deputation is em- powered to suspend their threat of a strike, and the refusal to do special duties, provided that any Proposed settlement be submitted to a mass me(Aittg ont'.4uty -11:-.;11t.".</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-13_WidnesExaminer_art0154" n="art0154">
        <head>Spiritualist Entertainment.</head>
        <p>Spiritualist Entertainment.</p>
        <p>A concert, followed by a social, was held in the Druids' Hall on Tuesday evening, in con- nection with the local Spiritualist Society, and was well attended. Councillor Skeliand pre- sided, and stated that while he still carried on his work at the Ragged School, he felt perfectly justified in helping what he believed to be the good cause of Spiritualism. Songs were well sung by the Misses E. Tanner, M. Jones, and A. itoughton, Mr. Griffiths (humorous), and other items were a duet by Mrs. Speed and Mr. W. Gilbert, a humorous recitation by Mr. Gil- bert, and a capital sketch, entitled "Lord Tomkins," by members of the Buckley-street Sketch Party. Mrs. Speed and Mr. T. Tanner ably presided at the piano. The chairman and artistes were thanked, on the motion of the President, Mr. Stout, seconded by Mr. Foster; senior.</p>
        <p>A Burst Driving Wheel. An inquest was held at NN, arrington on Thurs- day morning by Mr. S. Brighouse on Frederick Stockton, a single man, 23 years of age, who lived with his parents at 1, Hale-street, and who was killed by the bursting of a driving wheel on Monday morning at the Bewsey Forge of Messrs. Pearson and Knowles.—Mr. Thomas Llewellyn, foreman in charge of the engine, said that he passed through the " eight-inch " mill, where the accident occurred, a short while pre- vious to the explosion, and then the engine was running in a proper manner and was satisfac- tory.—James Holt, superintendent, stated that in May last the wheel was inspected, when it was discovered three arms were cracked. They had no wheels in stock at the time, and they repaired the wheel by strengthening it by the addition of plates, and that made it satisfactory. —The Coroner thought that the repairing seemed to have been done efficiently, and the jury returned a verdict of "Accidental death." An Unhappy Debut. Warrington's new motor fire engine made its debut to a local fire late on Sunday night, but it failed en route. The alarm was received at 11.15 that a fire had broken out at the St. Helens' Cable Works, and a full complement of men ' turned out on tlie motor engine. However, a stoppage was necessitated at the crossings near Messrs. Crosfield's works, and when they at- tempted, to proceed again it was discovered that something had gone wrong, for the engine re- fused to move. The assistance of an onlooker was obtained, and he righted the defect, only for the motor to again fail after travelling a few yards. No time was to be lost, and the members of the brigade rushed to the fire, a short distance away, and took appliances with them. In the meantime a few of the employes had been playing on the premises from the com- pany's hose, and, together with the aid of the Warrington Brigade men, all danger of the fire spreading to an adjoining building was avert?d. The horse engine " Major " was brought to the spot, but its services were not needed. The damage is estimated at ..Q2OO.</p>
        <p>• :go 4. •• • 4•:* 44 63 4* • • •4.4.4.4.4. 4. 4. .: • • • • • ALMANAC for 19H •</p>
        <p>"THE EXAMINER"</p>
        <p>4t*. will be given with our issue of 4:*</p>
        <p>SATURDAY NEXT,</p>
        <p>DECEMBER 20th.</p>
