        <p>4 "THE EXAMINER," 84 Cols., • 4•</p>
        <p>and ALMANAC.</p>
        <p>ONE PENNY.</p>
        <p>Preachers for To-morrow. Bold-street Wesleyan—Morning, Mr. J. Upson; evening, Rev. E. Morgan. Bewsey-road Wesleyan—Morning, Mr. H. Blane; evening, Rev. W. A. Beckett. Cairo-street Chapel—Morning and evening, Dr. Mellor. Emmanuel Reformed Church Evening, Special Advent sermon, Rev. W. T. Jaye. Wycliffe Congregational—Morning and, even- ing, Rev. T. T. James. Legh-street Primitive Niethodist—Morning, Rev. A. J. Chessum; afternoon and evening, "The Messiah." . Golborne-street Baptist—Morning and even- ing, Rev. H. Ogden. St. John's Presbyterian—Morning and evening, Rev. W. Reid. Latchford Baptist—Morning, Rev. J. S. Cited- burn; evening, Boys' Brigade parade, speaker, Councillor R. Henshall. Christadelphians, Toft's Assembly, Rooms, Bridge-street—Evening, Mr, H. A. Williams, Liverpool. Death of Mrs. W. W. Starkey. The death took place on December 4th of Mrs. W. W. Starkey, of Latchford, at the age of 56 years./ The deceased lady had been in failing health for some years, but has endured all with great patience and cheerfulness. She had been a regular worshipper at Christ Church all her life, and always took a keen interest in every- thing connected with the parish. She was a familiar figure in Latchford, and her death is much regretted by all. The funeral took place on Monday and the mourners were: Mr. W. W. Starkey (husband), Messrs. Arthur and William Starkey (sons), Misses Bessie and Doris Starkey (daughters), Mr. T. Starkey (brother-in-law), Mrs. E. Starkey (sister-in-law),. Mrs. Jackson and Mrs. Wells “iieces), Mr. J. Starkey, Liver- pool (nephew), Mr. H. R. Colton, Manchestet, Mr. and Mrs. D. Banks, and Nurse Carey. The coffin was borne by Messrs. Thomas, Harry, Jack and Alfred Starkey (nephews). The first part of the funeral service was held at Christ Church. The Rev. G. C. Briggs (vicar) officiated. Mr. 'l'. L. Fry presided at the organ. The cor- tege proceeded to St. Thomas', Stockton Heath, where the concluding prayers were said by the Rev. G. C. Briggs, and the remains reverently laid to rest. The following sent flowers: Hus- band and children, Mrs. E. Starkey and family, Mr. and Mrs. Alfred Starkey, Mr. and Mrs. J. Starkey, Liverpool; "Lily, Alf and the kiddies," Mr. H. R. Colton, Miss E. Taylor, Nurse Carey, Mr. and Mrs. D. Banks, Mr. and Mrs. James Lowe and family, Mrs. Harrison and Miss Atkinson, Mrs. North, Crewe; Mrs. Few, Mr. and Mrs. R. Gorton. Mr. and Mrs. Cross, Carlisle: Mr. and Mrs. J. S. Burgess, Mr. and Mrs. Ashley and family, Mr. and Mrs. H. Tay- lor, Mr. and Mrs. R. Sutton, Mrs. Charles Broadbent and the Misses Broadbent, Mr. and Mrs. Frank Fox, Mr. and Mrs. Fry and family. Mr. and Mrs. H. Craven, "Tom, Lela and Agi," Mr. and Mrs. Elliott and family, Miss Bennion, Miss Place, Miss Norman, Mr. Walter Moores and the Misses Moores, "Will and Jess," "The Neighbours," Mr. Horrabin and family, "Anonymous," Staff of the L. and N.-W. Tele- graph Department, Bank Quay Station; Latchford Hockey Club, Latchford Angling Society, Staff of London and North-Western Dis- rinnda Manager's Office. Arnlev Station.</p>
        <p>THE BEST PROOF of genuine value is an increasing sale. Mothers, daughters and grand- daughters have used Steedman's Powders, which still stand as an unrivalled medicine for children. Observe the EE in Stcedman. These powcict,s contain nc poison.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-13_WidnesExaminer_art0155" n="art0155">
        <head>Human Locomotives.</head>
        <p>Human Locomotives.</p>
        <p>At the Juvenile Court on Wednesday a num- ber of youths were charged with trespassing on the London and North-Western Railway, near the Black Bear Bridge. The police evidence showed that the defendants were pushing a large, eight-ton truck along the metals, and besides it being a danger to other people, the lads were risking their own lives.—They were each fined 2s. 6d. and costs. A Tale of Laziness. A pitiable tale of laziness was related at the Juvenile Court on Wednesday, when James H. Ashurst, of 64, Plumpton-street, was charged with neglecting to send his daughter Elizabeth to school. Mr. J. Moore Murray and two school attendance officers stated that it was a case of laziness, and one officer stated that often when he called at the house at mid-day the wife had to get up out of bed to answer the door.—A fine of 10s. and 6s. costs was imposed, with the alter- native of 14 days.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-13_WidnesExaminer_art0156" n="art0156">
        <head>Church Tennis Club.</head>
        <p>A Church Tennis Club.</p>
        <p>At the annual general meeting of the Emmanuel Tennis Club a report pf the past year's successful work was read, and a balance in hand was shown. The election of officers re- sulted in the treasurer and secretary being both re-elected, and the committee was chosen as follows: Rev. W. T. Jaye, Misses E. Jones and N. Gandy, and Mews. R. Lees, jun., and A: H. Davies. Thanks were accorded the officials for their services. Miss M. Booth was thanked for her able supervision of the tea arrangements. The Unknown's Death.,</p>
        <p>An old man of between 60 and 70 years of age died in the Workhouse on Sunday, and at an inquest on Thursday it was stated that one night last October he applied for lodging at a lodging-house, saying he had come from Aus- tralia and had no friends in England. He was admitted, and, on mounting the stairs, he fell and fractured his thigh. As he was unknown he was removed to the Workhouse, where he died from consumption. His name was believed to be John Walsh, but nothing could be found about him.—Verdict: "Death from natural causes."</p>
        <p>Heavy Betting Penalty. George Ryder, living at 70, Chester-road, and carrying on business as a jobbing jeweller, was fined .125 and costs for using his shop in Bew- sey-street for betting purposes by the Warring- ton magistrates on Tuesday. The excuse was that Ryder had received "inside information" about horses, gave it to his friends, who got him to accept bets on behalf of a bookmaxer.— Joseph Bate, labourer, 16. Schoolrbrow, was fined £5 and costs for loitering in Bewsey-street for betting purposes. The police alleged that he met men and got bks. These he took to Ryder's shop.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-13_WidnesExaminer_art0157" n="art0157">
        <head>Chprches and Peace Sunday.</head>
        <p>The Chprches and Peace Sunday.</p>
        <p>The Bishop of Liverpool, in a communication to the Secretary of the Peace Society, with reference to the observance of Peace Sunday (December 21st), says that he desires to assure Dr. Darby of his warmest wishes for the Peace Society, and to say that he supports the object with his heart and soul. ' The ex-President of the Independent Churches</p>
        <p>Mr. Will Price) s " I shall be very pleased _ • say .</p>
        <p>to interest all I can in the praisewatiii efforts of the Peace Society. If the Church had one voice on this question the mad race of arma- ments would surely be arrested in countries calling themselves Christian."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-13_WidnesExaminer_art0158" n="art0158">
        <head>Five Generations Alive.</head>
        <p>Five Generations Alive.</p>
        <p>By the birth of a. daughter on December sth to Mr. T. Davies, of, 45, Marsh House-lane, War- rington, there are,..4.0w living representatives of five generations in, the 'family. The 'great-great- grandmother, Mrs. Sarah Selby, was born on April 28th, 1823, and is thus 90 years of age. The great-grandmother, Mrs. Eliza Hargreaves, who is 70 years of age, was born on April 11th, 1843, whilst Mrs. Emma Tomlinson, the grandmother, is 51 years old, having been born on January 9th, 186; The mother of the child, Mrs. Eleanor Cicely Davies, was born on December 3rd 1886. _</p>
        <p>It is very interesting to note that each of the above-named are the first-born of, the families, and all are well. The aggregate number of their years now total 238.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-13_WidnesExaminer_art0159" n="art0159">
        <head>Harmless "Wind-raisers."</head>
        <p>Harmless "Wind-raisers."</p>
        <p>In the opinion of the Rev. G. C. Briggs, the vicar of Christ Church, Latchford, sales of work are not necessary evils, as some would have them believe. In the first place, they were not necessary, and in the second they were not evils, but a useful and harmless way of "raising the wind." This opinion was expressed at the Opening of the annual sale of work last Satur- day. The sale realised the handsome sum of X 96. The following is a list of the stalls and the attendants :—Plain work stall: Mrs. Briggs and members of the Church Workers' Associa- tion; fancy work stall: Mrs. George Dakin, Miss Fairclough (Barryfield), Mrs. Hendy Cock, Mrs. John Reynolds; toy stall: Miss Atkinson, Mrs. Venn, , Mrs. Skinner; refreshment stall: Mrs. Fairclough, Mrs. Harrison, Miss Ronald, Mrs. Williams, Mrs. Patrick; flower stall, Mrs. ' and the Misses Broadbent, Miss Hutton, Miss Gladys Adams; "White Elephant" stall: Miss Broadbent, Mrs. Lloyd, Miss Morgan.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-13_WidnesExaminer_art0160" n="art0160">
        <head>Women's Suffrage Debated.</head>
        <p>Women's Suffrage Debated.</p>
        <p>A lively and interesting debate on the Suffrage question took place on Monday evening at the Junior Liberal Club in Horsemarket-street. Mr. E. Lloyd Jones took the affirmative and Mr. G. Toulmin the negative. Mr. J. 11. Booth occu- pied the chair and briefly introduced the sub- ject for debate. Mr. E. Lloyd Jones, in his opening address, said that woman demanded the suffrage because she was a subject and demanded political rights equal to those of men. Because suffrage would make a woman a power for good in politics, and would give her a direct interest in local and national questions, she would be able to give great assistance in making better and more just laws. "Women's wrongs," he continued, "will increase as econ- omic competition increases, and it is only right and necessary that she should have the vote for her own self-protection. Women have entered every profession, commercial and industrial, and demands equal rights with men. Women have trade unions, but no power, because they have not the vote. She is now a wage depend- ent, and demands the right to improve her conditions through the vote."—Mr. G. Toulmin said that it was an utter fallacy to say that women had any special wrongs that men were not willing and anxious to redress. He quoted Lord Loreburn, who stated that our laws are more merciful to women than to men. Women's trade unions have just as much protection from the State as men's, and were governed by exactly the same laws. The contention that women' should receive the same wages as men, he said, was a mistaken one. A woman worker was generally a single person, living in a family with other bread-winners. Women never entered a profession with the desire to spend her life there; she only looked upon it as a meant; whereby she could gain temporary independ- ence. If a man is a bachelor he generally has more calls upon his purse than an unmarried women, and had to look to the time when his wage and capital would have to be emplo-pd to find a home for himself and wife, and perhaps a family. And that is the time when the injustice of giving equal wages to men and , women is an injustice to hardest-worked and most useful women in the country. A woman who is married receives her wages through her husband, which can at the most be only a share of his wages, and if her husband only receives the same wage as a single woman it is a gross and monstrous injustice to the married woman. And the tendency of such conditions would be for women to prefer such a position to the more exacting duties of a household, to the detriment of the country as a whole.—Mr. W. Sanders, Mrs. Jones. Miss Booth and other ladies and gentle- men took part, and both speakers briefly re- sponded. On ,a vote being taken, the chair declared that "the No's' have it by two votes."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-13_WidnesExaminer_art0161" n="art0161">
