      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-13_WidnesExaminer_art0163" n="art0163">
        <head>OLD MAN'S ORDEAL</head>
        <p>OLD MAN'S ORDEAL</p>
        <p>Shot in a Wood, Terribly Wounded</p>
        <p>and Crawls Home.</p>
        <p>HIGH UGH SENSATION.</p>
        <p>An extraordinary shooting affair is reported from High Legh. The victim of it is Joseph Skitt, 74 years, an ex-gamekeeper, of Hollowell Brow, and he was removed yesterday evening to the , Warrington Infirmary, suffering from severe injuries to his thigh, ascribed to a shot from what is known as a trap gun. An amazing story of the old man's pluck was elicited by "The Examiner" representative. Skitt was proceeding through a wood on the Knutsford side of High Legh, to meet a game- keeper friend, and in passing over some grass his foot caught a wire hidden in the grass. A second later there was an explosion from a hidden gun, and Skitt fell with a serious gun- shot wound in his thigh, from which he bled</p>
        <p>profusely. Skitt cried loudly for help, but as the spot was a lonely one his pathetic cries were not heard, and realising the seriousness of his posi- tion he made an effort to get home. He stag- gered a short distance, and then fell into a ditch, from which he had the greatest difficulty in extricating himself.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-13_WidnesExaminer_art0164" n="art0164">
        <head>FOUND UNCONSCIOUS.</head>
        <p>FOUND UNCONSCIOUS.</p>
        <p>Getting out of the ditch, the old man literally crawled out of the wood homewards. Painfully he managed to reach the vicinity of his home, where he collapsed, and lay unconscious for some time, until he was found by two young men, who removed him into the house and summoned Dr. Thorpe, from Lymm. The doctor gave every assistance to the in- jured man, and ordered his removal to War- rington Infirmary, where he was taken in the ambulance van last evening. Skitt is suffering from severe loss of blood and shock.</p>
        <p>-- -- A nephew, who was hurriedly summoned from Shropshire, seen by "The Examiner" repre- sentative, said it was a mystery how Skitt came by his injuries. As an old gamekeeper, he would possibly have been aware of the presence of trap guns in the wood, and would have acted accordingly. The guns were, it was alleged, placed there to keep poachers away, and evi- dently Skitt had forgotten about them when he went into the wood.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-13_WidnesExaminer_art0165" n="art0165">
        <head>NEAR A MAIN ARTERY.</head>
        <p>NEAR A MAIN ARTERY.</p>
        <p>"My uncle," he said, "is now progressing as favourably as can be expected, after his terrible experience. He has lost a large quan- tity of blood, and had the shot been half an inch nearer the inside of the leg it would have punctured the main artery, aloud he would pos- sibly have bled to death before he could have done anything to help himself." Mr. Skitt is one of the best known residents of High Legh, where he resides in. a cottage by himself. He is unmarried and is very popular in the district. Up to 20 years ago he was a gamekeeper, but .has since lived in retirement. The news of the extraordinary accident has created a profound sensation in High Legh and the district between Lymm and Knutsford. "FAIRLY SATISFACTORY." The report given at the Infirmary last even- ing to "The Examiner" representative was that " Skitt is injured severely. His condition is fairly satisfactory."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-13_WidnesExaminer_art0166" n="art0166">
        <head>INSURANCE CARDS.</head>
        <p>INSURANCE CARDS.</p>
        <p>Should Name of Trade Unions</p>
        <p>Appear Upon Them?</p>
        <p>THE MAYOR'S VIEW.</p>
        <p>•.4 . The principal discussion at the monthly meeting of the Insurance Committee took place on the following resolution, proposed by Mr. Edes : "That we consider it highly important that the names of approved societies should ap- pear on all contribution cards, as this would be a great boon to the smooth working of the Act." Mr. Edes moved that the delegates, the Clerk, and the Chairman, Councillor Dr. Joseph, sub- mit that resolution to the Commissioners at a meeting the. next day. Giving instances of difficulties which existed, he said that people who travelled from town to town had no par- ticulars whatever. They had only a card, and nothing to show to which society they belonged. The societies could refuse to give them benefits. , The Chairman said he believed the Commis- sioners objected to such a proposal at the be- ginning, because it revealed the fact that a man was a trade unionist. The Mayor: That is so. Mr. Edes : The workers did not like it at the beginning, but it is not so now. Ido not think employers are such unreasonable beings to trouble whether a man is a trade unionist or not.</p>
        <p>The Mayor's View. The Mayor: I hope this committee will have nothing to do with it. We don't want to put these men in the position of having to state their trade union on the card. Some employers do object to trade unionists. They object also to particular trade unions. Mr. Massey did not think it would be any benefit whatever to have the name on the card. The Clerk thought it would be unwise for them to do such a thing until public opinion had turned round in favour of the names being on the cards. At the Ant the Commissioners had thrashed the matter out. He suggested that the should have a distinguishing number. Councillor Roberts said he agreed with the Mayor to a certain extent. He could imagine it probably being unpleasant for an insured person in the time of a dispute with his em-</p>
        <p>ployer. Mr. Edes agreed with the suggestion as to numbers. The Mayor: Keep the name of the cypher out,</p>
        <p>lock, stock and barrel.. It was stated by a member that the trade unions would strongly object, but Councillor Roberts said he thought trade unions were well ' able to look after themselves. After further discussion the resolution in favour" of a mark was lost. 26,330 Insured. The Clerk said that the register on the 12th October, when the third count was taken, was as follows: Number of approved society mem- bers entitled to medical benefit, 26,330; deposit contributors, 408: as against 24,942 and 339 last year. The increase in deposit contributors was accounted for by the fact that a lot of them had only just been found. " Model" Employers.</p>
        <p>The committee considered a recommendation of the Finance Committee to raise a junior clerk's salary to 16s. per week. It was explained that the boy was now getting 10s., and the opinion of several members was that he should get 18s. from the Ist of January. The Mayor said he was in favour of the wages of the staff being increased. The boy would get practically Bs. 6d. advance in 12 months, which example might with advan- tage be copied by other firms in the town. Dr. Joseph: The Mayor's suggestion is a good one. One or two members were of the opinion that the boy was getting enough, and one member said "lie is not the only clever boy in War- rington." It was decided that the Finance Committee consider the question again as to giving 18s. per wepk. versmi.</p>
        <p>Improved freehold farms in the CANADA land of golden 25 opportunities, for million what British farmers pay in rent. A week from acres virgin Liverpool. 8011 2f• per acre. Dairying, cattle raising, fruit • , growing, gardening. Unlimited market. Grand 4 climate. Thousands of acres in peaches. Ideal social and educational conditions. Government guarantees . farm hands and domestics situations. Good wages. ." Marvellous natural resources await investors. Write—R. REID. Ontario Government Agent. r 6.3„ Strand, London. "</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-13_WidnesExaminer_art0167" n="art0167">
        <head>LAUDANUM TO SOOTHE ME BABY=</head>
        <p>LAUDANUM TO SOOTHE ME BABY=</p>
        <p>Warrington Young Mothers Said to be Deficient in Knowledge of Child Life.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-13_WidnesExaminer_art0168" n="art0168">
        <head>PLAIN TALK AT A LOCAL CONFERENCE.</head>
        <p>PLAIN TALK AT A LOCAL CONFERENCE.</p>
        <p>The young children in Warrington are to receive extra care, and, if a new committee appointed for the purpose can make it pos- sible, Warrington will shoot ahead of any other town in the country in the matter of protecting child-life.</p>
        <p>There %vat a lengthy discussion about the methods to be adopted at a conference held under the auspices of the Guild of Help, but every speaker agreed upon the urgent. neces- sity of undertaking the work.</p>
        <p>Some painful stories illustrative of the clamouring need for such a scheme were narrated. The ignorance of mothers in the care of young children was stated to be almost incredulous.</p>
        <p>A committee representative of the Council, Education Committee, and other active insti- tutions in the town, was appointed to go thoroughly into the matter, and to report at an early date to the Executive Committee of the Guild of Help.</p>
        <p>The Warrington Town Hall on Tuesday night was the scene of one of the most important con- ferences ever held in the town. It related to the care of child life below school age, and was convened under the auspices of the Warrington Guild of Help. Mr. W. J. Rowland, the secre- tary, submitted a number of apologies. • The MAYOR, who presided, said the object of the Guild of Help must recommend itself to every good citizen, and to everyone who had the interest of the people at heart. At the last meeting of the Health Committee it was decided to appoint another nurse to look after the chil- dren between one and five yeas. (Hear, hear.) Thus Warrington had done something in the direction suggested by the Guild, and if the Guild could bring forward any further scheme to look after the children, or benefit the com- munity at large, he felt quite sure the Corpor- ation would welcome it readily.</p>
        <p>of good habits was left undone; it meant that the child was exposed to the. hearing and seeing of much which he should rat hear or see. I was told that many of the children listen to the words of the street corner loafers, and when, at the age of five, they went to school they used oaths and other words that were shocking from anyone's lips, far less those of a child. I do not say they know what the words mean, but they use, when they get angry, the words they heard used by the angry men, the use of whose words passes naturally into the life of the children.</p>
        <p>NO RAY OF HOPE.</p>
        <p>The Rev. H. OGDEN said until they had a definite scheme there was no ray of hope. Canon Stevenson had referred to the street being the child's university, and to the child having to gain its English from the loafers at the street corners. He would ask in what part of War- rington would the child hear these oaths? He ventured to say that the loafers would not be found, nor the oaths heard, at the street corners \ of the better class of artisans. He could name numberless streets where no such loafers would gather, and he could name localities where they did congregate, and it was in those where they were up against the problem of overwhelming poverty. The problem wa's there, and there it must be met. _ _</p>
        <p>Amongst the better-class working people there was a sensible knowledge of how to bring up a child, but in the poorer localities there was intense and most woeful ignorance. They would have to remove that, and if the meeting could find a scheme to do it there would be a practical outcome from that gathering.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-13_WidnesExaminer_art0169" n="art0169">
        <head>"DRAGGED UP BY MOTHERS."</head>
        <p>"DRAGGED UP BY MOTHERS."</p>
        <p>Miss JANET BLAIR, of the Manchester Univer- sity Settlement in Ancoats, opened the confer- ence. She said there were many social evils at the root of the trouble. To have IA race of healthy men and women they must first raise the health •of the children. The problems of infant mortality were the problems of infant vitality. In poorer quarters, said Miss Blair, families grow quickly; the children seem more numer- ous, and the accommodation is more limited. I am even afraid the children are dragged up by mothers. They are not the victims of cruelty, but of too much kindness, and their life is made .qp of scoldings and caressings.. There is no time to reason with and to help in the mould- _</p>
        <p>ing of the child's character in any way. We might make more use of our parks as children's nurseries, and I never can gee why instead of men attendants there we should not have a few women to look after children de- posited in the parks by tired and weary mothers for a few hours. It would be a great boon. It might be said by that we are undermining parental responsibility and independence amongst the poorer classes. Well, most of us were kept in nurseries, and we did not have our mothers all the time with us, and lam sure we did not think our mothers shirked their responsibilities, and we are, per- haps the most independent of people. We make a fuss if we have not got a cook or a housemaid, and therefore I think we ought to be careful how we speak abOut our poorer sisters. Miss Blair gave illustrations of the work done in Manchester day nurseries, where for 4d. or 6d. per week mothers could leave their children in comfort and safety.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-13_WidnesExaminer_art0170" n="art0170">
        <head>OUTWARD RESPECTABILITY.</head>
        <p>OUTWARD RESPECTABILITY.</p>
        <p>Father TALLON said environment, physical defect and morality all came into the question. The environment question in Warrington was important. In his own district he could point out different spots, which if altered, would tend to elevate the people, if not in morality, in the out- ward respectability of parents and children. Out- ward respectability did not touch altogether the question of morality, and he thought they laid rather too much 'stress upon outward respecta- bility without touching the source and origin of moral defects seen in children. Life was sacred, because the source from which it came was sacred, namely, from God, and until they had brought that home to men and women they would fail to elevate and get a real moral tone in their citizens. Many people entered matri- mony with the idea of getting a home and hav- ing money to play with. They had no idea of the tremendous responsibility which came with parenthood. An effort should be made to induce mothers to realise their responsibility, not only to the State, but to God.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-13_WidnesExaminer_art0171" n="art0171">
        <head>CLASSES FOR MOTHERS.</head>
        <p>CLASSES FOR MOTHERS.</p>
        <p>The Rev. 11. OGDEN asked the number who attended the classes for mothers in Manchester. Miss TEALE, a superintendent of one of the schools, said the attendances differed in different neighbourhoods. ' Mr. OGDEN : What I am concerned about is the attendances from the poorest neighbour- hoods. From the most squalid portions of Man- chester is there a better attendance than from the better artisan quarters? Miss TEALE : The largest attendances are not from the very poorest districts, but in these districts the women come in largest number to the sewing classes. I agree that the very poor are not touched by the classes for mothers. Directly you get in contact with people with 18s. or 20s. a week you cannot touch them, and some other organisation is wanted to undertake this work.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-13_WidnesExaminer_art0172" n="art0172">
        <head>TAKEN YEARS TO LEARN.</head>
        <p>TAKEN YEARS TO LEARN.</p>
        <p>Mr. IT. KINGDON, the chairman of the Medic&amp; Inspection Sub-committee of the Education Committee, gave an instance of the great work being done in Bradford. In this country we were 20 years behind the Germans in the care of the children, and in the work being done we had no cause for congratulation. In Warrington the rate of infant mortality had been reduced to what was regarded as the wonderful figure of 92 per 1,000. Why was it wonderful? Because they recalled the time when it was over 200. But 92 was not a figure to boast about; it was a miserable thing. If they applied such a rate nationally it meant that, roughly, 100,000 of the babies born died every year. A significant fact was that among the middle class, who, without enjoying lux- uries, lived in comfortable surroundings, the infant mortality was 60 per cent. If they took it at 40 per cent. it meant that naturally 40,000 died every year. On the question of extending the school agr he thought that by allowing a child to leave 13 years that child left at the age when it con. be most impressed in every respect. The Go', ermnent would have to raise the school age, bm meanwhile the Education Committee could do it with by-laws. When they tried to do it, how- ever, they had bricks thrown at them. (Laugh- ter.) One councillor, who did not represent the Labour party, but was a candidate for a Labour constituency, said they were "education mad in Warrington." It was to the 'class that member represented they must look for help, because they were the people who were chiefly respon- sible for the present state of affairs, and had 'power to alter it. He hoped eventually to see all the voluntary agencies which were dealing with the question of child life grafted on to the main health machinery of the town, under the Medical Officer of Health.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-13_WidnesExaminer_art0173" n="art0173">
        <head>CHILDREN WHO SWEAR.</head>
        <p>CHILDREN WHO SWEAR.</p>
        <p>Canon MORLEY STEVENSON said they knew the extraordinary difficulties of mothers in some homes. It was all very well of them to talk as they did, but the fact was that in many homes a child at the age of three or four, when ho had his "piece" for breakfast, was sent out to play in the streets, and his life was largely a street-led life. It meant that much should have been den,: in the incial2atic-,2</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-13_WidnesExaminer_art0174" n="art0174">
        <head>FATHER'S BEER; MOTHER'S GIN.</head>
        <p>FATHER'S BEER; MOTHER'S GIN.</p>
        <p>Mr. B. S. TOWNEOE said they wanted to see a practical outcome of the conference. He had often talked to the poorer children in the Park, and learned that their meal had consisted of a little ice-cream or some pastry, and a sip of their father's beer or their mother's gin. (" Shame.") .These children afforded an ex- ample of the problem they had to face. The people who had the power to deal with it more than anyone else was the Education Committee. The children were in the melting pot, and the Education Committee could help enormously in arriving at a solution of the problem. The speaker then, to give the meeting some- thing definite, moved: "That a committee be appointed to consider whether aRy, and if so what, steps should he taken to improve the lot of children under school age, and this committee be requested to report to the Executive Committee of the Guild of Help."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-13_WidnesExaminer_art0175" n="art0175">
        <head>ECONOMIC BASIS.</head>
        <p>ECONOMIC BASIS.</p>
        <p>The Rev. T. T. JABIES seconded, and said he agreed there was a deeper question than had yet been met by the experiments amongst the very poor, and that they would have to meet. There was the economic basis which livuld have to be dealt with as soon as possible. Some of them might not want to look that in the face, but it was there, and they must face it and see if it could not be dealt with effectively. They could not put people in a position where life and everything was uncertain and expect from them all the moral virtues. Alderman Ipits EVANS, in supporting, said the Health Committee were not a philanthropic society, but they were cordially in sympathy with those voluntary efforts which had been outlined that night, and which did not crime within their own immediate purview. Many of the bad conditions were due to abject poverty. He had known of mothers who had struggled until they lost heart, and if something Gould be done to brighten their lives it would be done. The resolution was carried.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-13_WidnesExaminer_art0176" n="art0176">
        <head>NEW COMMITTEE.</head>
        <p>THE NEW COMMITTEE.</p>
        <p>Mr. F. W. MONKS moved that the committee be: The Mayor and Alderman James Evans, representing the Corporation; Mr. C. E. Parker and Mr. Holman Bingdon, representing the Education Committee• the Revs. Canon Steven- son, T. T. James and Tallon, Mr. B. S. Town- roe, Mrs. Broadbent, Mrs. Booth, Mrs. Dimelow, Mrs. Langdale, and Mr. W. J. Rowland. He said he had met many married women of 25 and 30 years who could neither read nor write. If they were so ignorant, it was God help the little ones' who were placed in their care.</p>
        <p>Mr. C. E. PARKER seconded, and dealt with the question of infantile mortality. In regard to the deaths below one year, Warrington was lower than the 18 large towns in Lancashire, but in regard to the ages from four to five years it compared badly with the other towns. The committee was appointed as suggested.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-13_WidnesExaminer_art0177" n="art0177">
        <head>LAUDANUM FOR BABY.</head>
        <p>LAUDANUM FOR BABY.</p>
        <p>Miss M. BRoADBE!rr, speaking about the work done in connection with the Babies' Welcome, referred to the ignorance shown by Warrington mothers. "One mother," she said, actually brought some laudanum, because she considered it a good thing to soothe her baby." Mr. J. MOORE MURRAY, Director of Education, thought much of the trouble arose through girls being sent to work without domestic training. The little they had learnt at school was lost, and they were utterly unprepared to undertake the responsibilities of a home. He felt that little good would be done until it was recognised that the future mothers must have a better and a longer education. The Rev. Dr. MELLOR thought those in posi- tions of influence should not hesitate to spread knowledge on matters of sex among young people—they should address themselves freely and .unblushingly to those fundamental ques- tions! He appealed to the medical profession to give the mothers expert knowledge by means of tho proposed schools. A vote of thanks to the Mayor, proposed by Mr. ROWLAND, concluded the conference.</p>
        <p>A LIFELONG CRIPPLE was to be His Lot—Paralysed from the waist down—Cured completely by DR. CASSELL'S TABLETS.</p>
        <p>The mystery of nerve action and nerve failure \ is yet to solve, but the cure of Paralysis is an accomplished fact. Mrs. Rosetta Sharman, of 81, Miall-street, Radford, Nottingham, says:— "My boy was taken with some obscure throat trouble. He wasted away, and then he became ralysed in his legs; he could not move a muscle from the waist downwards. We took him to hospital, where he remained eleven weeks, and was then turned out incur- able. We had made up our minds that he was to be a life-long cripple when we got some of Dr. Cassell's Tablets. Imagine bow pleased I was when, after a little time, he could move his foot. We went Sharman. on with the Tablets, and steadily he improved, till be was quite cured. He is now at work, for he is seventeen." Dr. Cassell' s Tablets act upon every nerve cell and tissue, vitalising, restoring, strengthening. That is how they cure. They act more brilli- antly than any other medicine known in cases of Nervous Breakdown, Anaemia, Debility, Sleeplessness, Nerve Pains, Heart Weakness, Kidney and Stomach Disorders, Children's Weakness, Spinal and Nerve Paralysis, and general bodily fatigue, brain fag, and all run- down conditions. Send 2d. to-day to Dr. Cas- sell's Co., Ltd., 418, Chester-road, Manchester, for a free sample. All Chemists sell Dr. Cas- sell's Tablets at 101 d., 1/11, and 219.</p>
