        <p>_ ...... ...,.....--- • : • ... 1ii.."% t .. a ..... . .. • : ' _ I. it. -W/I'4. ' 151 .. ICT :-'..4.- '. ' x „ ~ . , ....... ,e ~.., _ _._ ~.. ,</p>
        <p>PENKETH.</p>
        <p>Labourists Enjoy Themselv es.—A very enjoy- able evening was spent at the Penketh Labour Club on Tuesday, when an. excellent concert was submitted. Mr. A. T. Clay presided, and the artistes were Misses M. Watson, Dodds, H. Hall, and Messrs. E. Walker, F. Hall, B. Smith, G. Walker, E. Sanders and the Prize Quartet Party. A vote of thanks to all helpers was pro- posed by Mr. Gill and seconded by Mr. Foden.</p>
        <p>Tannery Band Honours.—The Penketh Tan- nery Brass Band did well in: a brass quartet contest held at Bolton on Saturday. The first part of the contest was open to all bands, and from the second nine of the leading prize bands were excluded. The competitors played their own selection. The awards of the adjudicator (Mr. Herbert Bennett, of Bolton) were as fol- lows :—First section: Penketh Tannery, War- rington (conductor, D. Dobson), 1; Wingates Temperance, 2; Skelmersdale. Old, 3; Good- shaw, 4. Second section : Skelmersdale Old, 1; Congleton Town, 2; •Earlestown, .Viaduct, 3; Penketh Tannery, 4.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-13_WidnesExaminer_art0183" n="art0183">
        <head>DEATH OF MR. THOMAS GANDY.</head>
        <p>DEATH OF MR. THOMAS GANDY.</p>
        <p>A Popular and Esteemed Resident.</p>
        <p>Quite a gloom has been cast over the village by the passing away, early on Saturday morn- ing, of one who was highly esteemed and re- spected in the district, in the person of Mr. Thomas Gandy, " Brierfield," Penketh. Mr. Gandy, who was 58 years of age, received his early education at the Friends' School, Pen- keth, and also at Grove Park, Wrexham. He ° then entered the furnishing -trade, passing from Messrs. Marsh, Jones and Cribb, of Leeds, to Messrs. Garnett &amp; Son, of Chester, with whom he remained for many years. He afterwards joined a large furnishing firm in Cincinatti; where he gained a wide American experience. On his return to England, he took up a position in connection with the Progress Works, connected with the Penketh Tanning Co., a, position which failing health compelled him to 'relinquish. He , was a thorough sportsman, but Chiefly • with a gun. A kind-hearted, genial man, he was ever a friend of the poor, who haveindeed lost a good _ _ _ .</p>
        <p>friend. He was eOnnected with the Methodist Church. He had a seizure, which proved fatal, about six weeks ago. He leaves a widow to mourn her loss.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-13_WidnesExaminer_art0184" n="art0184">
        <head>Funeral.</head>
        <p>The Funeral.</p>
        <p>The high esteem in which he ,was held was manifested by the number of people who aesem- bled to pay a last tribute of respect at the funeral, which took place on Tuesday at Pen- keth. A service was conducted at the house by the Revs. J. Gunnell and H. Middlewood, and at graveside the Rev. J. R. Jones officiated.</p>
        <p>The thief mourners were: Mrs. Gandy (widow), Mr. and Mrs. F. Gandy.,7lliss Dutton, Mr. and the Misses Hall, Mr. J. Hall, Mr. J. W. Garnett, Mr. Samuel Fildes,- Mr. and Mrs. Heal, Mr. and Mrs. P.' C.' Garnett, Mr.., and. Mrs. F. Coop, Mr. and Mrs. J. Coop, Mrs. Wil- liam Garnett, Mrs. Jenks, Mr. C. E. Parker, Mr. and Mrs. J. G. Withinshaw, Mr. and Mrs. Scott, Mr. Dow, Mr. J. Watson, Dr. Murray, und the Rev. E. J. Gawne.- .</p>
        <p>Wreaths were sent by the following: Mrs. Clark and family, Mr. and Mrs. Medley, Miss Moston, Mr. and Mrs. Moston, Mrs. Dodds and family, Mr. C. Gandy, Mrs. Kearns, Mr. and Mrs. Heal, Mr. and Mrs. Posnett, Mr. and Mrs. Parker, Mr. and Mrs. J. W. Garnett, Mrs. D. Garnett (Southport), Mxj. J. F. Dutton, the Misses Standish, Mr. and Mrs. F. Gandy, "Riversdale," Mr. and Mrs. Mills and "Jock and Gwen," Mr. and Mrs. Snoddy, Mrs. and Miss</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-13_WidnesExaminer_art0185" n="art0185">
        <head>WOMAN ILL TWELVE YEARS</head>
        <p>WOMAN ILL TWELVE YEARS</p>
        <p>Wants Other Women to Know How She Was Finally Restored to Health.</p>
        <p>"I think a woman naturally dislikes tz: ,kel troubles known to itie public, but complete restora- 'ion to health means io much to me that 1 :annot keep from tell- ig mine for the sake I other suffering 'omen. "I had beet ill bout twelve . years. ,nd had .eleven doe- ors. I had dragging down pains, bilious attacks, and wa's "get ling worse all the time:. I, would hardly get over one attack when I would be ill again. No tongue can tell what I suffered from cramps, and at times Lcobld hardly walk. The doctors said I might die at one of those times, but ' I took Lydia E. Pinkham's Vegetable Compound and got better directly. Your valuable medicine is worth more than mountains of gold to suffering women." (Name and Address on request.) Lydia E. Pinkham's Vegetable Com- pound, made from simple roots and herbs, contains no narcotic or harmful drugs, and is now acknowledged to be the most suc- cessful remedy for female ills. Thousands of voluntary testimonials prove this fact. Write to-day for a free private book (to "men only) to the Lady Manager, the Lydia E. Pinkhatn Medicine Co., 42a Hampstead Road, London, N.W. For. 2s. 9d. she will send you a bottle of the. Lydia E. Pinkham Vegetable Compound, or for 4s. 6d. a much larger bottle. Any ,-theiniat-will.eopiget-it at these figures.</p>
        <p>..iake her • • • • ~•,...:.:...:•,,,,,Eti.:.iiki-44,.. • tillp,'"" " • ililliotliii .t] 1....ir 1 iNii; cl iligilili ''-,,, Ip Atrlti 0, : v,r, !=gh„:liiii •-•--... iiliii!;ll..lii :Ilipiii. ,„,,,,:i,::i., ...._-• .• - • , ia.iiii,ii!liii •-•-,--: ,'4,,-..k 'a, riP'.•• ,/, if'," a . • . 1,1%* • tf i • I- ( k</p>
