      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-13_WidnesExaminer_art0186" n="art0186">
        <head>STOCKTON HEATH</head>
        <p>STOCKTON HEATH</p>
        <p>' TOWNSHIP TITTLE-TATTLE.</p>
        <p>More light is wanted. Alexandra-road complains again. Yet there has been. brilliant moonlight During these past few nights. But the residents want gas—gas. They won't be happy until they Get more gas lamps. They may Possibly get them soon, for the Parochial Committee are considering. Stockton Heath firemen have been smiling. Warrington has given them the sniN. Stockton Heath tire lighters can always Get to a fire when it occurs. Warrington's new .motor could not do So. Warrington has had the laugh at Stocld on Heath, • But now the boot is on the other foot.. • A Heathen on Monday wrote, What- has COMO over .the village clock?" • Curious enough the clock re-appeared In its old position again on Monday, After an absence of nearly three months. it was then ,on a time strike, but . • Now is regulating the hours correctly. Will the. Parochial 'Connhittee become house builders? - Councillor C; Dukes wants the Council to erect. A number of working class cottages. - • When the-subject •comes to, be, discussed, • We ought to have some interesting matter. To-day is the day of the great football match. Will Stockton Heath beat Latehford? The Heathens say that they shall—enough said. :Carol singers have arrived-at the Heath. The nights are brightened—or. otherwise— With the melody they dispense. 'Tisa sure,sign Christmas is coming. • The Wesleyans were delighted with The results of the sale.' of work. • AS announced in "The Examiner" last week; They 'made over £67 profit. It was worth feeling joyful aliout.</p>
        <p>Congratulations.—At the meeting of Stockton Heath Parochial Committee on Tuesday, Conn- cillor`H. J. Westbrook was accorded a hearty welcome after his long and serious illness, and was congratulated upon his recovery.</p>
        <p>Justices on the Bench.—The justices at the Police Court on Tuesday were Mr. W. Fletcher (in the chair), Captain Roberts, Mr. F. W. Monks, Mr. T. Baxter, and Mr. J. A. Ransome.</p>
        <p>"Over the Hurdle."—William Burke, 15, Winwick-street, Warrington, was fined ss. and costs at Stockton Heath Police Court on Tues- day for stealing an iron hurdle, value is., at Whitley Superior, on December 2nd, the pro- perty of Benjamin James Parsonage, jun.—De- fendant de-flied the theft.</p>
        <p>Workers' Houses.—At the conclusion of the Parochial Committee on Tuesday, Councillor C. Dukes asked it, was liecessa,ry .to give.anotice of motion if it would involve- expenditure of public funds, and on being answered in the negative he handed in the following motion: "That this Council is of opinion that owing to the scarcity of cottage property, the parish should seek powers to erect a number of work- men's dwellings."</p>
        <p>Met " Friends" at Market.—Thomas Morris, farmer, Little Legh, was summoned at Stock- ton Heath Police Court on Tuesday, for having been drunk- in charge of a horse and was stated that on the 17th November defendant was in charge of a horse and trap in Victoria- square. It was seen that defendant had not proper control of the horse. Inspector Breese and an officer assisted him out of the trap.— Mr. Steel said Mr. Morris regretted his position. He was nearly 70 years of age, and there had been no previous conviction. He had been to the market on the day in question, and ;had met friends.—A fine of 10s. and costs was im- posed.</p>
        <p>Let There be Light !—A. letter was read at the Parochial Committee meeting on Tuesday from a resident in Alexandra-road, complaining of insufficient lighting., The letter stated that. the Council received something like .e5O in rates from the district, and the only expenditure in- curred was in the emptying of ashpits and the provision of one street lamp.—Councillor Whit- ley said some time ago they had the matter up for discussion, and it was decided that they could not see their, way to erect a lamp. In his opinion it required more than one lamp, and there was no "doubt that owing to the unlevel state of the road it was dangerous to go aim-1g it when dark.—A member said two other resi- dents had written requesting the Council to erect lamps, and it was stated that one of these had a lamp near his house, whereupon the Chairman jocularly inquired, "Does he 'want this lamp inside or outside his house?—lt was eventually agreed that the matter be referred to the Lighting Committee.</p>
        <p>A " Nugget" N ight.—A service of song enti- tled " The Californian Nugget" was submitted to a good audience at the Wesleyan Schoolroom on Monday, on the occasion of the season's third entertainment in connection with/ the Hill Cliff° and Stockton Heath Band of HOpe. The chair was occupied by the Rev. Gebrge Curtis. Ths Drograthme was given by the Latchford Wesleyan Band of Hope Choir, and was well received. A special feature was the manner in which the choruses, solos and duets were rendered. Mrs. T. S. Hughes supplied the connective readings to an altogether excellent piece. The Rev. G. Curtis eulogised the valu- able assistance that had been given by their friends and neighbours from Latchford, and proposed a. vote of. thanks. Mr. G. Whitfield seconded and expressed a hope that the Stockton' Heath Band of Nope would be able to return the compliment by giving an entertainment .at Latchford. The vote was carried unanimously.</p>
        <p>The Fire Brigade.--,The report of the Tire Appliances Committee was submitted by Coun- cillor McDermid at the meeting of the Paro- chial Committee on Tuesday, and was unani, mouuly adopted. It stated that the brigade was</p>
      </div>
