      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-13_WidnesExaminer_art0188" n="art0188">
        <head>EXAMINER, SATURDAY,DECEiVIBER 13, 1913.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY,DECEiVIBER 13, 1913.</p>
        <p>called out ,to a house at 8, Warburton-street, but fortunately the alarm was a false one, and their services were not required.—A meeting of the committee was held at the Red Lion Hotel on Tuesday, December 2, when it .was :resolved that. thirty cppies of the revised list of hydrants should be obtained for the use of the committee and the firemen. At the meeting it was reported that the removal of the Fire Station and bell had been delayed through the death _of Mr. Stringer. It was further decided that if prac- ticable-the erection of the drying post should be proceeded with at once. A practice took place on Saturday, December 6, when, in addition to several, members of the committee, there were ten members of the brigade. The new equip- ment from Messrs. Merryweather's was in- spected and tried. The hydrants were tested and it was found that the pressure was very good. The practice was stated to be the most satisfactory held this 'season. It was stated that the ladder was long enough for most pur- poses, but they had now received the new one from Messrs. Merryweather.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-13_WidnesExaminer_art0189" n="art0189">
        <head>AvEliarry PROBLEM.</head>
        <p>A AvEliarry PROBLEM.</p>
        <p>The Bridge Topic Once More.</p>
        <p>The time occupied in turning the Stockton Heath Swing Bridge was a topic raised at the Parochial Committee meeting on Tuesday Councillor Clarke said that for the last 20 years pedestrians had been allowed to cross tile bridge while it was settling into position after boats had passed through, but during the last few weeks that arrangement had been altered, and now all foot passengers and vehicles must wait until the bridge was in position. It took for that operation from a minute and a quarter to a minute and a half, and the consequence was that people had been kept longer than ever before. He had not heard that there had been any accident or complaint about the former practice. The new system was more. dangerous, because when the gates were thrown open, foot passengers, cyclists,, motor cars and horses and carts all streamed on to the bridge together.</p>
        <p>Waste of Valuable Time. One day when he was at the bridge there were about 300 people on both sides, and if these people were kept a minute and a half it seemed to be a great waste' of valuable time. Ile thought they ought to send a lettei from that committee objecting to the alteration. Councillor Westbrook said he could not under- stand why the company had allowed foot passen- gers to go on the bridge before it was locked, and a cyclist, walking with his machine, refused: It was eventually decided to write as Coun- cillor Clarke suggested.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-13_WidnesExaminer_art0190" n="art0190">
        <head>SANITATION QUESTION. •</head>
        <p>SANITATION QUESTION. •</p>
        <p>parochial Committee's Scheme.</p>
        <p>The question of converting the pail system in Stockton Heath to that of the water-carriage system was mentioned at the Parochial Com- mittee meeting in the Hall Cafe on Tuesday, when the Ulerk read the report of the Stockton Heath Sanitary Sub-committee's meeting, held on Noveipber 25th. . -</p>
        <p>- This report stated that the sub-committee had considered the following Particulars: There are 813 houses On the Pail system, and taking the cost of conversion at .27 per house, this would entail an expenditure of £5,691. The cost of the repayment of a loan to this extent, spread over a period of 20 years and paid by half-yearly payments of principal and interest, would be £415 per year. Against this it could be esti- mated that the annual saving on the present contract for the removal of nightsoil by the emptying of pails would be about £225, and the saving of expenditure on new pails about £3O, making a total saving of £255. However, the additional cost to the parish would be ..2160 yearly; and it was stated that a penny rate would produce £7O.</p>
        <p>The report sets forth that in the event of the owners 'agreeing to eontributn one-half of the cost of the conversions, the work would entail no additional burden upon the rates.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-13_WidnesExaminer_art0191" n="art0191">
        <head>From a Health View.</head>
        <p>From a Health View.</p>
        <p>The sub-committee resolved: " That from a sanitary and health point of view it is, in the opinion of this-subLcomMittee, advisable to con- vert the existing pail closets in the parish to the water carriage system, and as the properties weuld be thereby improved, it be a recom- mendation to the Parochial Committee that all owners be approached with a view to their con- tributing one-half the cost." . _</p>
        <p>The sub-committee also considered the ques- tion of dealing with the removal of the refuie of the parish under the existing system, and it was resolved that it be a recommendation to the Parochial Committee that tenders for the work be at once advertised for.. In reply to a question, it was stated that the committee had power to compel the owners to convert the pail-closet to the ,water-carriage system, but the cost would be thrown on the rates.</p>
        <p>'Councillor Whitley asked if the sewers were capable of taking any extra matter.</p>
        <p>The Clerk: Yes</p>
        <p>It was further stated that the drain connec- tions to the houses would be satisfactory for the new system. _ _ .</p>
        <p>Councillor Pearson: I beg to propose that this report- be adopted. He understood that the next proceeding would be to get into communication with the owners, and if they declined to meet them in the way. suggested, they should proceed on their own lines. This was seconded and adopted.,,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-13_WidnesExaminer_art0192" n="art0192">
        <head>RISLEY.</head>
        <p>RISLEY.</p>
        <p>Independent Methodist Church, Fearnhead.— A social evening was held in the School on Sat- urday, when prizes were distributed to the fol- lowing for success in collecting on penny cards: Master E. Mason, obtaining the prize for most money collected; Mr. John Houghton and Master Fred Bold, receiving Bibles. Mr. Joshua Hughes occupied the chair. On Sunday, Open Sunday was observed, in the school, a large congregation being present. Mr. J. Barton occupied the chair and Mr. R. Bold gave an interesting address.</p>
        <p>Well-known Teacher Retires.—Miss M. W. Wightman, who has retired under-the age limit from the ,teaching staff of the Risley. School, was presented by old scholars and friends with a travelling bag and dressing case combined. The Rev. J. J. Glover presided over a good gathering of subscribers to the testimonial fund, and Mr. Joseph Clare, an old scholar, handed over the gift to Miss Wightma,n. In doing so he dwelt on reminiscences of his school days, and hoped she would enjoy her well-earned rest. Miss Wight- man made a suitable reply, thanking the friends for their useful gift. Short addresses apprecia- tive of Miss Wightman's work as a teacher were given by Mr. Clarkson, Mr. Bouchier, Mr. Holden, and 'apologies for absence were. read from Mr. Wilson, H.M.1., of schools in the dis- trict, and Dr. Beckett, chairman of the local Education Committep. Mr. Jos. C.. Warburton, who had acted as treasurer to the fund, was thanked for his services.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-13_WidnesExaminer_art0193" n="art0193">
        <head>C 11 AP PENH ALL.</head>
        <p>C 11 AP PENH ALL.</p>
        <p>Primitive Methodist Concert.—A successful concert was held in the new Assmblyjjall of the Independent Methodist Church on Satur- day- evening in aid of the building fund. By arrangement with Mr. Emlyn Davies and some of his pupils and. friends, an excellent programme was arranged, Owing to the. indisposition of Mr. A. Grounds, the humorons element was en- trusted to MP: Hale, of Grappenhall. The artistes, who Met with cordial receptions, were the Misses B. .Brough and.E. Taylor,.with Mi G. Jones at the piano. Messrs. G. Wilde and E. Walker sang very well together. A vote of thanks to the artistes was accorded, on the motion of CounCillor R. Henshall, seconded by .tr. R. Nelson.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-13_WidnesExaminer_art0194" n="art0194">
        <head>MAL</head>
        <p>MAL</p>
        <p>LINES FROM LVMM. The new bridge ds the topic. Work is to be commenced soon. It is to have a span of 109 ft. Lymmites say the bridge will be Quite equal to that at Warrington. _ There is nothing like local rivalry. There were ghosts at the school Of the Primitive Methodists on Wednesday. They were there to assist in a rebital By the talented elocutionist, Mr. J. Bernard Everybody was pleased with the ghosts.</p>
        <p>The New Bridge.—The contract for the erec- tion of a bridge to carry the new road from Cherry-lar e across the Upper Lake to Crouch- ley-lane i. 3 been let to Mr. Harry Fairclough, who will commence the work immediately. Tile structure is to be of ferro-concrete, and will be similar in design to the new Warrington Bridge. It will have a span of 109 feet, with a rise of 24 feet.</p>
        <p>-Drowning Mystery.--LDnring the week the body of Tito Alas Barker, 58 years of age, who lived at Agden 'Cottages, was recovered from the Bridgewater Canal near Burford Wharf. The deceased was an attendant on a threshing machine, and it is surmised that he was going alongside the canal on Thursday of last week, when he was seized by a violent coughing and fell during the coughing_ convulsions- into the water. He was married, but had no chil- dren. There was a doubt as to how Barker got into the water, and the jury decided to return an open verdict of `,` Found drowned."</p>
        <p>Temperance Women.—That the committee of the Lynam branch of the British Women's Tem- perance Association is doing its best to main- tain the interest of the members and *public is evinced by the excellent speakers they get to address their meetings. On Tuesday evening a capital address was given. by Mrs. Winchester, of Liscard, the lion. treasurer of the Cheshire ,County Union. The speaker, in a graphic man- ner, gave an outline history of the Association from its inception just forty years ago, when the wdmen of Ohio commenced the "whisky war," down to the present time, showing its phenomenal growth throughout the civilised world and its beneficial influence on humanity, irrespective of race or colour. Mrs. .L A. Pena- liggon, of Bucklow Hill, Very ably fulfilled the duties appertaining to the chair.</p>
        <p>.Home Rule and Pictures.—For the purpose of giving the people of Lymm an opportunity to hear something about Home 'Rule, from the Unionist standpoint, ,a cinematograph enter- tainment under the auspices of the Lymm Habi- tation (No. 1,141) of the Primrose League was held in the Drill Hall on Monday night. Speeches on the question were given by Mrs. Legh, the Ruling Councillor, and Mr. W. Peter Rylands. Mr. W. Parkinson, of Warrington, operated the cinematograph, showing pictures of Mr. Bonar Law's "Ulster campaign," which were, however, eclipsed in popularity by a num- ber of spools depicting the King's recent visit to Warrington, scenes in various streets, the attendance of their Majesties at the Town Hall, and Messrs. •L Crosfield's works, being thrown on to the screen. Light refreshments and music were also supplied.</p>
        <p>Christmas Carols.—On Wednesday night, in the Primitive Methodist Schoalroom, Mr. Janies Bernard gave another of his ctr,lebrated dramatic interpretations of life and character. The first part of the programme was devoted to scenes from Shakespeare's "Much Ado" About Noth- ing," specially arranged for the Lymm Gram- mar School students. Mr. Bernard's expositions of the characters in this comedy were given in a masterly style, the intrigues of the mischief- maker, the trials of the lovers, and the happy ending, were faithfully portrayed. The Second portion of the recital was occupied with the first and last staves of Dickens' "Christmas Carol." Here the elocutionist was on S. more popular ground, and the hearty applause which was accorded his efforts. was thoroughly merited, his ghosts being as perfect a creation of the super- natural as one would wish to meet, and one realised that it was no wonder old Scrooge was brought to ".see the error of his ways." Fur- ther enjoyment was atfforded by the contribu- tion of a trio of songs chaimingly sung by Miss Elsie Hewitt, of Warrington. Mr." Fred Champ was,the accompanist, and Mr. C. H: S. Willson the chairthan. .</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-13_WidnesExaminer_art0195" n="art0195">
        <head>GLAZE BROOK.'</head>
        <p>GLAZE BROOK.'</p>
        <p>Birthday "At Home."—The Primitive Metho- dist Church at Glazebrook has always shown itself to be full of enterprise and capable of carrying out a vigorous scheme of progress. To raise funds for its maintenance the members of of the church hit upon the idea of a Birthday "At Home," which was held on Saturday. The guests were invited by letter, and Were requested to subscribe one halfpenny: for each year they had lived. The schoolroom had been most taste- fully and artistically decorated. Furniture had been introduced, curtains hung at the windows, and a profusion of choice flowers suitably arranged around the room. The whole effect was pleasant in the extreme: The, guests were received by Mr: and Mrs. Robert Allen, of Cul- cheth, under whose genial welcome everybody soon felt "at home." In the unavoidable ab- sence of Mrs. Ratcliffe the presidency of the 'At Home" was assumed by her daughter, Miss Ratcliffe. A. splendid.programme-had been arranged, in which the following al:tikes' took Part: The young ladies gave two sketches, en- titled "Our at 'ome day," and "Wanted, a mother's help." The 'vocalists were Miss 'Rat- cliffe and Miss Martha Bridge. Miss Annie Bridge was the elocutionist, and a violin solo was rendered by Miss Maggie Bridge. Games and refreshments were provided, and a most Pleasant evening was greatly enjoyed by the numerous company who assembled for this unique • event , The funds of the church *ill Profit by this effort to the extent of £26 13s.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-13_WidnesExaminer_art0196" n="art0196">
        <head>PADGATE.</head>
        <p>PADGATE.</p>
        <p>Tableaux Vjvants.—A delightful entertain- ment is promised at the Wesley Hall, Padgate, on Tuesday evening, when representations of striking tableaux vivants will be given. These will be illustrated with songs by a talented company of artistes from Lymm. The proceeds will go fo the new schools building fund.</p>
        <p>Wesley Guild.—The monthly devotional meet- ing was held on Monday evening. The Rev. E. Morgan presided and the roll was called by Mr. H. Tomlin. Excellent short papers were read by the Misses Ball and Tomlin on "The Oneness of Love," and by the Misses Sykes and Sutcliffe on "Life Without Love." The lesson was read by Mrs. Plinston, and a duet was rendered by. Mesdames Dakin and Ward.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-13_WidnesExaminer_art0197" n="art0197">
        <p>PEOPLE come from all parts of Lancashire and Cheshire to have their eyes tested by</p>
        <p>Joblistoil Viatsoni Sight Testing Optician, 84, BRMGE 5-MEET, WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>Steel Spectacles from 2/6. Rolled Gold from 7/6.</p>
        <p>Please Note.—Mr. Wats-on -attends on Monday and Tuesday from 11-0 to 4-30; Wednesday 11-0 to 5-30; Thursday 11-0 to 1-0; Friday and Saturday 11-0 to 5-30.</p>
        <p>Hours of business, 9 to. 7-30. Saturday, 9 to 9-30.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-13_WidnesExaminer_art0198" n="art0198">
        <head>MOORE.</head>
        <p>MOORE.</p>
        <p>Wesley Guild.—The members who attended the meeting in the schoolroom on Wednesday evening had a treat in the visit of Mr. A. Apple- ton, who gave his humorous paper "Schoolroom humour." Mr. Appleton delighted with episodes, some of which were from his personal experi- ence, and a large number which he must have taken a lot of trouble to collect. Mr. J. Jack- soil presided.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-13_WidnesExaminer_art0199" n="art0199">
        <head>HIGH LEGH.</head>
        <p>HIGH LEGH.</p>
        <p>Shooting Accident.—A full report of the acci- dent to Mr. Skitt will be found on another page.</p>
        <p>Burning Accident.—A serious accident oc- curred to a young child Of Mr: Lee, a joiner, of High Legh. It appears that the.child was play- ing with matches, when its clothing became ignited. The mother, who was working in an: adjoining room, rushed to the child and put out the fire. The child is suffering from shock, as well as severe burns.</p>
        <p>Cricketers' Dance.—The High Legh Schools on Wednesday night were the scene of a most successful dance organised by the committee of the High Legh Cricket and Bowling Club on behalf of their funds. Mr. J. Bend was the M.C., and discharged his duties efficiently. The dancing was continued until a late hour to the strains of music supplied by Mr. Percy Healey, pianist, and Mr. Herbert Hughes, violinist.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-13_WidnesExaminer_art0200" n="art0200">
        <head>GREAT SANKEY.</head>
        <p>GREAT SANKEY.</p>
        <p>Women's Guild Bible Class.—The members of this Guild met on Tuesday evening at Sankey Schools on the occasion of their annual social, and they turned lip in good numbers. A pleasant time was spent in games, dancing, &amp;c., and a varied programme of music was also gone through.</p>
        <p>Whist Drive.—A whist drive took place at Sankey Sunday Schools on Thursday night in connection with the Primrose League (Newton Division). Twelve tables were occupied and the games were thoroughly enjoyed. Result: Ladies-1, Miss E. Craske;. 2,. Mrs. James Ray; 3, Mrs. H. Clarke. Gentlemen-1, Mr. W. Nor- cott; 2, Mr. F. Goulden; 3, Mr. F. Dendy. Miss Tinsley- distributed the prizes to the winners, and the refreshment room was in charge of Mesdames G. Clarke, William Dutton, G. Oar- nett, James Ray, jun., and William Woods.</p>
        <p>Footballers' Whist. —The Great Sankey Foot- ball Club held theip second whist drive and dance in the Sankey Sunday. schools. About 80 were present. The. arrangements were credit- ably carried out by Mr. Edwin Clarke (secre- tary of the club) and Mr. J. J. Worsley.. The refreshments were in the bands of Mrs. G. Clarke, and done in her usual good style. The Brothers .Hall • provided excellent music for dancing, and the M.C.'si for dancing and the whist .drive were Messrs. B. Darlington, E. Houghton, E. J. Dumbill and William Lightfoot respectively. The prize-winners were :—Ladies: 1, Mrs. James Ray; 2, Mr. William Lightfoot (plying as lady) ;± 3, Miss • Annie •Lightfee" Gentlemen: 1, Mr. James Ray; 2, 16. Le0n:17..: Davies; 3, Mr. A. Houghton.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-13_WidnesExaminer_art0201" n="art0201">
        <head>TABLE TALK.</head>
        <p>TABLE TALK.</p>
        <p>The World's Tribute of Interesting Items of News. QUAINT AND AinUSING.</p>
        <p>The Mexicans have a turn for the picturesque which displays itself in. the street. names • of the capital. •There is "Love of God-street," "The Holy Ghost-street," "Sad Indian-street," "Street of the Wood Owls," -" Lost Child Street," and a cautionary "Pass If You Can-street." Shop names, too, in Mexico, nre•out of the common. A drug store -calls itself " Gate :of Heaven" .(Surely ambiguous !), and a drinking saloon :describes itself, frankly. as "The Bait of • the 'Devil " I .</p>
        <p>Evil of Stuttering.</p>
        <p>Though much stuttering is due to such cases as heredity, imitation and fear, Dr. F. A. Bryant states that in many cases no explanation can be given. It is usually begun before the ace of seven, is known in persons of eighty, and, be- sides producing nervous disorders, prevents full aeration of the lungs, and thus gives rise to lung diseases and to morbid conditions from unoxygepated blood. In an industrial school, the breathing capacity of nine stuttering boys of 12 and 13 averaged 88 and 109 cubic inches, that of non-stutterers of same age averaging 166 cubic inches.</p>
        <p>Trips to the Moon.</p>
        <p>M. Esnault Pelterie, one of the ,best-known French aviatiori experts, is of opinion that the day will come when communication between the earth and the moon and stars will be pos- sible in aeroplanes. Providing that radium is one 'day found .in larger ,quantities, .and that some way! of harnessing its energies is discov- ered.by Scientiats; he calculates that an aero- plane weighing a ton should be able to cover the 238,000 miles which separate the earth and moon in three hours and five minutes. About 8001b.' of radium would be necessary to provide the fuel for the journey. At the current price about two thousand million pounds' worth would be required.</p>
        <p>, Eat Cheese for - Meat. A physician claim that cheese is .a most. im- portant element in the food of workmen. For centuries- Englishmen have been great beef eaters, and as a ConSetiuence their systems have developed a great need for meaty (proteid) foods. Meat is becoming higher in price every day, and the poor man finds himself with the meat appe- tite, but not a meat pocket-book. The only way he can save himself is by taking to cheese.. It is claimed that there is as large an amount of proteid in a half-pound of cheese as there is in a pound of meat, and it costs, taking the rate of consumption into account, we suppose,, less than one-fourth as much.—" Science Siftings."</p>
        <p>Red Hair.</p>
        <p>"All red-haired women dr(jealous," accord- ing to Professor C. 13. DavenpM.t, of the Univer- sity of Missouri. "When .a red-haired woman has dark blue or violet eyes and dark eye- lashes, she is good to have • for a friend and bad to have for an enemy, is 'catty.' A curious antipathy is that of red-haired persons of oppo- site sex for each other. Among thousands of matings I have found only two cases where both husband and`wife are red-headed, and I am assured by red-haired persons that the anti- pathy exists. If, as is. sometimes alleged, red hair is frequently associated with a condition of nervous irritability, this is a eugenic anti- pathy."</p>
        <p>The "Language" of Monkeys. Concerning the supposed languag6 of anthro- poid apes, Professor Boutan has recently taken up the question, and arrives at a negative result.: It will be remembered that Dr. Gamier came to the conclusion that monkeys have • a language analogous to human language, and express themselves by signs ..as well as by sounds which they emit.- According to Pro- fessor Boutan, there is only a difference or degree between this and human spee'ch and not one of kind. He observed the sounds made by a gibbon which he had captured When young, and his experiments .cover more thdn (lye years. He finds that the animal can pro- duce- only spontaneous and instinctive sounds corresponding' to a .state of satisfaction, or the like, or again hi -it Slate Of uneasines'Slir fear; also great excitement, and the sounds do not .appear to correspond to a real language, hut rather to what he - calls ." pseudo-language." While sounds of real language are acquired by education, those of s' ado-language are purely spontaneous. and fie thus differs froill Dr. Garnier's .conclu6iOns,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-13_WidnesExaminer_art0202" n="art0202">
        <p>Telephone No. 528.</p>
        <p>A Practical Xmas Gift.</p>
