      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-13_WidnesExaminer_art0205" n="art0205">
        <head>JOTTINGS BY AN OLD JOURNALIST.</head>
        <p>JOTTINGS BY AN OLD JOURNALIST.</p>
        <p>button School Question. The reference to "outside children " in the course of the discussion of this question at the 'Runcorn Guardians' meeting reminded me of a school lock-out that once occurred in i dis- trict in which I worked for some years. When I first went to the place there was an extensive School 'Board area, embracing two towns and several villages. One of the former obtained a Charter of Incorporation, and proceeded to form its own borough School Board. On the easterly border was a scho.ol to which a num- ber of children from beyond had been accus- tomed to &amp;, because it was the nearest to their scattered homes in, the rural area. The Borough Board declined further responsibility on account of these unless the County Author- ities assured them of compensation. The latter declined to make any grant, whereupon it was resolved to exclude the' youngsters in question. Then, of course, the parents began to agitate, for the only available school in the opposite direction was far, far away. The ultimate result, after a prolonged display of: obstinacy, was an arrangement whereby the locked-out section were re-admitted, the County Education Committee paying amply to ensure continuance of the old conditions.,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-13_WidnesExaminer_art0206" n="art0206">
        <head>Greece.</head>
        <p>Greece.</p>
        <p>The incidental allusions to this ,country in last week's "Women's Questions" Column and in "Table Talk" somehow recalled a little experience which a Methodist, minister told me of the other. week. He said that whenever he conducted .a prayer meeting at a certain chapel in his circuit one of the leaders invariably prayed for "showers of grease." Of course, he was himself profoundly thankful that that particular petition was never answered. The same reverend gentleman narrates a story of a voyager who was impressed by the whiteness Of land which was sighted in the distance. So he went to the captain And begged him to explain the peculiarity. "That, sir," was the reply, "mis Greece." Is it., indeed?" said the passenger ; well, now, I thought it was snow !" Ghostly Pranks.</p>
        <p>It. would be easy to support the claim set. up in the paragraph heaaed "Ghostly Jokes" in last week's "Literary Gleanings." There was the one that played pranks upon the residents at Asfordsby Rectory, Leicestershire, for over 30 years, and that only a few months ago was laid low by the rector, who, donning cassock. and -surplice, solemnly exorcised it.,s During the present year, too, Strabane, Ireland, has had its ghost, the head constable taking down the statements of. some railwaymen who re- ported • having seen the apparition. John Pinkerton, an esngine cleaner, describing the lirst visit of the "ghost," said that it appeared in the cleaning-shed after knocking loudly at the door. It gave unearthly yells. Armed with a crowbar, a signalman made a dash at the spirit, but could not strike it down. On the second night seven men saw the "ghost." It appeared again on a subsequent night, and "disappeared through a window.' "The form was that of a man of stout build," stated an- other engine cleaner. "It ran under, the engine, in front of it, behind it, and every- where.. We shivered."-</p>
        <p>Bewitched Maidservant. While I was in Ilalifax I had to record many more or less mysterious happenings, and I may lust mention one concerning a domestic, servant who seemed' to me to, be rather a handy sort of person to have-around,,speCially during a strike of colliers. This is how the fun began. The occupants of the house, who at the time referred to had this treasure in an earthern vessel, heard a tapping at one of their windows on the ground floor, and, on .the , cause being searched' for, a considerable quan- tity of coal was observed to be falling. To some extent the coal appeared to fall from the sky, but good deal seemed to come from the roof of one of the houses. An investigation of -site roof, however; failed to reveal' any Clue to the source. Toward midnight the shower teased, but instead of the coals falling from the roof they began to fall inside the houSe, and all Monday and Tuesday, at irregular .periods, coals fell in various rrooms. Sometimes pieces of coal fell singly, and sometimes a large num- ber of pieces would come together as though someone were *emptying the contents of a scuttle thFough a hole in the ceiling. ,It was noticed that a great quantity came down the stairs, and, when, the door at the foot of the stairs was closed, it was bombarded with frag- ments which struck against it with sufficient force to make permanent marks on the wood-- work. It was observed that the infeerious showers only ocairred when the servant maid —who had only Jut been engaged—was in the house, and, on being questioned, she confessed that similar occurrences had taken place at her home, where not only coal but hatpins, hair-, pins, buttons, and the like often fell apparently from nowhere. The -bewitched maidservf,nt soon left, and after her -departure the, extra- ordinary showers ceased. I interviewed neigh- bours and other independent and disinterested witnesses, and their assured me of the literal accuracy of the haunted householders' version. I could fill my column with similar experience's, but, ,this being the special month for such stories, I will not further anticipate what other " Examiner " contributors may have to write on the seasonable theme.</p>
        <p>Small Fry. On Saturday the angling notes began— fish were weighed in last week." I was re- minded of another Yorkshire story, and</p>
        <p>." No</p>
        <p>voliture to reproduce it in the hope that the reader may not have muCh ,difficulty with the dialect. It was the annual match oi the Katchem Fishing Club, and sport Was wretched- ly bad. The day after one of the anglers who had taken part in the contest was found in the smoke room of the Angler's Best by one of his "pals" looking very down in the mouth. " Wot's up, lad I" Didn't tha cop owt yister- day?" "Ay, ah.copped yan reet enough, th' only yan as iwor copped." "Tha- did wed, Jack, ah reckon than get furst prize." "Nay, 'twor a -bad, do. ,Ah'd put it pockit, an' w'en:.they comes ter weigh in ah,eudna' finn'd it .onyweer. But at neet w'en ah gets 'oam ah fun it i' me watch key !" The Olfactory Nerve.</p>
        <p>Two men with whom I have worked on dif- ferent-- papers have lost the sense of smell entirely. I imagine that if this should catch the eye of Councillor Gough, who, so bitterly complained to the ,Health Comthittee of the Warrington Town, Council respecting. offensive smells from works near his home, he will be ready to turn oily&amp; green with envy. I have 'a friend who last season went on holiday to Ostend, and left that • resort prematurely be- cause he was unable to endure the bad smells Which he encountered there of all places! Many years ago, Dr. Bowman, the • eminent Chemist, facetiously declared in ,my hearing that in the corridor of one hotel in Germany he detected no fewer than seven separate and dis- tinct odours of an 'objectionable kind. After all, there are much worse places than War- lington for evil scents.</p>
        <p>Doctors and Insurance. This has nothing to do with the matter upon Which Dr. Manson wrote last week; all the sarne, I happened to think of it as I read his letter to this paper. When the medical 14'ater- -114 had patched up their quarrel with the Chancellor over the Insurance Bill, I went into the Surgery of a member with whom I am familiar; and asked him what they hhd meant hY all the fuss. "Why," he replied, candidly hud gaily, "we were just bluffing for the best terms we could get." "And now you are ;Itislied, eh?" "personally," he assured me, 1` I have every reason to be," and he went on 10 explain why. SOme of his prolessiOnal ihrethren in that locality were not quite so rank, but I notice that three of them have _ giready got new motor oars.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-13_WidnesExaminer_art0207" n="art0207">
