      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-13_WidnesExaminer_art0208" n="art0208">
        <head>EXAMINER, SATURDAY, DECEMBER 13, 1913.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, DECEMBER 13, 1913.</p>
        <p>ME CREATION of TsE wopLD.</p>
        <p>The Clay Books of Ancient Babylon and the</p>
        <p>Remarkable Records They Contain.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-13_WidnesExaminer_art0209" n="art0209">
        <head>STORY OF THE DAYS OF CREATION, THE DELUGE AND THE ARK.</head>
        <p>THE STORY OF THE DAYS OF CREATION, THE DELUGE AND THE ARK.</p>
        <p>Among the very interesting things which the explorers of Babylon are bringing to the sur- face there is nothing that has more human interest than the-nuNerous books from the old libraries. Big palaces and towers are, of course, very valuable as revealing the skill of the ancient peoples, but their books show us what they read and believed, with what stories they amused their children, and give us also their superstitions and fears and quaint beliefs. Several very long chapters could be prepared .upon the contents of Babylonish and 'Assyrian books, and there would be much to amuse us in the ancient riddles and children's reading books, to say nothing of the charms and in- cantations used by older folks to prevent danger and disease. But we are selecting for the pre- sent some records which everybody, perhaps,</p>
        <p>the Tower of Babel and the scattering of man- kind. The legend says that men had "turned against the father of all the gods" -under a leader who was evil. At Babylon they began to build a mound and a tower, but the Winds destroyed it in a night, and the god .Anu "con- founded great and small on the mound. and confounded their speech and made strange their counsel." The word for confounded in this passage is the eNact word used in the -Book of Genesis.</p>
        <p>It is rather curious to notice that Xisuthos in the Deluge story not only resembles Noah, but Enoch also, for it is said that he was carried away by the gods and did not die like any of the rest of mankind: We have also in the narrative the three sons Of Noah-. .Shem is called on the tablets Sarhu,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-13_WidnesExaminer_art0210" n="art0210">
        <head>SCHOOLDAYS.</head>
        <p>SCHOOLDAYS.</p>
        <p>"Dad's going to give me a watch," Willie informed his aunt.</p>
        <p>"That will be nice," replied his aunt, "and it will help you getting to school in the morn- ing."</p>
        <p>"Yes, that's so, 'cause when I get to school I can .look at the watch_and see just how late I am."</p>
        <p>THE NEW BABY.</p>
        <p>"I hear you have a new baby at your house," said the grocer to. Susie. "Is it a girl or a boy?"</p>
        <p>"It's a boy," said Susie, "and I wish it was a girl." _ _</p>
        <p>"Maybe you could get it exchanged," sug- gested the grocer. "I'm afraid not,".. said the little girl sadly, "you see we have used it four days."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-13_WidnesExaminer_art0211" n="art0211">
        <head>HIDDEN PEARL.</head>
        <p>THE HIDDEN PEARL.</p>
        <p>Deep down in the sea lived and grew a pearl in an oyster-shell. It grew and grew until it was the size of a tiny pear, and was all the colours of the rainbow. It took exactly seven- teen years to grow. At the end of that time a fairy found it and presented it to the queen. the queen was quite young and very beautiful, also as-!,good as she was fair. ..She ruled her kingdom wisely and Well. Her name was Rosamond. Although her subjecfs loved her, they desired a king, but no one from far and near was good enough for Queen Rosamond. - Now, all round the borders' of the -kingdom were high towers, guarded by soldiers, who were supposed to be constantly on the look-out for the enemy. But the queen didn't seem to have any enemies; and her faithful subjects loved home and peace too well to desire fame ,as warriors. So, when townsfolks, were busy over trade, and country folk going to market, and•the soldiers enjoying themselves and- mak- ing merry instead of keeping watch, the enemy came to the kingdom and invaded it. The hostile king commanded that no-soldiers should enter the queen's palace or the gardens. King Elwin was a warrior, handsome And bold, , but as no battle could. be fought, since the people were all prisoners, he went to the queen , and said he would be quite willing to withdraw his troops and restore - the kingdom to Queen Rosamond again if she would make ,him a present of a beautiful- Pear-shaped pearl he heard she wore on a gold necklace, the like of which had never been Seen before or since. In her anxiety for the safety of her subjects, the queen granted the request, but, strange to ' say, the pearl had disappeared! One night, when the people were all asleep, the king wept to the palace, stole the pearl, and hid it in an old oak tree in the palaCe garden, the inside of which was quite hollow: The king climbed up the tree, and, fastening 'a rope to one of the branches, easily let hirnself down inside. He next-took the casket which contained the pearl, and buried it down very: deep, afterwards cover- ing up the place us' it was before. He slipped back just before daylight, and nobody knew 'anything about it. For months the kingdom was searched high and low for the Missing pearl, but nobody could find it, and none thought of looking in the oak. At last the queen issued 'd proclamation that whoever, could lind the pearl should have her hand in marriage. Now, this was just what King Elwin had been waiting for, lie h.ad fallen in love with Queen Rosamond the first time he saw her,,, and while the people were huriting for the lost pearl, had amused himself by observing the cUstoins of the people and the rich agricultural country, which he confessed to himself was better than his own ; the people, too, were More industrious., and he thought how mwil better it would be for them if they had :a king.</p>
        <p>One day, when Queen Rosamond was sadly walking about the palace gardens thinking' of her poor subjects, King Elwin approached.,her and asked her hand in marriage, as he knew where the pearl was. Now the queen was very haughty, the more so to King Elwin, as she con- sidered him the , cause of all her misfortune. Quietly she told him, that he who married her must first bring the pearl to her and restore the kingdom to its former state. At this the Icing. joyfully presented her with the casket, and said he would do anything to please her if she married him. Queen Rosamond consented, and soon the glad news spread far and near, and enemies became friends.</p>
        <p>At the marriage ,feast King Elwin was asked how he had found the lost pearl, but this he hesitated to confess, for he now saw he had done wrong. But he could not avoid it, and the story had to be told. Afterwards. he begged forgiveness, first from the queen, and after- wards from the people for deceiving them. and laid that it was his great love for Queen Rosa- mond pat had led him to do it. But he. would never take it from her again, which put every- one in a good humour. _ _</p>
        <p>The queen replied that she was bound to for- give one who had confessed so honourably, and her subjects saia they were delighted to have a king. So all were restored once more to peace and happiness, harmony and love.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-13_WidnesExaminer_art0212" n="art0212">
        <head>CAPTAIN DROWNED AT WIDNES.</head>
        <p>CAPTAIN DROWNED AT WIDNES.</p>
        <p>George Gandy (42), a ketch captain, was re- covered from the Widnes Old Dock on Sunday evening. At the inquest the evidence showed that Gandy was seen. late on Saturday night in h perfectly sober condition. Next day his cap was found floating on the water Rehr his boat, and the body was recovered at the same spot. A ladder used for boarding the ketch was in its position against the wall of the dock.- . It was stated that Gandy was shortsighted, hnd.Coroner Brighouse remarked that Saturday night‘was very dark and stormy, and it looked as though Gandy had slipped into the water when proceeding down the ladder.—The verdict was "Accidental death."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-13_WidnesExaminer_art0213" n="art0213">
        <head>WORLD OF LETTERS.</head>
        <p>THE WORLD OF LETTERS.</p>
        <p>*PROPERTY,</p>
        <p>Authoritative Protest Against an Over-dominant Individualism.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-13_WidnesExaminer_art0214" n="art0214">
        <head>DUTIES AND RIGHTS.</head>
        <p>DUTIES AND RIGHTS.</p>
        <p>A book of considerable value, combining as it does the. essays of different writers on various phases of the question of the ownership of pro- perty, has just been opportunely published by Messrs. Macmillan and Co., Ltd. The essays, which, collectively, go to the root of the land question, constitute an historical; philosophical, and religious survey of the problems which gather round the holding of property. There are seven essays, and. although they differ in emphasis on particular points, there is a general agreement running through the whole. The whOle, work is a protest against an over- dominant individualism, the assertion of the unqualified right Of acquiring, retaining, and perpetrating property Alien such creates con- ditions which render it almost impossible for many of the citizens of a State to develop their manhood and womanhood. There is one point which .receives prominence- in the essay of H. G. Wood, of Birmingham, which is fresh to us. We refer to the influence of the Reformation on ideas concerning wealth and :property. The writer traces the growth of individualism to the sense of individual respon- sibility, the outcome of the Reformation: This at first was especially in religious things.. The -Reformation emphaSized the idea of each man standing in his own lot, each man being respon- sible to and having free and 'independent right of access to his Creator. This separate indivi- dual right 'naturally extended-itself to material things; it removed, amongst other things, the moral witness of the Church (contaminated as it may have become) in regard to its moral witness .as to the use of wealth. But it failed .to, supply anything adequate in its place. Puritanism removed restrictions in the acquisi- tion-of .Money. lt, developed a public conscience which insisted on labour and sobriety for the poor, but.made no attempt to check. the accu- mulation of wealth. -True, it did concern, itself about usury, and a law was passed prohibiting a lender to exact more. interest than 6 per cent. But, on the whole, its chief emphasis was upon the freedom of the individual to do what he would with his own as long as he 'observed the laws, human and divine. It would be unfair, to blame the Puritans, because they failed to anticipate the social evils which, to some extent, .spring out of this very emphasis on individualism. No doubt one di the fruits of this can be found in the growth of what is generally known- as .the Manchester School of Politics, and the principle of Laissez Faire, the rooted trust in individual responsibility and self-help. Honesty in bargaining was part, of their religious inheritance. The only fault that can be found with this product of the Reformation, if such it be, is. not that they were wrong in their particular tenets, but that they failed to realise that these tenets did not embody the whole of the required truths. . .</p>
        <p>Mr. L. T. Hobhouse contributes a brilliant essay on the "Historical Evolution of Property," in which he differentiates between " property for use' and ,"property for power."- He illus- trates the difference between control and use of things which give • freedom and security, and the control of persons through things which give power to the owner.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-13_WidnesExaminer_art0215" n="art0215">
        <head>DIVINE RIGHT OF EVERY MAN.</head>
        <p>DIVINE RIGHT OF EVERY MAN.</p>
        <p>Possibly the best way to give an idea of the purpose and niain thought of the book is to cite a number of the pregnant passages in which the book abounds. .</p>
        <p>We will .with the 'introduction by Bishop Gore. lie says: • •</p>
        <p>We are bound to believe that, 'whatever in- equalities must subsist among men, every man has the divine and equal right to realise him- self. The success of a:civilisation for 'us must be measured not by the amount and character of its products or material wealth, nor by the degree of well-being which it renders possible for a privileged class, but by the degree in which it enables all its members to feel that an adequate measure of free self-realisation is granted them. On this ground then our civili- sation is open to the most serious indict- ment</p>
        <p>A state must be judged, and should judge itself, by its tendency to generate in all its citizens. a worthy type of life. If at any stage it finds that the institution of property, as it exists, is fostering luxury and exaggerated power in a few, and enslaving or hindering the many, there is nothing to prevent it rectifying whIA is amiss. Property is relative to char- acter: it is a means towards a good life,, and a good life for all men.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-13_WidnesExaminer_art0216" n="art0216">
        <head>PROPERTY AND PERSONALITY.</head>
        <p>PROPERTY AND PERSONALITY.</p>
        <p>The Rev. H. Scott Holland, who writes on property and. personality, refers to the teachings of the Bible and the early Fathers, and says The result of such a tradition might be summed up in the historic phrase that all pro- perty was held in trust. Those who used if had to answer to God, as good stewards, for its use. There was no' moral or legal possibility of standing on the bare claim of possession. The possession of private property was conditional, not absolute. And its public utility mist be -justified, and answered for„at the bar of diVine judgment. The masses of workers have dropped to the position of pure wage-earners. Their exist- ence is never in their 'own hands; nor are they responsible for their own maintenance. The stability, the power to look beforQ. and after, the assured hold on reality, the embodiment of their own wills in a material fact, which we philosophically recognise to be the Moral and spiritual value of private ownership—all this is denied them.</p>
        <p>Present conditions least the vast multitude of workers to become mere items on the surface, without any secured future, without any sure grip on facts,. without any stored reserve, with- out any established status. Personal value finds no public Witness. Character has no firm pivot round which it can build up its fabric. The inner life misses its outer support. We must establish the upremacy of justice over all conditions under which private pro- p2rty is held.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-13_WidnesExaminer_art0217" n="art0217">
        <head>Property Held in Common.</head>
        <p>Property Held in Common.</p>
        <p>Men may win the moral qualities which the sense of property evokes, by owning things in</p>
        <p>common.</p>
        <p>• Every individual citizen, who is conscious of what his citizenship means, gains,. so •far, the ethical value Of a collective owner. He is moralised by such self-possession, • mediated for him through his membership in the State. Collective ownership may come into play over and beyond the area which private ownership can'cover, and so can spread out, for the many, the opportunity which our present system con- fines to the few. And this will depend on how far collective ownership can work upon the individual conscience and imagination with the same force as we now attribute to private pro- prietorship. The confidence with which we meet this last inquiry will depend entirely on our psychological estimate of personality. . The writer does not carry his inquiry further as to the psychological estimate Of personality. He simply indicates, as above, that collective ownership may to some extent compensate for individual ownership.</p>
        <p>Back to God all rights run. His righteousness is the bond of all human fellowship. Man's authority to say of anything "that is mine-" restc, finally, on his power to say "I am God's."</p>
        <p>TIIE CHRISTIAN IDEA.</p>
        <p>The Rev. Vernon Bartley, of Mansfield College, Oxford, has an .illuminative paper on (Continued in next column.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-13_WidnesExaminer_art0218" n="art0218">
        <head>Christmas Numbers</head>
        <p>Christmas Numbers</p>
        <p>The Taller keeps up its reputation for admir- able illustrations in its Christmas number, and in addition to its usual array of wit and art presents a series of coloured pictures of • first- rate merit. Warrington readers will be .inter- ested to find a capital full-page picture by Mr. Wilmot • Lunt. There are stories by Muriel Hine and -Winifred Graham.</p>
        <p>The Illustrated Sporting and Dramatic Ne‘A issues its christmas number under the tit "Holly Leaves," and gives as a presentatio picture a splendid reproduction of Lady Butler' picture, " Quatre Bras." sThe contributors a stories are Eden Phillpotts,. John 'Oxenham Barry Pain and Richard Harding Davis, from whom much may be expected, and who 'keep well uP to their repute. Will Owen, Gordon Brown and. Tony .Sarg give us some splendidly humorous pictures.</p>
        <p>;s IC fl 7s</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-13_WidnesExaminer_art0219" n="art0219">
        <head>New Music.</head>
        <p>New Music.</p>
        <p>Messrs. Asclierberg, Hopwood and Crew, Ltd., 16, Mortimer-street, London, W. lse.—"Octobre." By Felix Godin (com- poser of "Septembre"). Is. 6d. net. A tuneful composition froin a gifted musician. Plenty of swing and not gifficult. .</p>
        <p>Waltz.—" Maiden Blush." By Archibald Joyce (composer of "Dreaming," &amp;c.). Net price, Is. 6d. Another of those beautiful dreamy waltzes, which we are sure will be appreciated by all dancers:- We • like the -second movement, as there is a striking melody. We advise all orchestras who wish to be up to date in their dance music to get it</p>
        <p>Songs.—The Song of the Column" ("March, Lads, March "). BY Emmeline Brook. . Price is. 6d. net. Suitable for bass or baritone.</p>
        <p>This is a rousing baritone song in march time. Very pleasing to both 'singer and player. Sure to be an unbounded success if sung on the con- cert platform this season. The accompani- ment also contains some beautiful_ harmonies, and is not at all difficult to play.</p>
        <p>" The ,Picture in my Heart." By H. Eane Wilson. Price is. 6d. net. Sung by Mr. John Harrison.</p>
        <p>All singers ought to get this song, as we Mel sure it will not only be appreciated on the con- cert platform, but in the drawing-room. There are some delightful chords for the accompanist; at the same time the singer can do himself jus- tice.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-13_WidnesExaminer_art0220" n="art0220">
        <head>Literary Gleanings.</head>
        <p>Literary Gleanings.</p>
        <p>THE SPARROW OF D</p>
        <p>Writing in an essay after a visit to Italy, Waterton makes some -interesting speculations as to the species of bird referred : to in the Psalms of David as a sparrow, and says: '' The Royal Psalmist, whilst bending down in peni- tential prayer *fore his offended Maker,' ex- claims: ' I have watched and am becom as a sparrow all alone on the house-top.' I have often wondered .what bird this could be, know- ing, by daily experience, that, it could not. be the house sparrOws for the house-sparrow is not solitary in its habits. My arrival in Rome let me at once into the secret. The • bird to which the repentant King of Israel compared himself is a real thrush in size, shape, -habits, and in song, with this difference from the rest of the tribe, that it is remarkable throughout all the East for sitting solitary-on the •hablfa- tions of man. I found out the bird was the. true solitary thrush', ' and paid particular at- tention to its habits. It only associates With its own mate in .breeding time; and even then it is often seen quite alone upon the house-tops, where it warbles its sweet and plaintive strains, and continues its song as it moves in easy flight from roof to roof. It breeds in holes in the walls, and lays five eggs of a very pale blue. They .much resemble those of our star- ling. The bird itself is blue, with blaCk wings and tail. The solitary thrush is seen in all countries of the East up to Syria and Egypt."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-13_WidnesExaminer_art0221" n="art0221">
        <head>TIIE BOOkLOVER'S LONDON.</head>
        <p>TIIE BOOkLOVER'S LONDON.</p>
        <p>The man who is tired of reading about Lon- don is no true Londoner. His heart.warms to every recollection of the streets that he knew as a boy, most of which have changed, god some of whfch are marvellously preserved. In Mr. St. John Adcock's London, the London of Defoe and Fielding, of Ainsworth, Dickens, and Thackeray, and of Besant and Gissing, the two authentic Londoners of the ,last generation of novelists—the old places and streets and houses never fade. They are there for ever, and splen- did people walk in them. You. can meet David .Copperfield at every turn; and Esmond is there, bowing familiarly to .Henrietta Temple; though she was not of his day; and Evelina ; and Nigel, pursuing his fortunes not far from Fleet-street. Nay, while in the neighbourhood of Staple Inn you may even question Edwin Drood about his mystery; and going along Holborn you would at last come to the guests of the famous dinner party which Gilead P. Beck gave in "The Golden Butterfly." But London is peopled with great shadows; it is full of the great 'houses of. the past, and, there is hardly a yard of its chattering streets that cannot be made inter- esting by those who know' it well. Such a guide is Mr. -St. John Adcock with whom one may never have a dull moment.</p>
        <p>RUSSIAN PILGRIMS TO JERUSALEM.</p>
        <p>In England and in Western -Europe gener- ally this is not the age of simple faith, but Mr. Stephen Graham's unique books reminds us that that age has not everywhere passed, away. His narrative brings us into an atmosphere most strangely compounded of mediEevalism and modernity, but in all essentials the Russian pilgrim appears as far more suggestive of the primitive era of Christianity than of the twen- tieth century. It is true- that much of the long journey may now be travelled by train, that the last stage' is a sea voyage, and that the Christian pilgrim is no. longer liable tb be murdered on hostile territory by Moslem fdeS. But before embark- ing at Odessa many of the peasant devotees have traversed enormous 'distances on foot. and they still suffer with the utmost cheerfulness hardships which to the people of almost any other European nation would seem intolerable,. author's'account of the passage in the pil- grims' :steamboats from Constantinople to Jaffa would in some respects be revolting were it not that the picture of herded squalor and extreme physical discomfort throws into higher relief the spiritual fineness of the men and women who endure these things with serenity of soul. Most of them are aged folk, and the pilgrimage is the crowning act of their lives. Many die before it is accomplished. The un- wonted exertions, long marches, privations, fastings, and even the sheer pressure of the crowds that gather at the holy places in Jerusa- lem prove. too much for worhFout frames. And yet it is in no spiplt of fakir-like asceticism that the moujik enter S on his holy and hazardous quest. "The pilgrim's -ideal," says the Rus- sian authority whom Mr. Graham quotes, "is a sweet feeling of the heart in prayer."</p>
        <p>. Mr. Graham thus describes his first view of the Sepulchre:. "It was a surprise. The whole was so ruined, so patched and grimed, so ancient, and withal so enigmatical. It seemed as if it might have been produced_ Only the night before by Some evil magician. Certainly that round which the, Crusader: and the Saracen had fought-, and round. which the Arab hawkers loafed and screamed, was not beautiful. It had in it an appearance of death.</p>
        <p>This is really rather a horror to the fastidious. The noise" about it and the offal of the East are appalling. What shall one say of the Turkish gendarme sprawling on a sofa at the entrance smoking his cigarette and lazily looking at his half-drunk cup of coffee? Even within there is heard the noise of the incautious movements of'Greek and Armenian priests; the church is vast and strange, ruined, dirty beyond won is, With VerminouS walls all cracked and chipped. One has entered into a mysterious and awful , chamber."</p>
