      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-13_WidnesExaminer_art0223" n="art0223">
        <p>INSCRIBED PIEC Containing Fragment of art Accou</p>
        <p>,111 BABYLON, Deluge, written B.C. 2100.</p>
        <p>• • •/,:.*V- ; • • • ' ,Lr' e -ra I"A)`‘:44'u 1 ':- • e 4 A" ;awe:, -';9,4t.z.x..-w.irgir-aui •.. -"........3 wir ~.''' ./.,..,- ie. ', ,: :• • -_, .4'0..., .(Mt ''': iki,,,4-,.., ~ , -t ', r •••-• s ..6.-• ikev Ikallir- .1 of ol :_fX:: fr., illaM-IPZ4* , 00 . ..,..".4,,.4WailfillegirproiMiblikeiAral, ' %.7.,gz.v42.A.w • , „ ''Ts:-r-r:-ei*:---lI2V .:7111--- • ill / l" • s'Atit . 'w-: ' .N.... ~..,_ ' Vakil rdit 7-,644,:zt a `", ••. eif,"ritz'-' : lfeLV te,,,,Vidlarr, ''' ,AlPPiWarilliV7lXo,4lllllr4-f ..., - -,- • E OF CLAY FRO nt of the</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-13_WidnesExaminer_art0224" n="art0224">
        <p>would agree are the most interesting of all, the old accounts of the Creation of the World and the Deluge.</p>
        <p>BABYLONISH BOOKS.</p>
        <p>We must, first of all, understand what ancient books were likes They, may have had parch- mentS; of this we are not quite sure. They certainly .had papyrus to write upon. But the common, every-day' book was made of clay, and many thousands of them have been dug up and carried to the Mu'seums of London, Berlin, Paris and America, to be deciphered by Western scholars. They toOk a small slab of clay, wrote upon it with a tool, and. then allowed it to dry in the sun. The people of Babylon were a literary people and filled large libraries with these clay books, but, as might have been ex- pected, after so long a period of time and the destruction of the cities by their enemies, the hooks are found only in fragments. - Yet we have a very accurate idea of .the con- tents of the books of ancient Babylon. Happily for us, when the Assyrians gained power they established a hUge library at Nineveh. Assur- bani-pal, one of the Assyrian kings, plundered 'the library of Babylon and— Set an army of scribes at work to copy the writings. The king tells us very often that the gods "had given him broad ears and enlightened his eyes" to see the-value of the old records at Babylon. We or very glad for those broad cars and enlightened eyes, because the copies he caused to .be made have been very well preserved under the desert soil, and we can read admiraole copies of books which were written 2,000 years before the Christian era.</p>
        <p>The Assyritui books were raper different from the Liabylonish 'Ones, They baked them _in oi,enB, and we can see the holes made in them by which the moisture' escaped. And as Bmall tablets were less likely to crack in the ovens than large ones, they made them of very small size. _</p>
        <p>The important thing is that these clay tablets made about 700 years B.C. are exact copies of tablet which Were written 2000 to 2500 8.C., and which contain the wisdom, beliefs, and history of an age which was almost forgotten. And among them are fragments of Several accounts of the Creation and Deluge. TIIE ACCOUNT OF :THE CREATION. - •</p>
        <p>It is just about forty-two years since the first of these was found by Mr. George Smith. It was a .mere fragment, but it raised quite a flutter of excitement and interest. Here is a translation :</p>
        <p>At that time the heavens above named not</p>
        <p>a name, Nor did the earth below record one : Yea, the deep was their first creator, The flood of the sea was she who bore them all. Their waters were gathered in one place and The clouds (?) were not collected, the plants were not grown. At that time the gods had not issued forth, any one of them. By no name were they recorded Then the great gods were made. Lakhmn and Lakhamn issued forth. They grew up ... Next were made the host of heaven and earth, The time was long then . The gods Anu .. • • The host of heaven and earth.</p>
        <p>The account_breaks off here, but another tab- let, the fifth of a series, tells that someone "made the stations of the great gods, even the stars, fixing the places of the principal stars. . . He ordered the year,'setting over it the decans ; yea he established three stars for each .of the twelve months." , Another fragment says: "At that time the gods in their assembly created (living creatures). They made beautiful the mighty . . . . They made living beings to come forth, the cattle of the .field, the beasts of the field, and the creeping thing." What follows cannbt ,be deciphered.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-13_WidnesExaminer_art0225" n="art0225">
        <head>DELUGE STORY.</head>
        <p>THE DELUGE STORY.</p>
        <p>_ The story of the Deluge ,as they told it, ap- pears ,in ever so '.many fragments - of clay writing. So numerous are they that by, com- paring them we can get a fairly complete story. The hero is Xisuthos, and he is told by the gods to build a ship, because the earth is, to be destroyed by water. Into the ship he takes his family, 'cattle, beasts and money. For six days the storm rages, on the seventh it is quiet, and the waters ,begin to subside. • Seven days. after .this he sends out a dove, which finds no'l'esting- place ; then a swallow, which also returns; then 'a. raven, which does 'not return. The ship rests upon a mountain, and Xisuthos offers • a sacrifice to the gods. They smell the savour, and it is good to Ahem. "The gods gathered like flies over the sacrifice. Thereupon the great goddess at her approaCh lighted up the rainbow which the god Anu had created accord- ing to his glory." Many points will be noticed in which this story is similar to the one in the 1300 k of 'Genesis,: but the point of • similarity which is most striking is that which ascribes the cause of the Deluge to the wickedness of mankind.. It is said to have been sent, as a punishment for sin. . ' *</p>
        <p>The Babylonians told also of the building of</p>
        <p>or "olive7coloured," Ham is called Hammu, or "burned,blaek," and Japet is Ippat, " the-white race."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-13_WidnesExaminer_art0226" n="art0226">
        <gap reason="ocrQuality" quantity="2" unit="words">
          <desc>skipped/gap: fewer than 20 non-control characters after cleanup</desc>
        </gap>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-13_WidnesExaminer_art0227" n="art0227">
        <head>DISCOVERY OF SOME ORIGINAL • FRAGMENTS.</head>
        <p>THE DISCOVERY OF SOME ORIGINAL • FRAGMENTS.</p>
        <p>_ . Perhaps we, had better say again that these pieces of clay are all Assyrian copies. • No original record was found with them. To find these originals in Babylon itself has been the fervent wish of ,explorers for a full generation, and the hope was entertained that even older versions might be foimd.</p>
        <p>Those who knONNTNTIi-ai changes are wrought in stories .by the process of time and imagina- tion felt quite sure that if such earlier cOpies did come to light they would prove to be much simpler and more in harmony with the accounts of the Book of Genesis.' Two fragments came to light to confirm this belief. One of them is in the Berlin Museum, and one was purchased by the late Mr. Pier- pont Morgan. Three years ago another was found, and of this we are able to give an engraving.</p>
        <p>In itself the bit. of hard clay is quite insigni- ficant. It is sun-dried and not baked; measures 2:1 inches At its greatest width and 2i,inches at its greatest length, and it is about of an inch thick. It had been covered with writing upon both sides and edges. That on the back is quite broken away, but. some of that on the edge can be read, and the artist who has made our drawing has shown this in-his sketch. The tablet, when entire, was, perhaps, 10 inches by 7 inches. • •</p>
        <p>We describe this fragment in order that some idea may be formed atrie difficulties which are experienced by those Who decipher and piece Lt4gether for us these very old records.</p>
        <p>The following is the best English 'version we can give of the lines. Unfortunately none of them are complete :</p>
        <p>thee . . .</p>
        <p>I will loosen</p>
        <p>. . . . it shall sweep away all men together; • . . life before the deluge cornett; forth; . . as .many as there are, I will bring over- throw, destruction, annihilation. Build a great ship and . .</p>
        <p>total height shall be its structure. • • • . it shall be a houseboat carrying what has been saved of life, with a strong deck cover • • . . which thou shalt make • . the beasts of the field, the birds of heaven . . . instead of a number . . . . . . and the family • • •</p>
        <p>• This is undoubtedly part of the Deluge story, and the date of the writing is fixed by experts at 2109 B,c. The ingenuity of the reader may be exercised to complete the imperfect lines. Assuming our scholars to be correct, we have. here a story which was written before the time of Abraham, and a story, which he must have known quite well. Long centuries afterwards it came 'to the ears of Moses, who, accordin g to Jewish tradition, gathered the narratives in the form in which we have them in the Bible. Without claiming too much for, these old stories, it may be safely said that they prove that stories of the Creation, the Deluge, and the building of the Tower of Babel were common among the ancient peoples of the East, and that in their outlines they agree very closely with the records preserved in the Book of Genesis.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-13_WidnesExaminer_art0228" n="art0228">
        <head>SCRAPS OF ANCIENT MYTH.</head>
        <p>SCRAPS OF ANCIENT MYTH.</p>
        <p>There are many other. old stories written oil the, Babylonish tOlets which would interest and . amuse young 'readers if we bad. space to Copy them. Some legends refer to the duds, and it would be easy to show that the Greeks borrowed them and made them the basis of their. oWn famous myths. Thus Tu was the "'divine storm-bird," who stole thq lightning of Bel, and was punished by the fathOr of the Gods. When we read this we recognise the Creek Prome- theus Who stole the fire ; and the Greek Aphro- dite and Adonis are copies of the Babylonish Istar and Tammuz.</p>
        <p>One curious legend tells how the seven storm- demons once warred against the moon and threatened to, devour it. SWIMS and Istar fled from the lower sky,- and the Moon-god would have been blotted out from heaven had not Bel and Ea sent Merodach in his "glistening armour" to rescue him. Those who will read this carefully will, perhaps, deteet its meaning. It is an ancient attempt to explain an eclipse of the 'Moon. • The 'Chinese tell similar 4ories. When an eclipse of the sun or moon takes place Popular belief has it that a huge dragon swal- lows the heavenly body, from whose grip it is rescued by some friendly „deity. • Just as the Greeks believed that the gods lived on Mount- Olympus, the Babylonians believed ethat they lived on Mount Bowandiz, "the moun- tain of the world," which was supposed to hold up vaults of heaven and all the stars. This belief, coming to -us from the old records, is very interesting to Bible readers, as it explains the words in Isaiah xiv. 13, 14, in Which the King ,of Babylbn is represented as saying in his heart, "I will ascend into heaven, I -will exalt my throne above the*tars of God ; I will sit also ,on the mount of the assembly of the gods in the sides of the north; I will ascend above the heights of the clouds." A. M.</p>
        <p>CHILDREN'S CORNER • k 1. Conducted by 'UNCLE JACK. nx,piovwxy, 0 0 0 0 0 0 0 0 0</p>
        <p>A VERY BIG TASK.</p>
        <p>My dear Children,— You have truly given me a very big task to read through all your letters: I have been hard at it all week, and have not nearly finished getting through them. It looks as if I shall have to spend Sunday over the letters. We have decided to give six prizes, but who shall get the prizes will have to be kept a secret until 'next week, when the results will be de- clared. The work of selecting the winners is difficult, so I have 'phoned to Santa Claus to come and help me, and I have asked other folk to help as well, so • that we may have the best judges possible. Truly you have given all of us .a big job. Now I must not write more, as all my time is required to get through your letters, so you will forgive me if I have not written much this week.</p>
        <p>Your loving "UNCLE JACK."</p>
        <p>NEW MEMBERS. • We welcome this week: 'MONA ARCHER, 11, Heathside, Warringten. EDITH HANKINSON, 7, St. Mary-street, Warring-</p>
        <p>AGNES MCCORMACK, 1, lolley-streeti Warring- ton. Donis OWEN , 23, Forshaw-street, Warrington. ALAN TUOMAS, St. Mary's4Oad-, Penketh. A. E. Ulu, \Vest, View, Greystone-road, Pen- keth.</p>
      </div>
