      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-13_WidnesExaminer_art0239" n="art0239">
        <head>RUNCORN MANUFACTURER DEAD.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN MANUFACTURER DEAD.</p>
        <p>I</p>
        <p>Mr. Duncan Mliechnie, a well-known chemical manufacturer, died on Sunday, night at the residence of his daughter, Heath House, Higher Runcorn. He was a native of Argyll- shire, and born in the year 1831. When a young man he went to Glasgow, and after a few years came to Runcorn, where he held a post in John- son's soda works. Upon leaving the latter he started the o.ld Quay Works, Runcbrn. Later he went to St. Helens, and there began a copper smelting and chemical works on his own account. He took an active part in the muni- cipal work of St. Helens; he was appointed alderman, and was mayor of St. Helens for the year 1887-1888. He was a prominent supporter of the Presbyterian Church, St. Helens.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-13_WidnesExaminer_art0240" n="art0240">
        <head>IT IS REALLY NECESSARY</head>
        <p>IT IS REALLY NECESSARY</p>
        <p>TO TAKE</p>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, DECEM LER 13, 1913.</p>
        <p>nutrli</p>
        <p>WYCLIFFE PS A.</p>
        <p>The- Rev. T. T. James gave an address to this P.S.A. on Sunday on "Lessons from the life of Dr. Livingstone." The speaker reviewed in de- tail the great work accomplished by Livingstone on behalf of the native races of Africa, a work lie began that was now steadily pursued by others. Thera were some who said this mission- ary enterprise was a failure. The People who said that looked too much upon the opening up of new countries from the commercial aspect. Christianity stood against evil, and wherever evil exists there the missionaries must go.</p>
        <p>Mr. B. Farrar, the secretary, presided, and Mr. Yearsley took part in the service. One new member received a hearty welcome. The meeting voted a guinea towards the, ....omen and children suffering through the Dublin strike. This is the second donation from the class.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-13_WidnesExaminer_art0241" n="art0241">
        <head>MEMORIAL SERVICE TO A BOY.</head>
        <p>MEMORIAL SERVICE TO A BOY.</p>
        <p>At St. John's Church on Sunday morning the Rev. W. Reid devoted the children's portion of the service to the memory of Sidney Smith, an old Sunday scholar and regular Sabbath morn- ing worshipper at St. John's, who recently lost his life in Northern Ontario, in attempting to save a girl companion from drowning. The sermonette was based on the story of "Gareth and Lynette," and; in closing, the preacher said that their "first thoughts on reading the news were those of gloom and pain, because a young and promising life had been snatched away pre- maturely, but clearer and more inspiring ones followed, wherein it could be seen that a bright and vigorous life was able to follow Jesus Christ so closely as to, like Him, surrender his life for another, and be found ripe and. ready for God's use in the sphere above."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-13_WidnesExaminer_art0242" n="art0242">
        <head>BOLD-STREET WESLEYAN CHURCH.</head>
        <p>BOLD-STREET WESLEYAN CHURCH.</p>
        <p>The pulpit was occupied on Sunday evening by the. nev. W. A. Beckett, who spoke from the words, "Seek ye first the Kingdom of God and his righteousness." The weight and worth of the injunction rested upon the personality of the Giver of the promise which followed. The in- tention of the Saviour was to give the guidance required in the daily life and to set life's truest aim before man. The warning note was uttered against the many voices which call to distract the attention to material things. Few persons disputed the beauty of the prin- ciples of the Sermon on the Mount, but not so few doubted their practicability in the twentieth century. Their practicability, however, de- pended upon the controlling object in the life. The Kingdom of God and His righteousness flourished in proportion as the human will was brought into line with the Divine will. TO seek the Kingdom of God was to live in accord- ance with the will of God, and this was the greatest objective in life. To seek this world's happiness was to sub- ordinate first things. Without a high guiding principle life was not the success it ought to be, and the first and greatest aim of this life was to seek first God's Kingdom and His right- eousness, and then the secondary things of life would be added unto them.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-13_WidnesExaminer_art0243" n="art0243">
        <head>BOLD-STREET MEN'S CLASS.</head>
        <p>BOLD-STREET MEN'S CLASS.</p>
        <p>The address on Sunday afternoon was given by Mr. James Gauld,,who dealt with the ques- tion of duty to the State. The speaker selected as a basis for his remarks the word, "Render unto Ctesar the things which are CEesar's." Briefly sketching the condition of the Jews in the days of Christ, Mr. Gauld pointed out that the Jews, though a subject race, had many privileges for which to thank their Rdman con- querors. Among these were mentioned a sound currency, protection of life and property, legal equity, and religious liberty. The Pharisees were thus rightly counselled by Christ to pay their tribute as honest men in return for these benefits. As Englishmen, we, too, are indebted to our country for many privileges, which demand a return in service and devotion. As electors, as representatives on local and national governing bodies, as social reformers, and in various other ways, England claims our intellects and our energies. To deny these is to fail in our duty as Christians. Having rendered to Ccesar the things which are Ccesar's, we shall also have rendered to God the things which belong to Him. . - .</p>
        <p>Several members joined in an interesting dis- cussion; after which the essayist replied to the points therein raised.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-13_WidnesExaminer_art0244" n="art0244">
        <head>HOUSING IN ST. HELENS.</head>
        <p>HOUSING IN ST. HELENS.</p>
        <p>Labour Candidate's Views.</p>
        <p>Mr. James Sexton, prospective Labour candi- date, speaking in St. Helens on Saturday, re- ferred to the housing question. They were hear- ing a good deal in St. Helens just now,, he said, about the housing problem„ and it was sug- gested that the local Council should do some- thing. The tenement houses idea had been suggested, and Liverpool had been referred to as an example. He knew something about the tenement system in Liverpool, and, if they re- ferred to the Victoria-square tenements, then he hoped their representatives would vigorously oppose the introduction of the tenement system to St. Helens. The Victoria-square tenement system was the costliest and the most cumber- some and the most dangerous system of housing the working classes that he knew. People should be spread over the land that God had given; then why in the name of health and hygiene and commonsense and physique should they put people up four or five stories high when there was plenty of land to spread them on? The price of land was the crux of the matter in St. Helens, or anywhere else. They had bought land in •Liverpool for housing purposes, and the price was so high that it could not be. used, and had to be sold again, for people to erect warehouses on. They in St. Helens had cheaper land than in Liverpool, and they should build good houses by their own Corporation. They were talking about 'giving the houses out to private contractors, and then buying them again. The past record of jerrybuilding did ' not encourage him to agree with that idea, and he hoped the people of St. Helens would insist on their porporation building and giving the people good houses to live in.</p>
        <p>He noticed that Mr. Rigby Swift had been giving them his political viesVs about Ireland, and supporting the idea that one corner of Ulster ought to have separate treatment for itself. If that was to be so, could not they in St. Helens ask, "What price Smithy-brow and Greenbank?" In Greenbank, particularly, the population was almost exclusively Irish Nationalist, and supposing it said, "We will not have the, ruling of your Town Council. We will have a town council in Greenbank," it would just be as,feasible for them to say that as it was for M. Swift to demand that a corner of Ulster should rule the whole of ,Ireland.</p>
        <p>The Smallpox Germ.</p>
        <p>The germ of .sinallpox, a protozoan so in- finitesinial that it has passed through the minutest filters and escaped the trained gaze of microscopists for decades, has (says "Science Siftings ") been finally discovered by Dr. Walter Fornet, staff physician at the 'Kaiser Wilhelm Academy. Dr. Fernet claims not only to have discovered the germ, but to be able to propa- gate it. This means, we are told, that it will henceforth not be necessary to inoculate a cow or calf with smallpox virus in order to secure lymph for vaccination, and consequently that a pure culture can be secnred. The lymph won by present methods' contains numerous bac- teria, which must be exterminated bqfore it can be used for vaccination. NVhat further Dr. Fornet's discovery may mean cannot yet be told,, but it opens the way for experiments along hitherto unknown lines in the treatment of smallpox.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-13_WidnesExaminer_art0245" n="art0245">
