      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-13_WidnesExaminer_art0246" n="art0246">
        <head>RAIL STRIKE DANGER.</head>
        <p>RAIL STRIKE DANGER.</p>
        <p>Trouble on the Cheshire Lines Comes to a Crisis.</p>
        <p>MEN MAY BE OUT ON SUNDAY</p>
        <p>By "A WORKER."</p>
        <p>Is the industrial storm which has rumbled over the local railway world about to burst upon Warrington in its full fury? . For some weeks now Warrington has been the centre of a remarkable dispute in connection with the railways worked by the Cheshire Lines Com- mittee; a group made up of three of the wealthi- est concerns in the railway world. The position, which has already been fully explained in "The Examiner," is that the platelayers and permanent way men generally have sought to improve their conditions, but have been fought at every point by the three wealthy companies. The men, who are responsible for the track which plays a more important part in the safe- carriage of human beings than anything else connected with a railway system, are paid a miserable wage. They earn on an average about 225. per week for long hours, every mipute of which surrounds them with the danger' of per- sonal injury, and even loss of life. They have to work in all weathers, without a tittle of the shelter provided in the signal box, on the engine cab, or in the guard's van, and yet, while exposed to all the elements and to grave per- sonal risk, and while absolutely essential to the working of the system, they are tke worst paid in the railway service and are worse off than many navvies, who have comparatively few risks to run.</p>
        <p>What the Men Want. The men seek nothing unreasonable. They ask for a wage of about 245. per week, and with the great increase in the cost of living they are not asking much. Not long ago the railway directors, gentlemen who draw .k5OO and up- wards a year for a little bit of work once a month, alleged that they could not run the railways at the then existing rates. They increased fares, excursion, contract and even ordinary ticket rates were advanced wholesale, the rates for the carriage of goods were in- creased: ay, and they even charge a penny to get on their precious railway platforms! This e was done to afford a living dividend for the wealthy shareholders. What is sauce for the goose is believed to be sauce for the gander. The shareholder goose has got the bottle of .sauce. but now the platelayer gander wants Just a little drop the sauce is, stubbornly refused. _</p>
        <p>Remarkable Patience. The platelayers on the Cheshire Lines have shown remarkable patience. When an advance was refused—an advance granted on other sys- tems—they decided not to perform special duties which for years had been heaped ,upon them, making their hours, especially in the time of fog, almost unbearable. In conse- quence of the refusal to do spectal alit, sixteen members of the union have been suspended. Several times the men have asked the Com- mittee to Meet them, so that the griqvanees might be made known, and the honest and reasonable request has been refused. The patience of the men has now reached breaking point, and unless the Committee climb down this week-end will certainly witness a strike amongst the permanent %yak men all over the Cheshire Lines.</p>
        <p>Public Sympathy. One feature of the dispute has been the amount of public sympathy with the men. It is not so long ago sine() the directors of railway ' companies could always command the public Opinion, but since August, 1911, there has been a marked change. Since then the public have realised how they have been fleeced by the com- panies. It is a well-known fact that after the 1911 strike one company boasted of their gener- osity to their employes, whom they conceded £70,000 in the shape of is. per week increases. Immediately after there was a rise in fares, and the returns showed that out of the increase in excursion tickets alone they had received £150,000 more per annum. If they gave £70,000 to their men, where did the other £70,000 go which came Out of the public's pocket in order "to give the railway employes a living wage"? The share- holders seem to have had the best of the deal, and, knowing that, the public do not like it. The Position Serious.</p>
        <p>' The position on the -Cheshire Lines is particu- larly serious, and, unless the Committee change their course of action there is a grave danger that the whole of the North of England will be Plunged into a great- railway strike, which would paralyse the Christmas trade and traffic A momentous meeting of the men will be held this week-end, and, should a strike occur, other companies' employes will refuse to handle goods diverted from the Cheshire Lines, and such an incident, even on a larger scale, may be wit- nessed like that which occurred in Birmingham and Liverpool in September. Surely the Com- mittee are not so superior as to be as unap- proachable as the Shah of Persia, or some other exclusive Eastern potentate. A conference would straighten matters out, and the Com- mittee should hold such a conference. They have a duty to perform to the public from whom they earn their divideads, and that is a duty to avoid a strike, at all costs. It can be avoided by giving the long harassed permanent way men a living wage.. The Postal Trouble.</p>
        <p>Personally I hardly think, as I have said all along, there will be a postal strike this Christ- mas, but a side issue has been raised in con- nection with the dispute, which is worthy of more than passing notice. It is reported that the Territorials are to be called out to act as " blackleg " postmen and telegraphists in the case of it strike, and that by their military oath they, will be compelled to become " knobsticks." Thus, if the story is true, trade unionists will be compelled to break up the cause of trade unionism. Ido not think, however, a respon- sible Minister would ever put- such a scheme into operation, but the threat has a very pointed bearing upon the present agitation for com- pulsory military service, and it shows in the main why trade unionists are opposed to such a scheme. They know how the conscripts would be exploited to break up their own class—the workers.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-13_WidnesExaminer_art0247" n="art0247">
        <head>RASH FROM HEAD TO FEET.</head>
        <p>RASH FROM HEAD TO FEET.</p>
        <p>9, King Pl., King St., Wednesbury, Staffs, Eng.—" Six weeks after my baby was born she had a rash on her head. She used to rub her head in her sleep and one day I could see it had been running water. I looked at it well, and I could see a lot of little pimples all over it. It 'started to spread. She was covered from bead to foot. I sent to the chemist's for a tablet of CuticUra Soap and a box of Cuticura Ointment. I used them till she was well, and I only had three bores of Cuticura Ointment with the Cuti- cura Soap. "Then later on an itchiness came on my fingers. When I rubbed them they burst and the water would run down my fingers. Wher- ever the water went more pimples would come. I rubbed them itill I rubbed all my finger nails off. I was very badly off. I had given up' hopes when I saw the advertisement of Cuticura Soap and Ointment, and I wrote for a sample and I used them and they gave me relief, so I went to the chemist's for the Cuticura .Soap and a box of Cuticura Ointment, and I used that, and I could see a new skin- coming over my fingers and my nails. I kept using the Cuticura Soap and Ointment and now lam cured." (Signed) Mrs. E. Beardmore, Jan. 31. • Cuticura Soap and Ointment are sold by chemists everywhere. A sample of each with 32-p. book post free from nearest depot. Address: Newbery, 27, Charter- house-souare, London; R. Towns and Co., Syd- ney, N.S.W.: Lennon. Ltd., Cape Town; Muller, Maclean and Co., Calcutta • and Bombay; or "Cuticura," Boston, U.S.A. Men who shave and shampoo with Cuticura Soap will find it best for skin and scalp.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-13_WidnesExaminer_art0248" n="art0248">
        <head>WARRINGTON BREWERS NEW HOTELS.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON BREWERS NEW HOTELS.</p>
        <p>At the transfer sessions of St. Helens sanction was given for the opening of the two new hotels at Thatto Heath, .which have been built by Messrs. Greenall, Whitley and Co., Warrington, at a cost of £lO,OOO.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-13_WidnesExaminer_art0249" n="art0249">
        <head>CHILD MORTALITY.</head>
        <p>CHILD MORTALITY.</p>
        <p>Lancashire is the Black Spot for</p>
        <p>Child Destruction.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-13_WidnesExaminer_art0250" n="art0250">
        <head>OLD TOWNS AND NEW.</head>
        <p>OLD TOWNS AND NEW.</p>
        <p>In iew .of the Conference on Children under School Age, held at the Town Hall on Tuesday evening, It may be interesting to take note of the problem of child mortality. As is well known to social students, Lancashire is the black spot in the country so far as child Mortality is concerned. There are some special reasons for this, but first of all here is a table showing exactly what the death-rates are in the towns of Lancashire, with a population of 50,000 or more. The figures deal with the years 1907 to 1910. A period of four years obviously is a fairer index of conditions than a single year, when an epidemic may create a false im- pression as to what is normal. , The first two columns of figures represent (a) the number of deaths of children between 1 and 5 years per 1,000 infants surviving the first year; (b) the number of. deaths of children within 12 months of birth per 1,000 births. • A. B. C. Name Death-rate Death-rate Birth-rate of town. 1-5, 0-1. in 1911. St. Helens 110.9 136.5 33.2 Salford 107:1 141.7 27.2 Liverpool 104.6 141.5 30.2 . Wigan 101.0 155.3 27.3 Bootle 95.7 127.4 29.9 Warrington 93.5 124.6 28.3 Burnley 93.4 171.4 23.3 Manchester 93.1 141.1 26.2 Oldham 91.1 , 137.8 23.7 Stockport 85.5 150.0 23.4 Preston 82.1 . 152.0 23.3 Blackburn 81.7 141.6 21.5 Bolton 78.6 134.0 - 22.8 Rochdale 76.4 124.4 20.7 Bury 68.9 129.9 20.6 Barrow 65.0 108.5 26.6 Blackpool 55.6 117.7 16.3 Southport 45.1 102.0 15.2</p>
        <p>If the towns were set out in the order in which they appear for deaths under 1 year, they would follow thus: Burnley, Wigan, Preston, Stock- port, Salford, Blackburn, Liverpool, Manches- ter, Oldham; St. Helens, Bolton, Bury, Bootle, Warrington, Rochdale, Blackpool, Barrow and Southport. Sometimes the position is strikingly reversed in relation to the two columns. Of course, certain towns cannot fairly be used for comparative purposes. Seaside resorts like Blackpool and Southport are not exactly self- contained towns. They naturally attract a large residential class, and no industries are encouraged except that of catering for visitors. The average death-rate in towns having a population exceeding 50,000 in England and 'Wales is 66.1 per 1,000 for the years 1 to 5, and 117.1 per 1,000-under 1 year. The correspond- ing rates for the 18 large Lancashire towns are 85.0 and 135.4. These figures are again for the years 1907-10.</p>
        <p>Towns Where Women Work. It is never very safe to generalise about sta- tistics, but it seems clear that the cotton towns and other towns where adult women are indus- trially employed, have a specially bad record for infantile deaths. The employment of mothers in the mills makes breast feeding a difficulty, and it is well known that the bottle- fed baby runs bigger risks than the baby fed in the natural way. The cotton towns have from 60 to 90 per...cent. of their women occupied between the "ages of 15 and 35. Warrington compares very well with other Lancashire towns for infantile deaths, and this is probably largely due to the fact that so many of its babies are breast fed. It does not stand so favourably, however, for deaths between, the ages of one and five years. This fact has been fully recog- nised by the local Health Committee, which is to be heartily congratulated upon its recent de- cision. This is, that a Lady Inspector shall devote her whole time to visit children over 12 months old until they reach school age. In due course it will be possible to discover more exactly why the death-rate is so high, but even now there are some factors which can be con- sidered. Probably unsuitable feeding has some- thing to do with it, and if so, every effort should be made to spread knowledge as to how little children ought to be fed, especially after wean- ing. •</p>
        <p>Large Families and Small Wages. One factor ought not to be lost sight of in the South-West corner of Lancashire, viz., the Irish immigration. Naturally the influx from Ireland tends to be the greatest at the chief port— °Liverpool (with Bootle), and in the towns near- est at hand, where unskilled labourer's are in demand. St. Helens, Wigan and , Warrington get a large Proportion of Irish, as compared with the towns, say, of North-East Lancashire. A glance at the third,column showing the birth- rates for 1911 wjll indicate how far above the average are towns just mentioned. It is cer- tainly true that there is always to be found a high birth-rate among the Irish, and, as most people are aware, the risks of a high child death-rate are great where the birth-rate is high. But as most Irish mothers breast-feed their children, the risks increase after weaning. A very large proportion of the Irish population is, engaged in unskilled, and therefore badly- paid, work. The situation created is this: Large families on low wages. It is safe to say that in household economy, food receives con- sideration before housing, so that the tendency of the Wage-earners with a small income and many children is to seek the cheapest possible house. Everyone knows the disastrous effects of bad housing upon health. Epidemics soon play havoc in close quarters, and the risks of infectiorl are, of course, greater in large thah in small houses. Where, as in the case of scarlet fever, isOlatiog is promptly resorted to, these risks are much reduced. But it is quite understandable that measles should ,work 'great havoc where children are all mixed up together. In Warrington \2O per cent. of the deaths of children between 1 and 5 are due to measles.</p>
        <p>Preventable 'Wastage.</p>
        <p>.An old town .is more liable to a high death- rate than a new town, for many reasons. If the bulk of the house property is modern it is natural to expect that there will be some ad. vantages over dilapidated property. Modern sanitation is also more likely to be in evidence, e.g., Barrow 'is an industrial town not much smaller than Warrington. But Barrow has the advantage of a water-carriage system, whereas Warrington enjoys (if that is the right word, the more primitive pail-closet system. In this connection the experience of Leicester is wort 4 of study. The introduction•of the water- carriage system resulted in that town in an im- provement in the statistics of infantile mortality. Only Lancashire towns have been referred to, because comparisons are • best made where somewhat similar conditions prevail. It is not much use, e.g., to compare industrial towns with some of the places that are classed as towns in official publications. Hornsey, Ealing and Wallasey are rally suburbs, with picked populations, while places like Bath, Hastings, and Eastbourne are not in any sense represen- tative towns. It may be said, however, that a comparison of the big towns of Lancashire with those of Yorkshire would not be altogether flattering to the first-named county, especially in respect of the 1-5 mortality rates. Only a fringe of a big subject has here been touched, but it will be useful if any are im- pelled to search further into the facts, and to seek for remedies that shall make for life.</p>
        <p>The present preventable wastage would grieve the heart of any intelligent stock-breeder. W. J. IL</p>
