      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-13_WidnesExaminer_art0252" n="art0252">
        <head>Rev. H. Ogden Prefers Voluntary Effort</head>
        <p>Rev. H. Ogden Prefers Voluntary Effort</p>
        <p>To Endowment Aid.</p>
        <p>GOLBORNE-STREET SCHOOLS.</p>
        <p>During the last few years the school of the Golborne-street Baptists has undergone an enormous change, and a greater improvement was concluded on Saturday afternoon, when the newly-decorated premises were publicly declared open by Mr. J. C. Holland, a prominent Wesleyan whose church enthusiasm is largely centred round that at Bold-street. The Pastor, the Rev. H. Ogden presided at the opening ceremony.</p>
        <p>Without State Aid.</p>
        <p>Mr. OGDK.N said that he ought to first explain that Mr. E. England very kindly consented to preside at that function, but something of a very urgent nature had interfered with his being .present. The speaker remarked how very grateful he was to Mr. Holland, who had con- sented to open the premises. He thought that so far as the Free Church life was concerned, Mr. Holland was one of those with whom he had been most intimate since he (Mr. Ogden) came to Warrington. He was struck that afternoon by the 'contrasts revealed that day. When he came to preach there for the first time, some five years ago, nobody ventured to suggest that within those short five years there would be such beautiful premises as those in which they were gathered. They as Baptists had always been ready, as a matter of principle, to shoulder. their own bur- dens and responsibilities for the erection of their Sunday school premises, and they had never been willing at any period of their-history to accept help from .the State, .because they, as a denomination and. as Free Churchmen, be- lieved that the State should have no voice, power or controlling agency in the work and the doings of the Free Church life. Of course, that meant that special burdens fell upon the rank and file of their church, and the money required to be raised entailed a great amount of personal sacrifice, which made them take more interest in their church than they would do if the money came to them through an endowment. He also believed that for the Sunday school they should have the very best and most arW- tic of premises, for the children, with adorn- ments, artistic arrangements and modern methods of teaching, must naturally imbibe some of the taste, and, it meant•an elevation also in the home life. They had arrived at the day when the individual churches would have to do their own religious teaching, and he viewed with great gratitude the gradual extinction of the non-provided school. Nothing would give him greater gratification than the day when they should know that their non-provided schools were absolutely abolished. (Applause.) :</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-13_WidnesExaminer_art0253" n="art0253">
        <head>True and Loyal Citizens.</head>
        <p>True and Loyal Citizens.</p>
        <p>Mr. HOLLAND said that some months ago he made a tour of inspection in company with their minister, and he was delighted to find they had such a comfortable place in which to worship. He was glad to be present to support a work which appealed to him very strongly, and with which he had been closely connected for the greater part of his life. It was a work lie could recommend to anybody who wanted to serve the Master.</p>
        <p>If the knowledge of Sunday school work went home to the rising.generation he felt sure they would turn out true and loyal citizens. Sunday school .teaching demanded from them their best talents; he believed there was nothing more noble than to be a Sunda3P school teacher. Perhaps things would not always run smoothly in their new school, but if the teachers and young men and women would be loyal to their faith, they would grow up to be good citizens and pillars of their church. "I hope the services wilrbe well attended," said Mr. Holland, in concluding, "and that these who have attended for ninny years will remain and be loyal to it. I have much pleas- ure in declaring• the building open for worship and Sunday school teaching." (Applause.) Mr. MICHAEL proposed a vote of thanks to Mr. Holland, and, in seconding, Mr. A. STILL said</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-13_WidnesExaminer_art0254" n="art0254">
