        <head>+0 4,4 wt. yy,„,....„ • • • eeV4 tOiNFA, itivt• mkri, 44463‘rv1s AgiPlAiew. 104,1,v ; trt., 4.0 41 TAM,' v 4?- k*.'t i‘t kiTz.o Tit 6</head>
        <p>+0 4,4 wt. yy,„,....„ • • • eeV4 tOiNFA, itivt• mkri, 44463‘rv1s AgiPlAiew. 104,1,v ; trt., 4.0 41 TAM,' v 4?- k*.'t i‘t kiTz.o Tit 6</p>
        <p>HOCKEY.</p>
        <p>STOCKTON HEATH v. STOCKPORT.</p>
        <p>Stockton Heath were at home to Stockport on Saturday, and after a hard, keen game, were defeated by two goals to one. Last season Stockton Heath won by three goals to one. For about the .first fifteen minutes Stockport had most of the play and were continually in the home half; the home defence, however, played up strongly and held out. Play then trecame more even until about ten minutes from the interval, when Stockton Heath pressed strongly. Baxter, stopping a hard clearance from the goalkeeper, opened the scoring for Stockton Heath. Half-time: Stockton Heath, one goal; Stockport, nil. After 20 minutes, Goodwin scored for Stock- port, and just before time Mather scored a second. The defence on both sides played a -fine game throughout, both goalkeepers bring- ing off several fine saves. Chorlton are the visitors to the Cricket Ground to-day.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-13_WidnesExaminer_art0257" n="art0257">
        <head>LATCHFORD WIN.</head>
        <p>LATCHFORD WIN.</p>
        <p>The visit of the Manchester Y.M.C.A. Hockey Club to Latchford did not produce a very good game. The style of game played by the Y.M.C.A. was quite different to that usually associated with Latchford. It wasn't often that the Y.M.C.A. attempted to dribble—the ball came to them anl they knocked, it' somewhere else. They are a rare bustling lot of players, and have evidently. bustled4o some purpose this season, for previous to Saturday they had only once been defeated. The score Of 4-1 in Latch- ford's favour about represents the merits of the two teams. Knight, Isherwood and Bruhl were the best of the Latchford side. The second eleven won by -7-1, their best per- formance, at Sale Cricket Ground. The visit- ors had the assistance of Minards at inside left, and he opened the scoring for them. Sale equalised,' but Minards gave Latchford a 2-1 lead for half-time. Latchford forwards and halves in the last twenty minutes came with a rush, gild five goals were actually scored by Minards and Furness; quite a surprising change.</p>
        <p>The first eleven play their return match with Ashley at Hale this afternoon, and the team is: J. Bruhl; W. Fletcher and C. Billington ; F. W. Isherwood, J. B. Wilkinson and A. Knight; H. Fletcher, R. A. Dale, J. E. Pearson, W. H. Minards and H. R. Colton.-,' Umpire, F. W. Grounds. Train from Arpley Station 1.16 p.m. The second eleven v. Ashley second, at home, is: G. Mynekyme ; W. T. Eccles and J. Albin- son ; A. E. Bradshaw, F. C. Newbold and T. Banks; W. Starkey, J. Whitfield, A. B. Starkey, A. G. Marsden and C. Newbold. Umpire, 11. Eccles. Bully-off 2.30 P.m.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-13_WidnesExaminer_art0258" n="art0258">
        <head>ANGLING.</head>
        <p>ANGLING.</p>
        <p>Warrington Workingmen's</p>
        <p>Association.</p>
        <p>N.A. CENTRE.</p>
        <p>Not a single member paid the Gowy a visit last week-end, and angling is practically at a standstill; Many are giving, it a rest until the Christmas holidays, when there will be a large muster at Mickle Trafford. Down South better results were obtained than for some weeks, but then these waters are out of reach of our locals. The waters near home have given such poor re- sults .as to almost exhaust: the patience of anglers. Although the season has been poor generally, some nice weights are carrying off the sets this year, with the exception of the. carial sets, and these are the worst we have ever recorded. •</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-13_WidnesExaminer_art0259" n="art0259">
        <head>1913 SPECIMEN WINNERS.</head>
        <p>1913 SPECIMEN WINNERS.</p>
        <p>Bream: 1, W. Bennett, 31b. 9oz. 12dr.; 2, W. Bennett, 31b. soz. 11dr. Chub: 1, 11. Mores, 11b. 15oz. 7dr.; 2, 11. Moores, 11b. 15oz. Trout: 1, H. Mounfield, 21b. 30z.; 2, 11. Brit- tain, 11b. loz. Bdr. Perch: 1, M. Manion, 11b. 10oz. 7dr.; 2, J. San- son, 11b. 10oz. 7dr. Roach: 1, H. Moores, 11b. 7oz. 15dr.; 2, E. Hill, 11b. 6oz. 7dr. Dace: 1, F. Trigger, 12oz. 2dr. ; 2, A. Bradley, lloz. 12dr. Gudgeon: 1, T. Singleton, loz. lldr.; 2, R. Clare, loz. 7dr. Daddy Butte: 1, W. Sanson. Pike: 1, 11. Mounfield, 141 b.; 2, J. Sanson, 31b. Boz. Roach (Norton) : 1, 3. Green, 7oz. 15dr.; 2, T. Dixon,. 7oz. sdr. Gudgeon (Norton) : 1, T. Dixon, loz. sdr.; 2, E. Wales, lldr. Eel (Norton) : 1, C. Morton. All the specials are won with the exception of the dace special from the Gowy. The notice of the amalgamated meeting, along with the agenda, will appear next week. A.S.</p>
        <p>COMING MATCH IN AID OF •MRS. ARMITAGE.</p>
        <p>We understand that many tickets have been sold for the charity match which is to be played next Monday evening in the well-appointed room at Rylands' Workmeh s Recreation Club, Gorsey-lane, in aid of the widow of the late Mr. J. Armitage. The contestants will be Mr. T. Horrabin's team v. Rylands'. The following players have been selected to represent Mr. Hor- rabin's team f Mr. T. Horrabin (capt.), Mr. H. Bennett, Mr. H. Hughes, Mr. H. Parkes and Mr. G. Marshall, Senior Conservatives; Mr. W. Ray and Mr. J. Jolley, Junior Conservatives; Mr. E. V. Walker and M. M. Cunningliam, Liberal Club; Mr. P: Quinn and Mr. T. Laws, -Widnes; and Mr. E. Walker, Earlestown. RYLANDS' 11. v. CROSFIELD'S it..</p>
        <p>This match took place in the Pavilion, Gorsey- lane, on Wednesday; and resulted in a win for the home team by 106 points. The handicaps were: Rylands', 40; Crosfield's, 20. Scores, Rylands' names first: A. Bramliall 100, 11. Sher- ratt 42; F. • Wadsworth 100, C. Cooper '6l; J. linsworth 84; C. Millington 100; G. Warburton 98, S. Littlewood 100; T. Humphreys 58, W. Whatmough 100; W. IL Phillips 100, D. Hughes 51. Rylands' 580, Crosfield's 474. CONSERVATIVE WARDS COMPETITION.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-13_WidnesExaminer_art0260" n="art0260">
        <head>Semi-Final.</head>
        <p>Semi-Final.</p>
        <p>The semi-final game between Town Hall No. 2 and Howley No. 1 took place on Thursday. Scores, Town Hall names first: R. Straw 100, H. Mather 98; T. Horrabin 100, J. Bluck 62; T. Weir 100, R. Burrows 65; W. Breward 67, H. Broadhurst 100; W. Ford 100, P. Gleeson 98. Town Hall 467, Howley 424. Majority for Town Hall, 43.</p>
        <p>SENIOR AND JUNIOR CONSERVATIVES MEET.</p>
        <p>At the Sankey-street club On Thursday. The Seniors finished with a majority of 91. Scores, Seniors' names first: J. H. Allen 100, A. Barker (capt.) 95; F. Holland 100, L. Hoekenhall 13; A. W. Allman 100, G. Spencer 71; IL 'C. Simpson (capt.) 94, J. Hayes 100; R. F. Andertbn 59, W. T.: Clark 100; D. Cotterill 100, F. Gardner 83. Seniors 553, Juniors 462.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-13_WidnesExaminer_art0261" n="art0261">
        <head>ST. MARY'S JUNIORS v. OUR LADY'S.</head>
        <p>ST. MARY'S JUNIORS v. OUR LADY'S.</p>
        <p>Played at St. Mary's. Scores, St. Mary's names first: W. Mannion 75, G. Ashurst 65; F. Mannion 75, L. Knight 67; R. Whelan 68, L. Morris 75; W: Malone 40, J. Knight 75; E. Kelly 75, R. Ryan 44; W. Houghton 75, J., Knight 11. St. Mary's 408, Our Lady's 337. Majority for St. Mary's, 71. g</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-13_WidnesExaminer_art0262" n="art0262">
        <p>Warrington Experience Hard Luck hi Their</p>
        <p>Match With Salford.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-13_WidnesExaminer_art0263" n="art0263">
        <head>MATCH MARKED WITH PENALTIES.</head>
        <p>A MATCH MARKED WITH PENALTIES.</p>
        <p>Warrington did not do co w. 141 at Weaste as iuost .of the -W if:del-spool supporters hoped. -4alford did not win by ,tny merit, and the try which put them ahead almost at the close was a very questionable one. It is doubtful if it was a try at all, but luck gave it to Salford, and thus by 1 goal 1 try (5 points) to 1 goal (2 points) Salford were lucky enough to run out winners. All the critics are agreed that their play did not entitle them to a victory—a draw would more adequately have represented the nature of the game. • The Wilderspool 'men got their score in the same old way—a penalty kick. It is time we saw some tries scored, the public are becoming tired of the continuous series of penalty goals. What is wrong with Warrington- that we can- not get tries? It is now 'a considerable time since we have seen a, try notched by the War- Iringtonians, and we are anxiously awaiting one. -Will we have the wish gratified to-day, when )harrow once more 'occupies the Wilderspool pitch? A Scrappy Game. •</p>
        <p>The match at Weaste was monotonous in its 4scrappiness. The Warrington forwards worked .hard, 'but not so effectively as on the previous •Saturday, and the threequarters rarely gave a .great display, although it is mime consolation to think that they were better all rouna than 'the Salford quartet. Salford were the first to score, and this came .from a penalty. Shortly after Bradshaw was 'obstructed, and from the resultant kick Jolley .made the score level, and so the teams turned at half-time. The second half was an even less interesting repetition Of the first portion, and •luring the latter part of the game the battle was fought out in semi-darkness, and this favoured Salford's tactics. It was while surrounded by the descending .gloom of night that Salford scored the winning —and very questionable—try. A kick and a rush set the home forwards scrambling on the Warrington line. A scrummage followed, and .May was supposed to have touched down, but in the dark and the scramble the referee could not see. -1-1 e consulted with alinesman and a 'ttry was awarded. It was extremely doubtful if 41 try had been scored.</p>
        <p>By 46 Scrum."</p>
        <p>of full-back play, and the. same •might also be said of Bevan.</p>
        <p>For Warrington tries were scored by Fearnley (three), C. Brown (one) and ,Waywell (two). Critchley was successful in improving one of these, and Harmer also piloted the ball between the uprights. • The scorers for Salford were Wild and Woods.</p>
        <p>BARROW ONCE AGAIN. Will Warrington Win? . Barrow pay their second visit to Wilderspool to-day, their first being in connection with the cup tie, when Warrington were beaten by 15 points to 8. This, result should .be reversed. Barrow, however, must not be under-rated. They have accomplished much good work since their October visit, and it behoves the Warring- ton men to put their best feet forward if they are to have revenge. Two changes have been made in the War- rington team since the visit to. Salford. H. Davis has been substituted by Tranter as part- ner to Ruddick at threequarter, and Fearnley has taken Tranter's place amongst the forwards. The sides will be : WARRINGTON. Jolley. Bradshaw, Dickenson, .Tranter, Ruddick. Nicholas, Young. Chesters, Cox, Skelhorne, Thomas, Clare, Fearnley.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON "A" AT PEMBERTON.</p>
        <p>The reserves go to Pemberton, where they qbould earn another two points. The side will be:—</p>
        <p>J. Brown; Baker, Shaw, H. Davis, C. Brown; A. Brown, Dainteth ; Harmer, Critchley, Green, Naylor, Boardman, Bevan.</p>
        <p>ST. HELENS AND DISTRICT JUNIOR LEAGUE. Rylands' Reserve v. Westfield_ Amateurs.</p>
        <p>This match was played at St. Helens, before a fair attendance. Potter kicked off and Rylands' were soon attacking through Horrocks and Mannion. From a quick pass ,by •Horrocks, Scarisbriek scored, the goal-kick failing. Ry- lands' were the nippier lot, and eventually won by 1 goal 3 tries (11 points) to Westfield 1 goal (2 points).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-13_WidnesExaminer_art0264" n="art0264">
        <head>Unsatisfactory Ending.</head>
        <p>Unsatisfactory Ending.</p>
        <p>This was a most unsatisfactory ending to a 'thoroughly unsatisfactory game. • The referee .of course, was well within his right in award- ing a try, even if it had not been scored, but in similar cases other referees have ruled against a score. Salford had such an experience the Previous - Saturday, when, under identical cir- 'clUnstances, the referee refused, an appeal - fur 'a try on the ground that he did not see it. Aad as..Mr..Jones did not .see this incident it would have been more satisfactory had he inlet! out the claim for a try. It was a game .which did not lend itself much to criticism, :unless to say that it was of a most disapiminting character. . The Warrington for- wards were the better pack. Yet they did not work with the unison •we saw against Leeds. The Warrington half-backs were at Sixes and :leverks. There was not the co-operation we anticipated from the two Welshmen' playing a similar type of game. It was the same old story about the threequarters again. The efforts of two fast wing - men were made negative by the lack of backing fro lia the centres. It was most palpable that the centre threequarter Position s still the weak spot.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-13_WidnesExaminer_art0265" n="art0265">
        <head>Bradshaw Shows Improvement</head>
        <p>Bradshaw Shows Improvement</p>
        <p>There is still hope, .however, of betterment, 'tnd if the committee persists with the team as it stands we may see them capable of winning taatches away, as well as at home. olley did tot .play .quite up to past form, but generally he was as sure ag•the Salford custodian.• Ruddick again was the most co/aspic:nom man in the Warrington threequarter line, and he ntade full use of his opportunities, but %-as.not supported as he might have een. Dickenson was more in evidence in connection with the defensive work than in the !:.ggressive part of the game.. Davis tackled well and, did not al- ways help Bradshaw as the totter man reqcired. The captain showed a sligat improvement in form, and some of his runs wire clever. Once lie had the hardest of luck in not Ating a try. The improvement in Bradshaw's play •A as a welcome feature in the game, and it is main- tained he should _soon be back to the ferm shoFn earlier in the season. Both sets of half-backs were ver3-sdisappeint- trig. They seemed unable to open up runs. The greasy ball, however, may have accounted for this. Nicholas was fairly sure at times, while nt others he seemed quite unable to do the right thing. Young was not equal to the form. he displayed • against .Leeds. Chesters was scarcely up to County form in the forwards. Clare was again more in evidence With his speedy dashes. Tranter and •Skelhorne :were hard workers.</p>
        <p>THE RESERVES STILL WINNING! Salford "A " Outplayed at Wilderspool</p>
        <p>By " Lakelander."</p>
        <p>The Combination team still continue in a winning vein, and on Saturday beat the Reserve side from the Royal Borough to the tune of 2 goals 6' tries (22 points) to 2 tries (6 points).</p>
        <p>The weather' conditions were wretched, and, lts a consequence, the ground was somewhat heavy and thk sphere slippery and very difficult to handle. The first half was productive of 6orne Clever football by both sides, but during the second portion the play fizzled out into a eniewhat dull, uninteresting game. After the locals had got a lead of 16 points they took ;_l,flatters fairly, easy, and only crossed the line 'twice in the second half, the Salford men also oeing successful in breaking through the War- .!'ington defence on .two occasions. Warrington 'lad a strong side out; and were a much. nippier lot than -their rivals. The threequarters 'showed some very smart combination, and the two wings—Waywell and Baker,—used • tlieir I'Peed to great advantage. Shaw was a capital "entre, and C. Brown also did very well, al- 'though once or twice he might have improved !natters if he had passed out to Waywell, when he latter was well placed.</p>
        <p>, C. Brown and Daintith made an ideal pair the luilf-back position, and were too smart pr the Salford couple, though the latter played • no means a had game', and very often ,had aintith ",spotted.''</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-13_WidnesExaminer_art0266" n="art0266">
        <head>Bustling Forwards.</head>
        <p>Bustling Forwards.</p>
        <p>The forwards again played a bustling game, individually and collectively outclassed the alford sextet. TV ground was too slippery .10 admit of players being much criticised, but repeatedly saw Fearnley in the picture, and B Was successful in tieing the "hat trick."</p>
        <p>Brown, as usual, gave a good expositiou</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-13_WidnesExaminer_art0267" n="art0267">
        <head>Players' Christmas Benefit.</head>
        <p>Players' Christmas Benefit.</p>
        <p>Ilylands' players take their benefit match on Christmas morning through the kindness of, the Works Council. An attractive match has been arranged with ,St. Helens Recreation on the ground at Gorsey-lane.</p>
        <p>Lancashire Junior Cup. Semi-Final.</p>
        <p>Rylands' Recs. meet Spring View (Wigan) at Gorsey-lane to-day in the semi-final round of the cup with the following team: F. Horrocks; J. Griffiths, S. Carter, A. Horrocks, P. Morris; G. Warren, J. Gallop; A. Stretford (capt.), J. Whittaker, 11. Morris, J. Brandwood, D. Slater, F. Mason.</p>
        <p>PICKS OUT OF THE SCRUM.</p>
        <p>Pithy Pars About -the N.U. Games.</p>
        <p>It was sad .form at Salford. Warrington should never have been beaten. The try in the dark did it. This was a most questionable score. It is very doubtful if May Ever crossed the line at all. The referee, however, gave the points, And there was no chance to appeal. This gave Salford a material Lift up in the League table. They are amongst the first four. Their play does not justify it.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-13_WidnesExaminer_art0268" n="art0268">
        <head>oc cc</head>
        <p>oc cc</p>
        <p>Ruddick was the smartest threequarter Out of the eight on the Weaste pitch. He did some gooil things, hut He still wants more backing. Bradshaw is returning to form. This is welcome news indeed. He had hard lines in one run. While on the verge of scoring He lost the ball, and it was Truly very hard on the captain. Davis defended well, but Tie .was very slow in the aggressive work.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-13_WidnesExaminer_art0269" n="art0269">
        <head>e3cceee</head>
        <p>e3cceee</p>
        <p>Dickenson is a demon to tackle. He laid several Salford men low, And they felt "sore" about it. If he had more speed he and Ruddick would be difficult to stop. What was wrong with .Young? He did not do so well as he Did against Leeds the previous week. The two Welshmen did not work WWI the harmony we all anticipated. It is time they got to understand Each other better than they do.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-13_WidnesExaminer_art0270" n="art0270">
        <head>ASSOCIATION TEAMS.</head>
        <p>ASSOCIATION TEAMS.</p>
        <p>Orford Athletic play Excelsior "A" with : .Jennings; Dooley and Timrains;. Mannion, Tranter and Fitzgerald; Monks, Fallon, Evans, Newton and Stanton.</p>
        <p>Emmanuel Old Boys v. Widnes' Albion.— Owing to the Albion having successfully lodged a..protest, the Old Boys have to journey to Wid- nes for the replayed tie. Team: Knight; Har- rison and Eccles; Warham, Hatton and J. Ash- ton; Wainwright, Carter, Isherwood; Caldwell and Singleton. Emmanuel Old Boys' Reserves v. Elmwood' avenue Congregational, at Fairfield. Team: Trainor; Brimelow and Day; Robinson, Carroll and Balshaw; Green, Edelston, Hankinson. -Rigby and Jones.</p>
        <p>. _ Orford St. John's play Warrington Excelsior. it Long-lane, with: Forber; Smith and G. Red- ford; Denny% Stretford and Atherton; Tickle. Nfeacliiii, Millwood, Hankinson and Sudell.</p>
      </div>
