      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-13_WidnesExaminer_art0278" n="art0278">
        <head>YOU HAVE HEARD ABOUT</head>
        <p>YOU HAVE HEARD ABOUT</p>
        <p>DOWLING'S Wonderful Value in ehristmas eards MAY WE SHOW YOU OUR STOCK AT F. J. DOWLING, Nl5, Sankey Street (close to Bank Quay Bridge).</p>
        <p>the wing, which E. Kendrick received and drove the ball hard at the goalkeeper. In bringing off the save the goalkeeper fumbled the ball, and J. Nurse, with one of his fine dashes, made no mistake. The Wesleyan forwards kept on the attack all the time, but were driven back each time by the stern defence of the home backs. Half-time found the Wesle,yans leading by one goal to nil. The Earlestown men came to the front, and from one of their raids Silverwood sent in a fine shot, which beat the visitors' goalkeeper. The Wesleyan forwards got on the move, and T. Kendrick put the ball into the net. Gilman, one of the home men, with a fine shot, beat Win- stanley, making the scores level. Result: Bew- sey-road Wesleykins, two goals; Earlestown Y.M.C.A., two, goals.</p>
        <p>TEAMS FOR TO-DAY.</p>
        <p>Grappenhall St. Wilfrid's team agaiiist Wid- nes Juniors, at Grappenhall, will be: Shake- shaft; Evans and H. Hankey ; T. Ford, Brazen- dale and F. Parr; Watson, J. Ford, Richards, H. Parr and Stubbs.</p>
        <p>St. Wilfrid's Reserves against Orford Albions : Barber; Thomas and Booth; Fairhurst, Evans and Hough; Woodcock, Hampson, Ravenscroft, Carey and Davies.</p>
        <p>The Stockton Heath and Latchford Clubs have mutually arranged to play their Cheshire Amatenr Cup tie to-day, instead of the 20th, the original date on which the ties should be played. The members of bah teams are confident of their abilities to win, and a record " gate " is expected at West Avenue. Mr. W. S. Walker, of Timperley, will officiate as referee.</p>
        <p>Stock-ton Heath: Sinicock; Gosling and Wood- ward; Magill, Denny and Gifford; Antrobus, Lockhart, Jones,- Smith and Taylor. Latchford (picked from): Kite; Robinson and Bardsley ; Downs, Webb and Carter; Smith, Webb, Siddall, Lees, Haspell and Fogg. The Heathens' Reserves journey to Farnworth In meet the Instituta in the Widnes and District Alliance, with: Harrison: Jefferson and Broad- hurst; Glover, Hamblett and West; Woodhead, Isherwood, Richardson,, Rigby and Houghton. Wilderspool Brewery meet Fairfield United with this side: Livingstone; Hunt and Dawson ; Brady, Barnes and Hughes; Davenport, Oakes, Rutter, R. L. Briscoe and J. Sankey. The Adult. School meet Earlestown Y.M.C.A.. at Victoria Park, with: Woods; Garnett and Spilsbury ; Leigh, Barton and Clark ;' Moody, Dykes, Challinor, Muckley and Banner. Orford Albion play Grappenhall St. Wilfrid's "A" at Gorsey-ltne with: Warburton; R. Hum- phries and A. Humphries; 'Holbrook. Higham and Woods; Lager, Robinson, Taylor, Smart and Yates.</p>
        <p>Padgate United play Latchford Baptists at Latchford with: Nork; Warburton and Sankey; Hodgkinson, Wadsworth and • Williams; John Houghton, Derbyshire, Rigby, iliardman and Buckley.</p>
        <p>Emmanuel Institute meet Penketh Friends' Scilool at Penketh With , Broadhurst; F. Hughes and Newton; Heacoek, S. Hughes and Banner; Ayres, Oakes, T. Hughes, Roberts and Berry.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-13_WidnesExaminer_art0279" n="art0279">
        <head>GOSSIP FROM THE CLUBS.</head>
        <p>GOSSIP FROM THE CLUBS.</p>
        <p>Gra ppenhall St. Wilfrid's Reserve journeyed to Paddington, and, after thirty minute? play, the game had to be abandoned owing to the football bursting. No points were recorded. This club has a few open dates. Secretaries. of clubs requiring dates kindly communicate with 11. E. Hankey, Mayfield Villas, Grappenhall, as soon as possible.</p>
        <p>MATCHES FOR TO-DAY. Cheshire Amateur Cup Tie. Stockton Heath v. Latchford; W. S. Walker.</p>
        <p>Warrington and District League.</p>
        <p>Great Sankey v. Daresbury ; G. 11. Rushton. Crosfield's v. Weston; W. Dennett. Fairfield United v. Wilderspool Brewery; J. Sherlock. J3ank Quay Recs. v. Fairfield ; S. C. Essenhigh. Orford St. John's v. ,Warrington Excelsior; J. Smith. Widnes and District Alliance.</p>
        <p>Farnworth Institute v. Stockton 'Heath Res.</p>
        <p>Friendly Matches. Adult SChool.v: Earlestown Y.M.C.A. • Penketh Friends v. 'Emmanuel Institute. Grappenhall St. Wilfrid's "A" v. Orford Albion. Latchford Baptist, v. Padgate United.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-13_WidnesExaminer_art0280" n="art0280">
        <head>SOME SENSATIONAL GAMES t=.Nil RESULTS.</head>
        <p>SOME SENSATIONAL GAMES t=.Nil RESULTS.</p>
        <p>Owing to the miserable conditions last week- end, and intervening cup-ties causing fixtures to be withdrawn, there were no senior engage- ments in the town. The 'Latchford Club, not being able to fix up, had to have an idle day, while Crosfield's, owing to the wretched condi- tion of their ground, and the failure which attended the efforts of their secretary and com- mittee to obtain another: field, had to cancel their fixture with Stockton Heath.</p>
        <p>The Adult School journeyed to Moore, where another even display was witnessed. Daresbury failed to find the net, whilst the visitors scored five times, and had they been steadier in front of goal they would probably have added more. The Wilderspool Brewery- met with a severe reverse on their own ground, to.- the tune of 12 goals to 3, Bank Quay Recs. being their victors.</p>
        <p>In the Widnes and District Alliance the Stockton Heath Reserve entertained Ditton St. Mary's at West-avenue, and after a hard and well-contested game the points were divided, two goals each being the score. In the Widnes and District Medal Competi- tion the match between Longford United and Warrington Excelsior proved a great attraction. A strenuous and lard-fought first half yielded no points, and the second portion was just as keenly fought. The visitors managed to gain the verdict in their favour a few minutes before call of time,</p>
        <p>Orford St. John's met Runcorn Heath at Long. lane, Orford,: and after, a well-contested game the home team ran out winners by' three goals to one.</p>
        <p>Emmanuel Old Boys met Widnes ./‘ ibion in the same competition at Victoria Park, and wc:.e ,victorious by three goals to none. Orford Juniors made the journey to .13 unenrn and met the Weston team, and after a keen and well-fought game the score was one goal each. The Juniors were handicapped by hav- ing to, play three reserves in their team, and therefore should be able to win the replay on their ground. • Bank Quay Recs. and St. Barnabas' had a bye in the first round, and there should be six Warrington junior clubs in the next round. The draw takes place at Widnes on Tuesday next.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON AND DISTRICT LEAGUE. Orford Barracks v. Divex.</p>
        <p>On the Soldiers' ground. Orford Barracks— Morrison; Tansley and Stevens ;. Moodie, Bir- kenhead and Topping; Roberts, Walker, 'Acton, Trafford and Spicer. Divex—Williams; Flute and Bullock; Rennie, Ashworth and Savage; Dronfield, Roberts, Curwen and Penfold. Referee, Mr.- T.. Pittam., , For thirtY, minutes the visitors held their own against their. More experienced opponents. ,Then the home team opened the score, and from now until the interval the Soldiers had the best of the game, and further goals were added. At half-time Orford Barracks were leading ,by four goals to nil.</p>
        <p>On resuming, the Visitors were unable to keep the home forwards in check and the score was added to in a prolific manner. At the expiry of time the result was: Orford Barracks, 11 goals: Divex, nil. Roberts (three), Walker (two), Trafford (two), Acton (two), an Spicer (two), were the goal scorers.</p>
        <p>By 4 4 Linesman."</p>
        <p>aged by this early success, the visitors were hav- ing the best of the exchanges, Jefferson and Broadhurst being extended to clear their lines. The home forwards made a grand combined effott and Rigby equalised the scores with a splendid shot. From the centre-kick both teams made efforts to gain the lead, but no fur- ther score took place, and the interval arrived one goal each. On resuming, it was soon evident it would be a keen struggle for the lead, and the visitors were the first to become dangerous, but Jeffer- son drove them back with some fine returns, being ably assisted by Broadhurst. The home. half-backs now set their forwards going, and they paid a visit to the opposite end, Woodhead being a source of danger to the visitors' defence, troubling them with some fine centres,, and from a mistake by one of the visitors' backs a scramble took place in the goal-mouth, the ball being rushed through the goal. The home team was placed in front by this score. The game was now very fast The visitors were awarded a free-kick just outside the penalty area, this being well placed to their inside-right, who made no mistake with his shot and equalised the scores. From now to the close there was a hard §truggle for the lead, but no further score took place. Result: Stockton Heath Reserve, 2 goals; Ditton St. Mary's, 2 goals.</p>
        <p>WIDNES MINOR MEDAL COMPETITION. Longford United were drawn against their neighbouring club, Warrington Excelsior, the match being played on the ground behind the Horse and Jockey Inn. The teams were : Longford United Holloway ; Mosley and Holden; Lea, H. Finch and Sherratt; A. Finch, W. Houghton, Blears, Helsby and Barton. Excelsior—Rigby; Boyd and Hughes; O'Neil, Spencer and Welsby ; Long, Walker, Leah, Hunt and Pritchard. Referee, Mr. S. C. Essenhigh. Owing to the local rivalry between the clubs, and the first time they had met in a competi- tion, there was a good number of supporters present. Blears kicked off and play began very even, and it was soon evident the game would be keenly fought, Longford were the first to get into a dangerous position, when Blears shot over the bar from an easy angle. The home team still had the best of the exchanges, but missed chances of scoring, and Boyd and Hughes eventually raised the siege. The visitors' for- wards carried play into the home quarters, and during an attack were awarded a penalty. Leah was entrusted with the kick, but 'Holloway made a clever save, and at halftime neither side had scored.</p>
        <p>Play was fast and exciting. The visitors gradually gained the upper hand, and Walker opened-the score. This success made the players more keen, and each end was visited, the home side missing a good chance of scoring, by one of their forwards handling the ball. However, this did not dishearten them, and during an- other attack they obtained a corner. Barton placed the ball well in front of goal and it was headed past Rigby, and the team were now on level terms again. From the centre kick both sides now played hard for the lead, and many exciting incidents occurred, both teams missing chances through being too eager. When the second half had been in progress 35 minutes Long put the visitors in front again. The home team made strenuous efforts to get level, but Rigby, Boyd and Hughes wore all in fine form and prevented any further score, and time was called with the result: Warrington Excelsior, 2 goals; Longford United, 1.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-13_WidnesExaminer_art0281" n="art0281">
        <head>Daresbury v. Adult School.*</head>
        <p>Daresbury v. Adult School.*</p>
        <p>These teams met on the ground of the former at Moore with the. following: —Darcsbury-- S. Ravenscraft; W. Forster and F. tle Prez; A. Cash, A. Ridyard and V. Ryder; W. Fleet, D. 'Adams, F. Forster, A. Ryder and 31. Forster. Adult •School—Welsby; Spilsbury and Garnett: Barton, Hunter and Leigh; Rowe, Dykes, McMutrie, Muckley and Banner, Referee, Mr r. J. Kite. The School, by good combination, forced the play to .the home. end, Ravenscroft mulling a shot in the opening stages. Dykes, with a grand opening, placed the ball wide of the posts. Receiving from the goal-kick, Fleet, Adams and H. Forster made a brief excursion to the visitors' end, but Hunter was alert, and, gaining possession, placed the leather to McMutrie. The latter sent out to Rowe on the extreme right, who beat Ryder and raced along the line, and centred at the right moment for Banner to rush in and score with a grand low- shot, leaving Ravenscroft helpless. At this point .the Adults were all over their opponents, and during an attack on the home goal the yisitors were awarded a penalty against W. Forster for a foul. The kick was taken by Hunter, and Ravenscroft, throwing himself full length across the goal, made a b,yillant save. The interval was called in favour ?If .e Adults by two goals to nil. The visitors made, tracks for the I :;me goal, and the game was confined chiefly around that citadel. 'Rowe added a couple of goals, while Muckley scored once. The home 'forwards could make very little impression on the visitors' defence, and Welsby had very little to do, only a few long shots coming his way. Result: Adult School, 5 goals; Daresbury, nil.</p>
        <p>Wilderspool Brewery v. Bank Quay Recs. On Wilderspool Park: Wilderspool—Bostock; Dawson and Rutter; Brady, Barnes and Moores; Davenport, Hurst, Briscoe, Oakes and Hughes. Bank Quay Recs.—Bushell; Berry and Taylor ; Worrall, Lagar and Chesters ; Eccleston, Deakin, Bradshaw, Davies and Green. ' The visitors; after two minutes' play, were awarded a penalty against Rutter. Bradshaw was entrusted with the. kick, but Bostock saved, and, owing to an infringethent, the kick hid to be taken over again. Bradshaw made no mistake at the second attempt. The visitors were soon at the home -end again, and, owing to the home backs miskicking, Davies got through and scored a second goal, and directly after Chesters added to( the score. Bad play .on the part of Rutter let in Davies foi. a fourth .goal; Up to this point ,the visitors were by far the better team, and the home defence was being severely tested, Bostock saving several shots in a very smart manner, although Deakin beat him with a beauty. After these reverses the home team suddenly roused themselves and gave a surpris- ing display of. combination for three-parts of the field, and Oakes finished up with scoring for; the Brewery. From the centre-kick the .visitors rushed the home defence, Bradshaw finding the net, and two other goals were added by the latter and Davies, whilst Bristoe scored a second goal for the home team, the half-time score reading: Recs., 8 goals; Brewery, 2. The second portion opened more evenly and the home team looked like making amends, but their final shots were ;very, erratic, and from good clearances by the visitors' backs Green -got away on the left, and scored with a shot just inside the post. After this the visitors' :goal had a narrow 'escape from being captured, Bushell tipping the ball over the bar, conceding a corner. The flag-kick was well placed in the goal-mouth, and Moores put, the ball in the net, making the score three for the Brewers. The - visitors were awarded another- penalty, from which Bradshaw scored, and further scores were made by Berry and Eccleston. Result: Bank Quay Recs., 12 goals: Wilderspool Brewery, 3.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-13_WidnesExaminer_art0282" n="art0282">
        <head>WIDNES AND DISTRICT ALLIANCE.</head>
        <p>WIDNES AND DISTRICT ALLIANCE.</p>
        <p>Siockion Heath Reserve v. D:tton St. Mary's. On the West-avenue ground. The home for- wards, getting possession, were the first to attack, but the final efforts sent the ball wide. A spell of .loose work by the home "half-backs allowed the visitors' forwards to break away, and their centre headed a fine goal. Encour-</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-13_WidnesExaminer_art0283" n="art0283">
        <head>ASSOCIATION ITEMS.</head>
        <p>ASSOCIATION ITEMS.</p>
        <p>Saturday was a miserable day for spectators and players in the " Soccer " code. Cup-ties and medal competitions are interfer- ing very much at present with the League fixtures.</p>
        <p>There were five Warrington junior clubs en- gaged on Saturday in the Widnes Medal Coin- petition. There should be six eligible for the next round..</p>
        <p>The Orford Barracks team enjoyed themselves at the expense of the Divex to the extent of 11 goals to nil. Five of their players contributed to the score. Roberts claiming three. Williams, Bullock and Penfold were the pick of the losing side. Daresbury were defeated on their own ground by the Adult School by s.goals to none. , Hunter is the mainspring of the ,Adult team. Spilsbury, Leigh, McMutrie and Rowe also did well.</p>
        <p>Ravenscroft was good in goal for Daresbury. Ridyard, at centre-half, was the pick of the home team.</p>
        <p>Bank Quay Recs. had a day out at Wilders- pool Park. They piled up 12 goals_ against the Brewers..</p>
        <p>- - Teddy Webb, of Latchford; was plating again for Altrincham in the Lancashire \Combination. And so was Hinde, of Lymm. The Cheshire Amateur Cup tie between Stock- ton Heath and Latcliford takes place to-day. The Heathens are confident of winning. And so are Latchford players. The " Premier " says "Wait and see."</p>
        <p>The Heathens have got a horseshoe for luck. Warrington and District League Committee are expecting a record entry for their Medal Coifipetifion. I The Adults have captured a fine half-back in Barton.</p>
        <p>Dykes scored all the goals in the Adults' first three matches of the season. He has now lost his shooting boots. The meeting of Orford St. John's and Ex- celsior on Saturday at Long-lane will be a fight for the championship. • The John's intend to wipe out that defeat they sustained at the Excelsior's ground.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-13_WidnesExaminer_art0284" n="art0284">
        <head>FRiENDLY MATCHES.</head>
        <p>FRiENDLY MATCHES.</p>
        <p>Grappenhall St. Wilfrid's v. Padgate United. At Grappenhall. -Richards started for Crap- penhall and after good combination on the part of the right wing the " Saints " forced a corner, which proved futile. The "'Saints," keeping up the pressure, were awarded a penalty, which Ford sent over the bar. Hardman made good progress on the left, but Evans' cleared, in grand style. Ford, receiving in the centre of the field, sent the ball out to Davies on the left, who put in a grand centre, which Woodcock met and gave the visiting custodian no chance. Houghton got going on the right, but Hankey was not to be beaten, half-time arriving with the score Grappenhall, 1 goal; Padgate, nil.</p>
        <p>In the second -half the play was of a: scrappy nature, and each goal was visited in turn, no further goals being recorded, the home team running out winners by 1-0. • ' Emmanuel Institute v. Excelsior "A." At Victoria Park. When the match had been in progress twenty minutes Oakes put the home team: in front. In' the last minute of the first half the visitors forced a corner. This being well placed, Barton headtd through. Haflf- time : Emmanuel, one goal; Excelsior, one goal. After some nice combination between the visitors' forwards, Williams put behind: In the last few minutes of the game S. Roberts put the home team in front, and they won by two goals to one.</p>
        <p>Bewsey-road Wesllyans v. Eariestown Y.M.C.A.</p>
        <p>At Earlestown. After some fine play between the, Wesley-an half-backs .and forwards, Jones, the visitors' left • half, Set his forwards going, and Ashworth sent in a rattling centre from</p>
      </div>
