        <p>"The Biblical and Early Christian Idea of Pro- perty," and 'confirms what is said by , Scott Holland. He finds the conception expressed by David in addressing God on the occasion of the offerings of Israel for the building of the Temple: "Thine, 0 Lord, is the greatness, and the power. and the majesty: for all that is in the Heaven, and in the earth is thine; thine is the kingdom, 0 Lord, and thou art exalted as head above all. Both riches and honour come of thee, and thou rulest over all." Property rights in the Old Testament he claims to be "purely relative, derivative, • con- ditional, in the presence of God's Sovereign Lordship in all He has produced, ,and is pro- clueing, through the subordinate agencies of Nature and man." In other words, the general or naltional wel- fare is prior', to and determinative of that of the individual in the Divine order, and so by right. He cites such passages as: "Woe unto those who join house to house, who add field to field, till there is no more room, and ye are settled alone in the midst of the land." Property had no rights that were not relative to this great law of human life, that everything is to be judged .as having the sanction of God only so far as it subserves, or at least does not stand in the way of it. Thus every institution of society is to be regarded as liable to modification, -as in practice it fails to work in such a way as. to respect the sovereign rights of God in 'humanity • C. E. P. * PROPERTY: us DUTIES AND RIGHTS. By various writers, with an introduction by Bishop . Gore. London: Macmillan and Co., Ltd.</p>
