        <p>PUBLIC AMUSEMENTS. AN OPIUM DEN DREAM. Fine Programme at the Hippodrome. "A Dream in an Opium Den" is an excellent turn by the Six Ceylons, a troupe of alert and tricky acrobats and tumblers. The most daring tricks are performed in the most matter-of-fact way, whilst the antics of the diminutive mem- bers of the troupe cause roars of laughter. "The Vagabond" is an intensely interesting playlet, in which the pathetic and humorous aspects blend admirably. Mr. E. D. Nicholls ably plays the part of the vagabond. A mystifying turn is given by The Violas, who thoroughly bale the audience. and the illusions of Professor Nor- ton are cleverly carried out. Money Given Away. The millionaire entertainer, Mr. T. Elder Hearn. the marvellously clever illusionist, beads the bill for next week. He will give hundreds of postal orders away, at each performance. He Aitohl24., • •-• , . ./ *WV (SOVAi&lt;o.4igigfirti:M° •i;eatanoseint*,:ar.3sob..::./ MR. T. ELDER HEARN.</p>
