        <p>will also appear as "The Lazy Juggler" and in "The Romance of a Studio." Other turns will be Harry Moore, poster manipulator; Ernie Ream, the new comedian, at the piano; Elton and Edwin. in classic and rag; Anita Wayman, soprano vocalist; Alex. Lawson, dancing equili- brist. "PET OF THE RANCH" At Royal Court Theatre. "The Pet of the Ranch," by Mrs. F. G. Kim- berley, is a play which bristles with sensational incidents, although the element of Comedy in- troduced is a happy detrattion.. Miss Iza Lyn- don appears with success in the title role, and her acting is greatly appreciated by the audi- ence. Mr. A. Austin Lea is equallY successful as Philip Trelyan. The Boy Detective." Mr. and Mrs.- Harry Lyndon's Company will present "'The Boy Detective" at the Royal Court Theatre next week. This is a drama with a clever plot, which develops in a sensational manner. It is one of the best detective plays on tour. "JEALOUSY" AT THEATRE ROYAL. " Jealousy " will be presented to-night; and the presentation of this romantic drama by the Stock Company is an excellent one. "vengeance," another romantic production, will be staged next week. "A CHILD MOTHER" at PICTUREDROME. "A Child Mother" will be the principal film for Monday. Tuesday and Wednesday, whilst "Guerilla Menace" will occupy premier posi- tion for the 'remainder of the week. THE GREAT CIRCUS AT OLYMPIA, LIVERPOOL. Tremendous interest is being centred on the great Continental Circus, which Mr. Ernest Wighton has arranged as the Christmas attrac- tion at Olympia. Nothing since the days of Barnum and Bailey ,so elaborate, costly and mag- nificent, has been seen in England. It is the greatest aggregation of Circus novelties the 'world can produce. Mr. Ernest Wighton has been very 'fortunate in securing the absolute cream of talent from all the big circuses of the world. HOW I RUBBED AWAY A STONE OF FAT FROM MY HIPS AND ABDOMEN IN TWO WEEKS TIME. For years I tried every possible means to hide the excessive fat on my hips and abdomen; it seemed as if it had accumulated on just the two places that it would be most noticeable. Everything that I tried failed to make me any thinner. But thanks to a lady .friend who had studied herboristerie, I learned the secret of a harmless plan which enabled -me to rub away with eakie 4 stone of useles§ fat hi •Otily two weeks. The plah ii ô simple and some of my friends seem so glad to know of it that I think there may be sister readers Of the Warrington " Examiner " who have perhaps been longing for this very advice. First, get from any good chemist 1 dram of quassia chips and 3 ounces of cirola bark extract. Take them home and after putting the quassia chips in a pan pour over it a teacupful of boiling water. When this has Stood for about a minute. strain through a cloth and add the cirola bark extract. Then pour the mixture into a bottle and apply it every night and morning with your hands for about ten minutes, using a circular movement. Each time you can almost see some of the fat melt away. At first I thought it must be a dream, but when I measured there' was no longer any doubt. I soon grew too Slender for my clothes, and had to have them taken in, but it was a pleasure to do it,. and I shall feel eternally grateful to the lady who showed me how easy it was to regain my youthful figure. E. L. A. WESLEYAN CAUSE THRIVING. Church Extension at Latchford. The Warrington Circuit quarterly meeting was held at Bold-street on Thursday. Tea was provided by the ladies of the Bold-street Church and the beautifully decorated tables aroused much admiration. The ladies were heartily thanked. The Chairman, the Rev. W. A. Beckett, was supported by all the circuit ministers and more than one hundred representatives from all parts of the circuit. The Chairman reported the mem- bership to show a very considerable increase on the corresponding quarter of last year. The stewards reported the finances to be in a very healthy state, showing a good balance in hand. The retiring circuit stewards, Mr. F. A. Har- ris and Dr. Ferguson, were heartily thanked for their services, and Mr. j. G. Withenshaw and Mr. R. 11. Meldrum were unanimously elected to fill their places. Mr. Murphy, of Penketh. was elected secretary of the quarterly meeting. The following appointments were made : Auditors, Messrs. Barlow and Pendlebury education secretary, Rev. E. Morgan; Guild secretary, Mr. K. Bishop. The local preachers were heartily thanked, many tributes being paid to the value of their services and their general efficiency. Mr. Wheatley „reported that a very successful class leaders' convention had been held, and Mr. Jackson had an equally good report as to the ,success of the aged -preachers' fund. The Rev. a Williamson reported that the Centenary Missionary fund collection in the circuit would, when all amounts were paid in I reach £550, and Mr. W. .Silcock said that dr usual missionary contributions were quite u to the average. The Chairman reported that land had, bee, secured adjoining the Wesleyan Church a Latchford for an extension of the premises, anN Iluilding operations were about to commence. 1 It was also, reported that Mrs. Dodds ha promised to give an organ to the Sankey Bridgr Wesleyan Church in Memory of her husban the late Mr. James Dodds. It was decided to accept the invitation of Bewsey-road Church to hold the next meet there.</p>
